WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.417
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome to
the Huberman Lab Guest Series,

00:00:02.417 --> 00:00:04.620
where I and an
expert guest discuss

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.840
science and science-based
tools for everyday life.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.610
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.440
I'm Andrew Huberman
and I'm a professor

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.540
of neurobiology
and ophthalmology

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.190
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.040
Today's episode marks
the first in a series

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.630
with Dr. Andy Galpin.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.780
Dr. Andy Galpin is a professor
of kinesiology at Cal State

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.540
University Fullerton and
one of the foremost world

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.120
experts on the science
and application of methods

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.890
to increase strength, speed,
endurance, hypertrophy,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.610
and various other aspects of
fitness, exercise, and sports

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:36.330
performance.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.470
Across this six episode
series, Dr. Andy Galpin

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.470
pulls from his expertise
working with everything

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.920
from professional athletes
to recreational exercisers

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.570
and teaches us the mechanisms,
logic, and specific protocols

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.617
for how to achieve any of the
number of different exercise

00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:53.700
adaptations that I mentioned
a moment ago, ranging

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.410
from strength to
endurance, hypertrophy,

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.180
and everything in between.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.960
We get really far into details,
but at all times paying

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.450
attention to the
macroscopic issues.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.480
That is, how to create
a program for endurance

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.370
or strength or
hypertrophy or speed,

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.270
or one that combines
all of those.

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.930
We also talk about
supplementation and nutrition

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.050
and how to maximize recovery
for each of the different types

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.460
of exercise adaptations.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:18.960
During today's
episode, Dr. Galpin

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.120
teaches us how to assess
our level of fitness

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.210
and, more generally, how to
think about fitness so that we

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.840
can best achieve our fitness
exercise and performance goals.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.990
Dr. Professor Andy Galpin.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.640
Super excited to have you here.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.470
You're such an
immense treasure trove

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.140
of information on physical
training and optimizing

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:42.540
for specific goals and outcomes
with physical exercise.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.530
I'm curious, however--
so many people have

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.130
different levels of fitness.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.320
Some people are professional
athletes, of course,

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.580
but most people are not.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.440
Many people exercise regularly.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.128
Some people are trying
to do that more.

00:01:57.128 --> 00:01:59.670
Some people are doing too much
of that; they're overtraining,

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.190
they're not recovering enough.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:05.580
If we were to take a step back
and each and every one of us

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.660
ask how fit are we, with
the word fit, of course,

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.800
being a very broad
encompassing word,

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.900
could encompass endurance,
certainly it does,

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.110
strength, the
ability to run fast,

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:24.520
even if for short distances, it
might even include hypertrophy

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:27.910
or directed hypertrophy, trying
to balance one's musculature

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:31.600
to offset asymmetries, recover
from injuries, et cetera.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:35.880
How should I, or anyone
else for that matter

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.020
think about their
level of fitness?

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:41.460
I know my resting
heart rate, but what

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.580
do I do in terms of really
assessing whether or not

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:49.170
I'm as fit as I could be
and should be both for sake

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.430
of health and performance?

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.765
And here I'm asking you the
question not as an athlete,

00:02:53.765 --> 00:02:55.140
but as somebody
who's been pretty

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.550
consistent as an exerciser.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.040
But if we were to throw our
arms around this question of how

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.710
do we assess our
fitness, what would

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.800
be the different
levels of assessment

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:08.555
that we should
think about and do?

00:03:08.555 --> 00:03:10.180
ANDY GALPIN: When it
comes to exercise,

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.300
people generally have
two major goals in mind.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.520
Goal number one is achieving
some sort of appearance.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.020
Right?

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:19.780
This is I want to be big,
or I want to not be too big,

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:20.770
or I want to be lean.

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:21.490
Something, right?

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:22.990
It doesn't matter
what that goal is,

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:25.523
but there is an aesthetic
component to almost everybody.

00:03:25.523 --> 00:03:27.940
They want to look a certain
way or not look a certain way.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.413
The other one is functionality.

00:03:29.413 --> 00:03:31.330
So I want to be able to
perform a certain way.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.560
Now, again, that definition
differs per person.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.740
So I want to be
better at strength,

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.620
I want to be better
at mobility, I

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.080
want to be able to have energy
throughout the day, whatever

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:40.580
it is.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:42.610
So there's some sort
of appeal to aesthetic

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.930
and there's some sort of
appeal to functionality.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.570
So within both of
those categories,

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:52.030
we want to be in a position
where we can understand, where

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:53.800
do I need to go with
my exercise training

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.320
so that I can be as
fit and as healthy

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.350
and achieve these
goals that I want now,

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.260
as well as be in a position
where I can maintain them

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:03.950
for a long period of time.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:06.370
So this blends both
immediate goals.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:09.550
So say you're just interested
in squatting a lot of weights.

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:12.610
Say you're interested in
running a 5K time the best run.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:13.810
It doesn't matter.

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:18.220
It blends that with the desire
to have a long wellness span,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.589
to be fit throughout life,
to achieve all those things

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:21.850
for as long as possible.

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:23.500
So then the question
kind of comes back

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.340
to saying, well, how do
I know which area I need

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:30.280
to focus on the most, and why
am I not achieving these goals,

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.690
or how can I get there
more effectively.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.330
And if we look at
the big picture,

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:37.780
we have to understand that there
are several major components

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:40.270
to physical fitness that
are going to be required

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:42.400
in all of these categories.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:45.160
And to achieve that, there
are a handful of components

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.858
that have to happen to be
able to hit those goals.

00:04:47.858 --> 00:04:49.150
Now there are infinite methods.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:52.120
So the saying we actually use
here a lot is, the methods are

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:53.650
many, but the concepts are few.

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:55.960
So what I'd love to do
today is, over the course

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:59.170
of our discussion is hit
exactly what those concepts are

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:01.300
and then cover a whole
bunch of different methods.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.070
And we could do that for hours.

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:05.357
But we'll cover a number
of them for various goals.

00:05:05.357 --> 00:05:06.940
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
one of the reasons

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:10.930
I went into neuroscience and
not into exercise science is

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:13.360
because of this thing
neuroplasticity,

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:15.783
the nervous system's
ability to adapt.

00:05:15.783 --> 00:05:17.200
But the more time
I spend with you

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:21.130
and the more I learn from you,
I realize that many, if not all

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:24.730
of the organ systems of our body
have this incredible ability

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:25.630
to adapt.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:28.570
And when we're talking
about physical exercise,

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.897
there are incredible
adaptations that,

00:05:30.897 --> 00:05:32.480
of course, involve
the nervous system,

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.340
but also involve muscle and
connective tissue and so

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.170
many other cell
types and tissues.

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:43.200
That said, when we
talk about fitness,

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.870
what are the major
types of adaptations

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:48.090
that underlie this thing
that we call fitness?

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:50.880
And later, I know
we're going to get

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:53.010
into how different
forms of exercise

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:55.320
can trigger different
types of adaptations,

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.340
but what are the major
adaptations that one can create

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:02.010
in their body using exercise?

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:03.720
ANDY GALPIN: There
are many reasons why

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.430
one should exercise,
and we could perhaps

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.260
cover that later in our chats.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.360
But the physiological
adaptations

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.800
can be bucketed really
in a nine areas.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.430
So the very first one is what
I call skill or technique.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:17.340
So just learning to move
better, more efficiently

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:20.460
with a specific position
and timing and sequence,

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:21.600
or whatever that is.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.790
This could be running
more effectively,

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:26.325
this could be practicing
a skill like shooting

00:06:26.325 --> 00:06:29.760
a ball or an implement,
swinging a golf club.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.650
Anything like that, I call
that skill development.

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:34.590
The second one is
speed, so this is simply

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:37.200
moving at a higher
velocity or with a better

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.360
rate of acceleration.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.880
That's very similar to the
next one, which is power.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:46.755
And power is speed
multiplied by force.

00:06:46.755 --> 00:06:48.630
The next one then, of
course, on top of that,

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:50.190
is force or strength.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:52.830
So those are really
synonymous terms.

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:54.840
How effectively can
you move something?

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:58.290
Now, this is often
confused-- strength, rather--

00:06:58.290 --> 00:06:59.490
as muscular endurance.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:02.340
So what I mean by
that is strength truly

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:06.060
is a marker of what's the
maximum thing you can move

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.450
or what's the maximum amount of
force you can produce one time.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.000
It's not how many repetitions
in a row you can do.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.190
That's actually another
one of our adaptations

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:16.830
called muscular endurance.

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:22.080
So that is typically under
the order of say five to 25,

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:23.340
maybe 50 repetitions.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.880
Think of a classic how many
push-ups can you do in a row?

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.680
How many sit-ups can
you do in a minute?

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.640
Things like that are
muscular endurance.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.430
Muscular endurance
tends to be localized,

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:35.250
so this is specific
to just, say,

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.410
your triceps and your deltoids.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:40.740
It's not a overall
cardiovascular endurance marker

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:41.680
or anything like that.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:43.013
So that's strength, number four.

00:07:43.013 --> 00:07:44.820
Number five is
muscle hypertrophy.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:46.320
And this is the
first time now we're

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:49.830
talking about an
appearance rather

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:51.160
than a functional outcome.

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:53.850
So moving better, moving
faster, and moving heavier

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:57.733
are indicators of how
well you can move.

00:07:57.733 --> 00:07:59.400
This is the first one
that's just simply

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:00.840
how big is your muscle?

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.630
And that's muscle
hypertrophy or muscle size.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:05.710
After that is
muscular endurance.

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:07.950
So this is how many
repetitions you can typically

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:09.100
do of a movement.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.310
So think of how many
push ups in a row

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:14.100
you can do, how many sit
ups in a minute you can do,

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:15.840
things that are
typically in five

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.810
to 50 repetition sort of range.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:22.750
And it is often or it is
almost always local muscle.

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:26.260
So what I mean by
that is a push-up test

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:28.350
is really how many
reps that your triceps

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.930
and pecs and deltoids can do.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:32.850
It is not a
cardiovascular endurance.

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:35.549
It is not a global
physiological endurance.

00:08:35.549 --> 00:08:39.330
It's specific to, typically,
one or a few muscle

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:40.809
groups at a time.

00:08:40.809 --> 00:08:43.679
And this is why you have to do
multiple tests for every group

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:44.700
there.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:47.310
After that, now we've moved
into number seven, which is

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.320
what I call anaerobic capacity.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:52.260
This is more synonymous
with maximum heart rate.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:54.270
And now we're actually
looking at, rather

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:57.330
than a single movement
or muscle group,

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.930
it is a total
physiological limitation.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:03.240
So it is the maximum
amount of work

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.010
you can do in, say,
30 to 45 seconds,

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:10.180
maybe even up to 120
seconds, of all-out work.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:13.240
Think of your classic
interval type of stuff here.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:16.110
So how much work can you
do at a maximum rate,

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:18.120
where you're going to
enter tremendous amounts

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:19.980
of global fatigue?

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:23.335
The next past that is
maximal aerobic capacity.

00:09:23.335 --> 00:09:24.960
And this is probably
actually something

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:28.200
like in the eight to 15
minute range, where you're

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.080
going to reach probably
both a maximum heart rate

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:35.610
as well as a true
VO2 max, which we'll

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.860
talk a lot more about
what that is later.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:42.660
So that is different
from the previous one,

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:45.300
where you can't reach this
in a matter of seconds.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:48.390
It simply takes multiple
minutes to get to a position

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:50.250
to where your VO2
max is actually

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:52.290
going to be
sufficiently challenged

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.410
or an indicator there.

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:54.660
And then the last
one, number nine,

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:55.868
is what I call long duration.

00:09:55.868 --> 00:09:59.640
And this is just your ability
to sustain submaximum work

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.080
for a long period of
time with no breaks,

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:03.540
no reduction whatsoever.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.090
This is often called steady
state training or a lot

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.090
of people just think of
this when they think of,

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:09.930
quote, unquote, "cardio,"
but your ability

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:13.680
to continue to move without
any breaks or change or drop

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:15.480
is the last and
final adaptation.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:18.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And for
long distance steady state,

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.150
I'm guessing it exceeds
15 minutes because--

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:22.050
ANDY GALPIN: Correct.

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:23.508
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--the previous one

00:10:23.508 --> 00:10:25.350
was eight to 15 minutes or so.

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:29.550
What sort of rate ranges are
we talking about in terms

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:30.870
of this long duration?

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:31.710
ANDY GALPIN: Well, that's
actually wonderful.

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:33.543
You're going to be
anything past 15 minutes.

00:10:33.543 --> 00:10:36.330
So really, if you look at
a minimal number there,

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:39.150
it's generally 20 minutes
of what we're looking for,

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:41.603
but a more typical would
be 20 to 60 minutes.

00:10:41.603 --> 00:10:43.020
But anything past
that would still

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:46.140
be limited by your
long-duration endurance,

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:48.067
so your ability to
sustain work over time.

00:10:48.067 --> 00:10:48.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:52.140
So given that there are nine
different major adaptations

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:56.280
that can be induced with
exercise of specific types,

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:59.850
is there any one global
test or assessment

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:04.140
that people can take or do
that allows them to determine

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:09.268
what level of ability, of
fitness they have in each

00:11:09.268 --> 00:11:11.310
and every one of these
nine different categories?

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:13.980
ANDY GALPIN: There are
probably dozens or more tests

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.380
that you can do for each one
of those nine categories.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:17.880
And what I would
actually like to do

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:19.950
is walk you through
my favorites for each

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:22.390
and giving you both the
scientific gold standard--

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:25.080
so if you have the ability,
unlimited resources, what

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:26.130
should you go do?

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:28.770
As well as some that
are equipment-free,

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:32.590
that are cost-free, things that
anyone can do across the world.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:34.590
In addition to that,
I want to walk you

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:36.690
through what those
numbers should be,

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:38.940
how do you identify if you're
really poor in something

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:40.020
or if you're great.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:42.425
And then if you aren't as
good, maybe, in a category

00:11:42.425 --> 00:11:43.800
and you want to
get better at it,

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:46.470
exactly what to do
in terms of protocols

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:49.180
for how to achieve optimal
results in each of those steps.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:51.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So I noticed
in your list of the nine

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:53.820
different adaptations to
exercise that you did not

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:57.000
mention fat loss
or health-promoting

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:00.750
benefits, which are two reasons
that a lot of people exercise.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.440
Was there a specific reason
that you did not mention those?

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:04.440
ANDY GALPIN: Absolutely.

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:07.470
It's because those things are
actually not specific training

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:08.400
styles.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:11.430
They are byproducts
of these nine.

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:16.060
So what I mean by that is if you
understand how fat loss occurs,

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:17.940
which we can
certainly talk about,

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:21.330
you'll realize some of
these nine protocols

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:24.330
are effective for fat
loss and some are not.

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:25.710
General health is
the same thing.

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:27.210
When we understand
what it actually

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:30.150
means to be healthy from a
physiological perspective,

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:32.790
then the rationale
for what to train for

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:34.540
is going to determine itself.

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:37.800
So what I mean is,
looking at things like,

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:41.040
in order to be healthy, you have
to have sufficient strength,

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:42.770
you have to have
cardiovascular fitness,

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:46.150
and you have to have sufficient
muscle and et cetera.

00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:48.930
Therefore, training
for one's health

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.820
is determined by
those restrictions.

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:55.140
So for you, Andrew, you may need
to do more strength training

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:57.480
to be healthy, where me,
because I'm strong already,

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:00.320
way stronger than you, I may
not need to do as much strength

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:00.820
training.

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:03.180
So our, quote, unquote,
"health-based protocols"

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:07.350
are based on our current status
or limitations in physical

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:09.730
fitness among these nine areas.

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:11.760
So what I would
like to do today is

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:14.850
to cover a brief history
of exercise science.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:19.050
And the reason is it's going to
explain a lot about why people

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:21.840
are not getting the goals
in their exercise programs

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:23.880
that they want as
