00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:23.220
Before we get into how the
history of exercise science

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:25.500
informs the mistakes
that we are all making

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:28.680
and how to remedy those
mistakes, I'm curious as to

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:31.350
whether or not you have
any favorite one or two

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:37.320
studies that point to a
naturally occurring example

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:40.690
of how people can become
very fit in one area and not

00:17:40.690 --> 00:17:41.190
another.

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:44.680
I'm familiar with seeing
endurance athletes that

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:46.570
apparently have
terrific endurance

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:49.450
but, at least to my eye, don't
look like they are particularly

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:50.110
strong.

00:17:50.110 --> 00:17:53.110
I'm also familiar with seeing
individuals that are very, very

00:17:53.110 --> 00:17:55.370
strong, particularly
on social media,

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:57.490
but that don't look
like they could walk up

00:17:57.490 --> 00:18:00.160
a flight of stairs,
much less run a mile.

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:01.930
Do you have any
examples of studies

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:03.910
in or outside the
laboratory that point

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:05.380
to that in a concrete way?

00:18:05.380 --> 00:18:07.430
ANDY GALPIN: There's
a lot to discuss here,

00:18:07.430 --> 00:18:09.697
but I'll answer really clear.

00:18:09.697 --> 00:18:11.530
If you look across the
literature-- and this

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:13.970
is actually back to as
early as the mid 1950s.

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:15.970
In fact, it actually goes
back previous to that,

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:20.890
to the Harvard Fatigue
Lab, 1927 to 1947 area.

00:18:20.890 --> 00:18:23.860
People actually were
advocating, at that point,

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:26.240
a combination of strength
training and endurance.

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:27.490
ANDREW HUBERMAN: In the 1920s?

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:28.190
ANDY GALPIN: Way back then.

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:29.857
In fact, it actually
goes prior to that.

00:18:29.857 --> 00:18:33.790
In the late 1880s, there is
scientific evidence back then.

00:18:33.790 --> 00:18:37.330
It became more well-developed
in the mid 1950s and '60s.

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:39.815
In fact, there was
the initial stages

00:18:39.815 --> 00:18:42.190
of what's called the Exercise
As Medicine Movement, which

00:18:42.190 --> 00:18:45.010
is the movement now, but the
initial stages of that actually

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:46.510
route back to the 1950s.

00:18:46.510 --> 00:18:48.700
And I could actually go
into that whole discussion

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:51.400
and the story of how
that all came about,

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:54.130
but that's the Health
Is Wealth mantra

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:58.090
that came from the 1950s, from
the scientific community then.

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:00.790
All those data points
are going to suggest

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:03.640
you need a combination of
some sort of broad strength

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:05.230
training and broad endurance.

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:08.470
Now, if you have a specific
goal five months from now,

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:11.230
you want to compete in a race
or hit a certain physique

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:13.690
thing, that's fine to focus
on one area of training.

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:16.100
Certainly, if you're an
athlete, that's different.

00:19:16.100 --> 00:19:18.040
But if you want
to maximize health

00:19:18.040 --> 00:19:20.142
and overall functionality
throughout time,

00:19:20.142 --> 00:19:21.350
it needs to be a combination.

00:19:21.350 --> 00:19:22.420
And to really, really
highlight this,

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:24.295
I can actually talk
about a couple of studies

00:19:24.295 --> 00:19:24.970
that I've done.

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:28.000
One of them we actually
did in Stockholm, Sweden.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:31.420
So I did this at the Karolinska
Institute, which you probably

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:32.440
are aware of.

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:34.510
It's actually one of
the founding places

00:19:34.510 --> 00:19:36.643
of all of exercise physiology.

00:19:36.643 --> 00:19:37.810
Generally, it started there.

00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:39.643
It was called something
different back then,

00:19:39.643 --> 00:19:42.190
but really, our
entire field came out

00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:44.380
of Stockholm and the
Karolinska Institute.

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:47.500
And we worked with a whole
bunch of cross-country skiers

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:49.150
that were in their 80s and 90s.

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:52.000
And so they were competitive
skiers in the 1940s and '50s,

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:53.740
and they had been
skiing competitively

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:55.550
for that entire duration.

00:19:55.550 --> 00:19:59.440
So you're talking 50 to 60
consecutive years of competing.

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:02.380
So these are 80 to 90-year-olds,
living alone and healthy.

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:05.140
And we compared them to
a group of individuals

00:20:05.140 --> 00:20:09.653
here in America who are the same
age but were not exercising.

00:20:09.653 --> 00:20:11.070
And what we wanted
to do is to see

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:14.517
and of look at, what are these
lifelong endurance individuals?

00:20:14.517 --> 00:20:15.475
What do they look like?

00:20:15.475 --> 00:20:18.070
And when we brought them into
the lab, which is, by the way,

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:22.120
amazing, to do a VO2 max
test on a 92-year-old,

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:24.820
especially in a language
that they don't speak,

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:27.183
you can imagine, you're
doing this in the hospital.

00:20:27.183 --> 00:20:28.600
And you're running
people through,

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:29.770
this is a cycling task.

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:32.890
And so for a VO2 max test,
you have a mask on your face,

00:20:32.890 --> 00:20:34.390
you're hooked up
to a metabolic cart

00:20:34.390 --> 00:20:35.890
so we can collect
all the gases that

00:20:35.890 --> 00:20:38.080
are coming out of
your mouth, and you're

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:39.130
chanting these people on.

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:40.875
And basically, every
minute, the workload

00:20:40.875 --> 00:20:42.250
gets harder and
harder and harder

00:20:42.250 --> 00:20:44.560
until you can't complete it.

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:47.560
And we're doing this
in a cardiology center,

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.200
and the cardiologists are
usually waiting for their heart

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:52.420
rate to get slightly
elevated and they stop them

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:55.067
because they're
85, 86 years old.

00:20:55.067 --> 00:20:56.650
And not only are we
not stopping them,

00:20:56.650 --> 00:20:58.895
but we are screaming in
their ears, just go, go, go.

00:20:58.895 --> 00:21:00.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: In
Swedish or English?

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:01.810
ANDY GALPIN: In English.

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:03.657
And then the translator--
but it doesn't

00:21:03.657 --> 00:21:05.740
take a lot of translation
when someone's screaming

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:08.590
at your face, go, go, go.

00:21:08.590 --> 00:21:11.232
So we ran them through a
whole bunch of VO2 max tests.

00:21:11.232 --> 00:21:12.940
And we did the same
thing for those folks

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:15.070
back here in America.

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:18.400
And what was incredibly
clear from that study

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:20.103
was the VO2 max--

00:21:20.103 --> 00:21:21.520
you can think about
these numbers,

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:23.080
and this is what's
called relative.

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:25.060
And the relative
terms are milliliters

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:26.240
per kilogram per minute.

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:28.690
And so a standard
number is about 18,

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:30.610
is what we call the
line of independence.

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:33.580
So if your VO2 max is below
18 milliliters per kilogram

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:35.980
per minute, it's very hard
for you to live by yourself.

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:38.187
So your fitness is
so low, you probably

00:21:38.187 --> 00:21:40.270
are going to need to have
somebody living with you

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:42.890
or you'll need to be in some
sort of assisted living home.

00:21:42.890 --> 00:21:47.920
So if you are in a VO2
max of 20 or 21 or 22,

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:49.900
you're not below that
line of independence,

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:51.950
but you're on that threshold.

00:21:51.950 --> 00:21:55.120
And so what we found was
our folks here in America,

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:57.198
the group average was
right around that number.

00:21:57.198 --> 00:21:58.990
So they were living at
home, by definition.

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:01.150
We picked them to be
people living by themselves

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:03.340
in their 80s and not
in a living home,

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:04.880
but they didn't
have any bandwidth.

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:07.613
So if they got a cold or
they had anything pop up

00:22:07.613 --> 00:22:09.280
where they lost a
little bit of fitness,

00:22:09.280 --> 00:22:10.970
they were going to
drop below that line

00:22:10.970 --> 00:22:13.720
and would probably have to go
to some sort of assisted living

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:15.040
situation.

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:19.300
The folks in Stockholm, the
cross-country skiers, the group

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:22.930
average was much closer
to 35 to 38 milliliters

00:22:22.930 --> 00:22:23.960
per kilogram per minute.

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:26.950
Now, that number is
about the VO2 max

00:22:26.950 --> 00:22:30.400
you would find for a
normal college male.

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:34.390
And so these folks that
were literally 80 or 90--

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:38.020
the joke, if a sabertooth tiger
ran in the room or whatever

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:39.820
and it chased it
down and we all had

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:41.850
to run to see who
didn't get eaten alive,

00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:43.600
the college men would
probably have gotten

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:46.600
eaten before the 90-year-olds.

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:48.950
And in one case, we had
a 92-year-old individual.

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:50.680
And I think his VO2
max was 38, which

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:52.840
was, in our estimation,
a world record,

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:56.410
the highest VO2 max for
somebody over the age of 90.

00:22:56.410 --> 00:22:59.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN: May I ask what
is the typical resting heart

00:22:59.410 --> 00:23:04.330
rate for somebody very
fit, like these older

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:06.640
Swedish cross-country skiers?

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:10.060
If somebody has-- let's
say their number is

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:14.740
35 millimeters per kilogram
in this VO2 max test,

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:16.330
but since most of
us don't have access

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:19.300
to that kind of equipment, but
we can measure our pulse rate.

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:20.260
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:21.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What was
a typical resting heart

00:23:21.820 --> 00:23:22.990
rate, resting pulse rate?

00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:23.850
ANDY GALPIN: Sub 60.

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:24.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sub 60.

00:23:24.850 --> 00:23:25.300
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

00:23:25.300 --> 00:23:26.883
I mean, typically
that's a good number

00:23:26.883 --> 00:23:29.800
to go off of for anybody,
regardless of age.

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:31.330
Any time I see
somebody above that,

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:33.040
I'm going to start
asking questions.

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:35.457
Certainly above-- you'll see
in the literature people will

00:23:35.457 --> 00:23:39.970
say 60 to 80 is normal, and I
don't agree with that at all.

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:42.960
If your resting heart rate
is 75 beats per minute,

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:45.443
there's either something
going on or you're not fit.

00:23:45.443 --> 00:23:47.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN: How
much cross-country skiing

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:50.390
were they doing, on
average, in the previous,

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:52.880
let's say, if we
take the previous 20

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:55.130
years since they'd been
longtime cross-country skiers.

00:23:55.130 --> 00:23:57.200
Divide that by 20 years.

00:23:57.200 --> 00:23:57.950
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah,

00:23:57.950 --> 00:23:59.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN: On average,
are these people cross-country

00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:02.390
skiing five hours a day, two
hours a day, an hour a day?

00:24:02.390 --> 00:24:03.570
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, that's
actually a good question.

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:04.310
I don't remember.

00:24:04.310 --> 00:24:06.587
It's been many years,
but they were not

00:24:06.587 --> 00:24:07.670
doing it every single day.

00:24:07.670 --> 00:24:09.950
And the volume would
not have shocked you.

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:13.132
It was the consistency over
50 years that got them there.

00:24:13.132 --> 00:24:14.840
Now, obviously, these
people were, again,

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:17.240
world champions and Olympic
gold medalists in the 1940s

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:19.010
and '50s, so they were elite.

00:24:19.010 --> 00:24:21.500
They just continued
consistently over time,

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:24.920
but it wasn't a shocking
amount of physical fitness.

00:24:24.920 --> 00:24:29.600
They also didn't go out of
their way to train hard.

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:31.190
They were busy chopping wood.

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:33.420
They were busy doing a
number of other things.

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:35.628
And then they just happened
to do some of these races

00:24:35.628 --> 00:24:37.940
and ski along the way, but
it wasn't a crazy amount too

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:39.440
where you're like,
oh, that's great,

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:41.120
but I could never
hit that number.

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:42.842
It was something
much more reasonable.

00:24:42.842 --> 00:24:44.300
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So is the takeaway

00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:48.140
to be consistent about getting
cardiovascular exercise?

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:50.300
And we can define what
"consistent" means

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:52.310
in terms of days per
week a little bit later,

00:24:52.310 --> 00:24:53.420
and I know we will.

00:24:53.420 --> 00:24:54.170
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

00:24:54.170 --> 00:24:56.087
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What
are some other examples?

00:24:56.087 --> 00:24:58.710
I love these examples
from the real world.

00:24:58.710 --> 00:25:00.590
ANDY GALPIN: So here's
the downside, though.

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:02.700
So I only told you
about the VO2 max.

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:05.570
What I didn't tell you
about is their leg strength

00:25:05.570 --> 00:25:06.920
and functionality.

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:09.080
And that part was
no more superior

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:12.740
than it was their counterparts
who were not exercisers.

00:25:12.740 --> 00:25:16.370
So what that showed really,
really clearly-- and many

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:18.800
other studies have been
done since then that

00:25:18.800 --> 00:25:20.210
look at the classic,
what we call

00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:22.550
lifelong endurance exercisers.

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:25.430
You will see, in
general, their VO2 max,

00:25:25.430 --> 00:25:27.960
their cardiovascular function,
their resting heart rate,

00:25:27.960 --> 00:25:29.040
their blood pressure.

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:33.080
It will be markedly healthier
than folks who don't exercise.

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:35.240
It is extraordinarily
clear that type of exercise

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:36.950
is very important
for chronic disease

00:25:36.950 --> 00:25:38.970
management, no doubt about it.

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:43.430
However, it is not sufficient
for overall global health

00:25:43.430 --> 00:25:46.340
because it does almost
nothing for leg strength,

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:48.110
for any other marker
of health, which

00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:50.450
we can talk about, what are
the things that are actually

00:25:50.450 --> 00:25:54.260
going to predict mortality,
morbidity than most.

00:25:54.260 --> 00:25:59.220
So was a big smashing
indication that's like,

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:00.840
hey, this is great.

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:03.220
However, you're leaving
things on the table

00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:04.220
for your overall health.

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:05.930
Now, one could
argue, they're 80,

00:26:05.930 --> 00:26:09.110
and they're doing pretty well,
but they weren't doing as well

00:26:09.110 --> 00:26:09.960
in these areas.

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:12.500
And so a study we
did later, actually,

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:15.517
as a follow-up was looking
at monozygous twins.

00:26:15.517 --> 00:26:16.850
So this is actually interesting.

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:18.830
Being a scientist, this
is a classic example

00:26:18.830 --> 00:26:21.200
of one of my graduate students
who had been in my lab

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:23.060
for probably three
or four years.

00:26:23.060 --> 00:26:25.370
And she was in our single
fiber physiology lab.

00:26:25.370 --> 00:26:29.540
And you can imagine, she's
isolating individual muscle

00:26:29.540 --> 00:26:32.600
fibers, from an athlete,
one by one with a tweezer.

00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:35.960
And she's going to do several
thousand individual cells.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:39.945
So you're down there for hours,
and things happen down there.

00:26:39.945 --> 00:26:41.070
You kind of lose your mind.

00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:44.710
And she was going on one day
with one of my colleagues

00:26:44.710 --> 00:26:46.460
and just talking, and
she's like, oh yeah,

00:26:46.460 --> 00:26:49.430
my uncle is really, really
fit and something or other

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:50.060
and then.

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:51.385
Oh yeah, he's a twin.

00:26:51.385 --> 00:26:53.010
And I was like, oh,
is he a monozygous?

00:26:53.010 --> 00:26:53.810
And she's like, yeah.

00:26:53.810 --> 00:26:54.890
ANDREW HUBERMAN: For
those that don't know,

00:26:54.890 --> 00:26:56.270
monozygous are identical twins.

00:26:56.270 --> 00:26:57.170
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah,
which is interesting.

00:26:57.170 --> 00:26:59.170
So you basically have--
what I'm setting up here

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:02.510
is this is the perfect
exercise scientific experiment.

00:27:02.510 --> 00:27:06.860
Monozygous, identical twins mean
they have the exact same DNA.

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:10.340
So an egg was fertilized,
split, and then two humans

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:12.320
grew out of that with
the exact same DNA.

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:13.730
And so now we can
start answering

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:14.990
the question, well, yeah, OK.

00:27:14.990 --> 00:27:17.510
What about, maybe, these
cross-country skiers?

00:27:17.510 --> 00:27:19.190
Maybe they were
just genetic freaks.

00:27:19.190 --> 00:27:20.390
Maybe it didn't matter.

00:27:20.390 --> 00:27:21.830
It's like some
people have-- well,

00:27:21.830 --> 00:27:24.950
genetics are always a
component to it, but how much?

00:27:24.950 --> 00:27:26.870
Well, now we have a
scenario lining up

00:27:26.870 --> 00:27:27.560
where it's like, wait a minute.

00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:28.643
You have monozygous twins.

00:27:28.643 --> 00:27:31.183
So we have a replica of a
human being, exact same DNA.

00:27:31.183 --> 00:27:33.600
The only differences that we
would see in their physiology

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:36.590
now would be due to
lifestyle circumstances.

00:27:36.590 --> 00:27:37.640
Interesting.

00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:41.390
So monozygous twin
dad and uncle, right?

00:27:41.390 --> 00:27:42.050
Uh-huh.

00:27:42.050 --> 00:27:42.650
Great.

00:27:42.650 --> 00:27:43.910
Do they exercise?

00:27:43.910 --> 00:27:45.770
Well, one of them does.

00:27:45.770 --> 00:27:48.410
He's a lifelong endurance
exercise, runner, cyclist,

00:27:48.410 --> 00:27:50.910
swimmer, Ironman,
all these things.

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:52.440
What about the other one?

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:52.940
Nope.

00:27:52.940 --> 00:27:55.000
He doesn't exercise at all.

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:57.610
And at that point, I wanted
to kill my graduate student

00:27:57.610 --> 00:28:01.690
because I'm like, you've been in
my lab for three years or more,

00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:03.670
probably, and
you've never told me

00:28:03.670 --> 00:28:07.000
that in your household is the
perfect scientific experiment

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:09.760
for exercise you
could ever create.

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:12.857
And Jesus, the look on her
face when my colleague and I

00:28:12.857 --> 00:28:13.690
were staring at her.

00:28:13.690 --> 00:28:15.190
She's just like, oh my god.

00:28:15.190 --> 00:28:17.672
So I'm like, call
them right now.

00:28:17.672 --> 00:28:18.880
They are coming into the lab.

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:19.960
Fly them in from Chicago.

00:28:19.960 --> 00:28:21.430
I don't care what we have to do.

00:28:21.430 --> 00:28:22.640
We're getting them in.

00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:24.963
And so I wanted to--
actually, going back

00:28:24.963 --> 00:28:27.130
to the model that was first
developed by the Harvard

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:29.088
Fatigue Lab, one thing
that's interesting about

00:28:29.088 --> 00:28:31.300
that community is
they started off

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:34.090
with the concept of trying
to examine human performance

00:28:34.090 --> 00:28:35.230
through a holistic lens.

00:28:35.230 --> 00:28:39.190
And so it was the antithesis
of looking at either organ

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:40.862
by organ, so we're
going to only look

00:28:40.862 --> 00:28:42.070
at the cardiovascular system.

00:28:42.070 --> 00:28:43.600
We're only going to
look at skeletal muscle.

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:46.160
And then we're saying, we're
looking at this entire picture.

00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:48.520
And so that model, we wanted to
carry through in these twins.

00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:50.812
And I said, all right, I want
to bring them in the lab,

00:28:50.812 --> 00:28:52.830
but I'm not just going
to look at one system.

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:53.830
I want to do everything.

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:55.450
So we took stool samples.

00:28:55.450 --> 00:28:56.590
We took blood.

00:28:56.590 --> 00:28:58.540
We did vertical jump tests.

00:28:58.540 --> 00:28:59.860
We did maximum strength tests.

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:02.410
We did MRIs of muscle mass.

00:29:02.410 --> 00:29:05.790
We did VO2 max tests.

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:06.790
We did efficiency stuff.

00:29:06.790 --> 00:29:07.900
We did genetic testing.

00:29:07.900 --> 00:29:08.792
We did an IQ test.

00:29:08.792 --> 00:29:10.000
We did psychological battery.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:11.660
We wanted to look at
everything to figure out

00:29:11.660 --> 00:29:13.618
of these things, what
differ between the twins?

00:29:13.618 --> 00:29:17.870
And if so, the second key
question there is, by how much?

00:29:17.870 --> 00:29:20.890
So can I improve my VO2 max?

00:29:20.890 --> 00:29:21.430
Sure.

00:29:21.430 --> 00:29:23.500
Everyone knows
that, but how much?

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:26.267
Can it change by 5%, 80%?

00:29:26.267 --> 00:29:27.100
Where is the number?

00:29:27.100 --> 00:29:28.870
And so putting some
quantification on this

00:29:28.870 --> 00:29:30.470
was very important.

00:29:30.470 --> 00:29:32.530
And so again, we
had another example

00:29:32.530 --> 00:29:35.530
of a classic endurance-only
training paradigm

00:29:35.530 --> 00:29:37.672
compared to a non.

00:29:37.672 --> 00:29:39.130
So this is a person
who's, I think,

00:29:39.130 --> 00:29:41.160
he's truck driver by vocation.

00:29:41.160 --> 00:29:43.660
I think, actually, he drove for
a potato chip company, which

00:29:43.660 --> 00:29:45.070
was even funnier.

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:47.080
The endurance athlete
actually was great

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:48.970
because, like any
endurance people,

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:53.440
he had physical books of
all of his training mileage

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:55.198
for the last 35 years.

00:29:55.198 --> 00:29:56.740
And we just went
through them, and we

00:29:56.740 --> 00:29:58.660
calculated the total
amount of miles he ran,

00:29:58.660 --> 00:30:00.550
his averages, his
heart rates per time.

00:30:00.550 --> 00:30:02.988
We had this unbelievable
thing, what races he was in.

00:30:02.988 --> 00:30:04.030
He had the documentation.

00:30:04.030 --> 00:30:06.580
He was just totally nuts,
something that endurance people

00:30:06.580 --> 00:30:08.830
are like shaking their head
right now going, oh, yeah.

00:30:08.830 --> 00:30:09.580
I got that too.

00:30:09.580 --> 00:30:11.530
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And endurance
folks are pretty nerdy.

00:30:11.530 --> 00:30:12.280
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

00:30:12.280 --> 00:30:13.210
Super nerdy, right?

00:30:13.210 --> 00:30:16.960
So it was great because now we
could validate, as close as one

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:20.810
could, to actually how much
you ran and things like that.

00:30:20.810 --> 00:30:22.998
So they had about
a 35-year discord.

00:30:22.998 --> 00:30:24.790
They both exercised up
through high school.

00:30:24.790 --> 00:30:26.590
About 18, they stopped
doing it, and by the time

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:28.030
I got them in the lab,
they're in their mid-50s.

00:30:28.030 --> 00:30:29.960
So it was about 35
years of difference.

00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:32.680
And when we ran them
through the testing,

00:30:32.680 --> 00:30:35.860
if you look at the measures
that were similar to the Sweden

00:30:35.860 --> 00:30:37.840
study, it was almost identical.

00:30:37.840 --> 00:30:42.160
The exercising twin was
significantly better

00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:44.410
at things like a lipid
panel, resting heart

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:46.150
rate, blood pressure, VO2 max.

00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:50.140
Any of those markers, as
predicted, were much better.

00:30:50.140 --> 00:30:51.605
What was very
interesting, though,

00:30:51.605 --> 00:30:53.230
was the things that
were in the middle.

00:30:53.230 --> 00:30:55.960
First of all, their total
amount of muscle mass

00:30:55.960 --> 00:30:58.870
was almost identical,
to the gram,

00:30:58.870 --> 00:31:01.870
within the margin of error of
a DEXA scan could possibly ever

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:02.650
be.

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:06.280
The non-exerciser, though,
was a little bit fatter.

00:31:06.280 --> 00:31:08.260
So the difference in
actual body weight

00:31:08.260 --> 00:31:12.100
was explained almost entirely
by body fat or non-lean tissue,

00:31:12.100 --> 00:31:14.140
really, same sort of deal.

00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:14.890
So OK.

00:31:14.890 --> 00:31:16.750
No one's surprised
there that the exerciser

00:31:16.750 --> 00:31:18.700
was a little bit
leaner, even though it

00:31:18.700 --> 00:31:21.760
didn't change total amount
of muscle mass at all.

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:23.965
When we looked at some of
the more functional tests

00:31:23.965 --> 00:31:25.840
and we looked at things
like muscle quality--

00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:28.160
so this is a metric you
can get from an ultrasound.

00:31:28.160 --> 00:31:30.910
You can kind of think
about this as how much fat

00:31:30.910 --> 00:31:33.473
is inside the tissue,
which is sometimes

00:31:33.473 --> 00:31:35.890
an advantage for an endurance
athlete to have a little bit

00:31:35.890 --> 00:31:37.432
more of what are
called intramuscular

00:31:37.432 --> 00:31:39.890
triglycerides because it's a
fuel directly in the tissue.

00:31:39.890 --> 00:31:44.500
But in general,
the muscle quality

00:31:44.500 --> 00:31:48.180
he was not in favor
of the exerciser.

00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:50.030
If you looked at the
performance testing

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:55.390
and if you looked at strength,
it favored the non-exerciser.

00:31:55.390 --> 00:31:57.470
And so now, again, we
have the same finding

00:31:57.470 --> 00:32:00.380
we saw in our Sweden study,
but in identical twins.

00:32:00.380 --> 00:32:03.110
And so it really, really
highlighted the fact

00:32:03.110 --> 00:32:06.440
that if you want to move
forward with optimal health,

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:09.355
simply picking one silo is
not going to get you there.

00:32:09.355 --> 00:32:10.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
One silo meaning

00:32:10.730 --> 00:32:12.680
just running, just cycling?

00:32:12.680 --> 00:32:13.580
ANDY GALPIN: Right.

00:32:13.580 --> 00:32:16.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Does this
mean that the twin that did not

00:32:16.310 --> 00:32:20.720
exercise could jump higher
or win an arm wrestling

00:32:20.720 --> 00:32:21.620
competition?

00:32:21.620 --> 00:32:23.240
Not that that's a
vital thing to be

00:32:23.240 --> 00:32:25.760
able to do, but just in
terms of measuring strength,

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:27.920
it's our isometric strength.

00:32:27.920 --> 00:32:31.910
Was the non-exercising twin
stronger or at least as strong

00:32:31.910 --> 00:32:33.080
as their exercising twin?

00:32:33.080 --> 00:32:35.160
ANDY GALPIN: Yes,
particularly in grip strength.

00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:35.670
Yeah.

00:32:35.670 --> 00:32:38.970
And any of the measures like
the vertical jump, leg extension

00:32:38.970 --> 00:32:41.610
power, and a number
of things, they often

00:32:41.610 --> 00:32:44.343
favored the non-exerciser,
which you're still

00:32:44.343 --> 00:32:45.760
a little bit of a
chicken and egg.

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:48.360
You don't know if, necessarily,
the endurance training reduced

00:32:48.360 --> 00:32:50.250
that other twin's strength.

00:32:50.250 --> 00:32:52.120
It doesn't even
really matter, per se.

00:32:52.120 --> 00:32:54.780
I think the highlight
of it is, can you

00:32:54.780 --> 00:32:57.120
change some of these
metrics of VO2 max?

00:32:57.120 --> 00:32:59.220
Yeah, not even close.

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:02.320
These things are very responsive
regardless of your genetics.

00:33:02.320 --> 00:33:05.280
Your genetics will give you a
starting place very clearly.

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:07.980
Even the non-exerciser
was a pretty healthy guy.

00:33:07.980 --> 00:33:09.240
So they were in a good spot.

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:10.950
Mid-50s, doesn't
exercise, doesn't really

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:12.360
pay attention to
his diet at all,

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:14.290
and he was in a
pretty good shape.

00:33:14.290 --> 00:33:21.030
However, if you want to actually
move progress and move forward

00:33:21.030 --> 00:33:25.380
high functionality, you have
to do something besides just

00:33:25.380 --> 00:33:27.000
run, just distance run.

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:29.850
Now, I could say the same
thing for strength training.

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:31.278
That alone--
because I don't want

00:33:31.278 --> 00:33:33.570
to make this thing like I'm
saying endurance exercises.

00:33:33.570 --> 00:33:34.470
It worked.

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:36.930
In both cases,
both these studies,

00:33:36.930 --> 00:33:40.650
those folks were much
better off in metrics

00:33:40.650 --> 00:33:42.810
that are incredibly
important to mortality,

00:33:42.810 --> 00:33:45.900
how long you're going to
live, VO2 max, et cetera.

00:33:45.900 --> 00:33:49.110
It's just not going to get
there in terms of strength.

00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:51.712
We took a look at
muscle fiber physiology

00:33:51.712 --> 00:33:53.170
as well, which is
very interesting.

00:33:53.170 --> 00:33:54.810
So what I mean is
there's generally

00:33:54.810 --> 00:33:58.440
two types of muscle fibers,
fast-twitch and slow-twitch.

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:01.320
And one of the things that
is a hallmark of aging

00:34:01.320 --> 00:34:05.380
is a selective reduction
in fast-twitch fibers.

00:34:05.380 --> 00:34:07.740
And that's because it's
difficult to activate them

00:34:07.740 --> 00:34:10.260
unless you're doing
high-force activities.

00:34:10.260 --> 00:34:12.659
You're going to activate
slow-twitch fibers doing almost

00:34:12.659 --> 00:34:14.500
any activity of daily living.

00:34:14.500 --> 00:34:16.025
And so they stay around.

00:34:16.025 --> 00:34:17.400
Fast-twitch fibers,
unless you're

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:19.603
doing something of high
force or going, not be used.

00:34:19.603 --> 00:34:21.270
And they're not going
to be kept around.

00:34:21.270 --> 00:34:22.710
And that's a
problem because when

00:34:22.710 --> 00:34:25.350
you look at things like
the need for leg strength

00:34:25.350 --> 00:34:28.650
through aging, the ability to
catch yourself from a fall,

00:34:28.650 --> 00:34:30.360
these things are
incredibly important.

00:34:30.360 --> 00:34:32.040
If you don't have
fast-twitch fibers,

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:34.110
you don't have the speed to get
your foot out in front of you

00:34:34.110 --> 00:34:36.193
on time, and you don't
have the eccentric strength

00:34:36.193 --> 00:34:37.728
to stop the fall from happening.

00:34:37.728 --> 00:34:40.020
And so if you look across,
again, the aging literature,

00:34:40.020 --> 00:34:41.940
they're very clear
about the importance

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:45.460
of maintaining strength and
fast-twitch fibers over time.

00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:48.300
So we know that this is
an important distinction

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:49.710
here overall.

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:53.250
And people will often talk
about, how much of that

00:34:53.250 --> 00:34:54.420
is genetically determined?

00:34:54.420 --> 00:34:55.860
Can I change my fiber type?

00:34:55.860 --> 00:34:58.650
And the answer there
is resoundingly yes.

00:34:58.650 --> 00:35:00.450
And can I change
it with exercise?

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:02.520
And the answer is,
absolutely you can.

00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:05.040
And then the next
question is, how much?

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:07.590
So now, again, we're going
to see an order of magnitude.

00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:10.620
In general, without going
too far down an area

00:35:10.620 --> 00:35:13.620
that maybe we can
save for later,

00:35:13.620 --> 00:35:15.660
each one of your
muscles in your body

00:35:15.660 --> 00:35:18.150
has a different percentage of
fast-twitch and slow-twitch,

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:21.000
for example, your calf.

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:23.760
If you look at your soleus,
which is the smaller one that

00:35:23.760 --> 00:35:26.970
goes in the back, that's
generally mostly slow-twitch,

00:35:26.970 --> 00:35:29.632
typically 80% or so slow-twitch.

00:35:29.632 --> 00:35:31.840
The gastroc, which is the
other one right next to it.

00:35:31.840 --> 00:35:34.507
So if you were to point your toe
next to your face and that part

00:35:34.507 --> 00:35:37.267
that kind of flexes out
in the middle pops out,

00:35:37.267 --> 00:35:38.100
that's your gastroc.

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:40.080
That is almost the inverse.

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:42.750
So it's generally 80%
fast-twitch, maybe 20%

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:43.860
slow-twitch.

00:35:43.860 --> 00:35:46.260
Generally, anything anti
postural or postural,

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:48.378
rather, anti gravity,
spinal erectors,

00:35:48.378 --> 00:35:50.670
things that are meant to keep
you up or moving all day,

00:35:50.670 --> 00:35:51.600
are going to be slow-twitch.

00:35:51.600 --> 00:35:53.975
And things like your hamstrings,
which are for explosion,

00:35:53.975 --> 00:35:55.660
are going to be fast-twitch.

00:35:55.660 --> 00:35:58.370
Well, we biopsied the
quad in these individuals.

00:35:58.370 --> 00:36:02.080
And in that muscle, it's
generally about 50/50,

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:06.010
fast-twitch, slow-twitch,
as a really broad number.

00:36:06.010 --> 00:36:09.310
Well, one of the things that we
found was in the non-exerciser,

00:36:09.310 --> 00:36:11.800
it was almost textbook
what you would predict.

00:36:11.800 --> 00:36:15.820
It was about 50%
or so slow-twitch,

00:36:15.820 --> 00:36:18.640
a little bit of percentage of
fast-twitch, and then about 20%

00:36:18.640 --> 00:36:20.840
or what are these
called hybrid fibers,

00:36:20.840 --> 00:36:22.610
which are a hallmark
of an activity.

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:23.110
All right.

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:23.860
Great.

00:36:23.860 --> 00:36:28.070
In the exerciser, it was
about 95% slow-twitch.

00:36:28.070 --> 00:36:29.870
And so it's extremely
clear-- again,

00:36:29.870 --> 00:36:33.140
I don't know if maybe their set
point was a little bit higher

00:36:33.140 --> 00:36:36.992
towards that, and the
non-exerciser devolved down

00:36:36.992 --> 00:36:39.200
to his place or the other
one, but it doesn't matter.

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:41.510
I mean, you're going from
40% slow-twitch in one case

00:36:41.510 --> 00:36:44.310
to 95% slow-twitch
in another case.

00:36:44.310 --> 00:36:47.960
It shows you that the limits
of physiological adaptation

00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:51.090
are darn near boundless
given enough exposure.

00:36:51.090 --> 00:36:54.380
In this case, 35 years of
extremely consistent training,

00:36:54.380 --> 00:36:56.870
and his muscle
morphology was completely

00:36:56.870 --> 00:37:01.220
different than his identical
twin with the exact same DNA.

00:37:01.220 --> 00:37:03.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Those are two
beautiful examples of people

00:37:03.860 --> 00:37:06.690
doing endurance work
for a number of years

00:37:06.690 --> 00:37:08.390
and what that
gives them in terms

00:37:08.390 --> 00:37:12.320
of benefits and functionality.

00:37:12.320 --> 00:37:16.070
Has the opposite experiment
been done or observed,

00:37:16.070 --> 00:37:17.930
where somebody's
just weight lifted

00:37:17.930 --> 00:37:21.840
or just sprinted for
a number of years?

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:25.022
I don't know that there's
a identical twin control.

00:37:25.022 --> 00:37:25.730
That's a little--

00:37:25.730 --> 00:37:25.790
ANDY GALPIN: No.

00:37:25.790 --> 00:37:27.080
I wish we had a third twin.

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:27.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--too much to ask for.

00:37:27.770 --> 00:37:28.700
Right, triplets.

00:37:28.700 --> 00:37:32.547
So triplets out there, if you're
exercising in different ways

00:37:32.547 --> 00:37:34.130
or people who have
triplets, maybe you

00:37:34.130 --> 00:37:36.410
assign one kid to be a runner,
one kid to be a weightlifter,

00:37:36.410 --> 00:37:37.827
and the other one
to be sedentary.

00:37:37.827 --> 00:37:39.630
Please don't do
experiments like that.

00:37:39.630 --> 00:37:43.440
But the expectation,
as I understand it,

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:47.360
would be that the person
that sprints or that does

00:37:47.360 --> 00:37:51.930
heavy squats, explosive
work, would then

00:37:51.930 --> 00:37:54.480
have more fast-twitch
muscle fibers in their quad.

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:58.050
And their non-exercising
counterpart would have fewer.

00:37:58.050 --> 00:37:58.980
That would make sense.

00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:04.020
But what happens if you assess
the endurance level in somebody

00:38:04.020 --> 00:38:06.810
who's just done strength
training or just sprinted?

00:38:06.810 --> 00:38:07.560
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

00:38:07.560 --> 00:38:09.990
So we don't have those
data specifically.

00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:13.200
We're actually just starting
to have studies come out

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:15.235
on lifelong strength trainers.

00:38:15.235 --> 00:38:16.860
And there's actually
a very good reason

00:38:16.860 --> 00:38:21.120
for this, which is a whole
story we can get into,

00:38:21.120 --> 00:38:23.670
but the quick
answer is, we don't

00:38:23.670 --> 00:38:25.440
have a lot of people
who've been lifting

00:38:25.440 --> 00:38:27.450
weights for 30-plus years.

00:38:27.450 --> 00:38:30.057
We have a whole swath
of people who've

00:38:30.057 --> 00:38:31.890
been doing endurance
training for that long.

00:38:31.890 --> 00:38:33.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is that
because fewer people

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:36.123
have been weight training or are
the weight trainers all dead?

00:38:36.123 --> 00:38:38.247
ANDY GALPIN: You've got
to go back to 1953, 1954.

00:38:38.247 --> 00:38:40.080
You had two major things
happen that changed

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:42.660
the entire course of exercise
physiology and exercise science

00:38:42.660 --> 00:38:44.230
and, really, exercise
as we know it.

00:38:44.230 --> 00:38:47.510
It's important to understand
the history of our field.

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:51.170
A lot of the questions
I get are based

00:38:51.170 --> 00:38:56.030
on false assumptions of what
exercise can and can't do.

00:38:56.030 --> 00:38:59.060
As an example,
questions like momentum.

00:38:59.060 --> 00:39:01.130
Should I use momentum or
that's cheating, right?

00:39:01.130 --> 00:39:03.020
Or it doesn't work.

00:39:03.020 --> 00:39:04.830
It compromises my results.

00:39:04.830 --> 00:39:06.450
It's actually totally untrue.

00:39:06.450 --> 00:39:09.440
There are excellent reasons
when you should use momentum

00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:10.250
when you lift.

00:39:10.250 --> 00:39:11.990
There are reasons
when you should not.

00:39:11.990 --> 00:39:14.570
It is sometimes very
beneficial to go fast

00:39:14.570 --> 00:39:15.830
with the exercise repetitions.

00:39:15.830 --> 00:39:17.840
Sometimes very slow and
controlled is better.

00:39:17.840 --> 00:39:19.790
Any question I
get-- in fact, I'm

00:39:19.790 --> 00:39:24.170
very infamous for always
responding with, "It depends."

00:39:24.170 --> 00:39:27.230
The reason I say it depends
is it depends on the goal.

00:39:27.230 --> 00:39:30.110
When you're training for speed
or power or muscular endurance,

00:39:30.110 --> 00:39:33.590
the answer to some of these
very common question differs.

00:39:33.590 --> 00:39:35.870
What people fail
to realize is they

00:39:35.870 --> 00:39:38.900
think they're asking the right
question because they don't

00:39:38.900 --> 00:39:41.960
understand this history, what's
being planted in your brain

00:39:41.960 --> 00:39:44.720
subconsciously, is
driving that question.

00:39:44.720 --> 00:39:46.350
And it's not necessarily
the right one.

00:39:46.350 --> 00:39:48.080
So if we walk through
that a little bit,

00:39:48.080 --> 00:39:50.420
you'll see what
that field has led.

00:39:50.420 --> 00:39:52.460
Why you think
certain things matter

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:55.430
when they actually don't or
maybe your assumptions are

00:39:55.430 --> 00:39:57.822
incorrect and then exactly
what to do about them.

