00:40:50.880 --> 00:40:53.300
ANDY GALPIN: So
in 1953, 1954, you

00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:56.090
had Roger Bannister breaking
the four-minute mile,

00:40:56.090 --> 00:40:57.440
so subfour-minute mile.

00:40:57.440 --> 00:41:00.230
And then you also had Sir Edmund
Hillary and then his sherpa,

00:41:00.230 --> 00:41:04.100
Norgay, summit Everest in the
same, basically, two-year span.

00:41:04.100 --> 00:41:06.740
That exact same year after
that was the formation

00:41:06.740 --> 00:41:09.500
of what's called the American
College of Sports Medicine.

00:41:09.500 --> 00:41:11.480
Now, that is still around today.

00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:14.450
It is the preeminent group
for this Exercise As Medicine.

00:41:14.450 --> 00:41:17.390
So if you're interested
in things like exercise

00:41:17.390 --> 00:41:20.777
for obesity prevention, for
cancer treatment, for things

00:41:20.777 --> 00:41:22.610
like that-- it's not
really sports medicine.

00:41:22.610 --> 00:41:24.800
It's more for
clinical exercise--

00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:27.410
that's the place to go, American
College of Sports Medicine.

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:30.980
So we have this
launching of both a ton

00:41:30.980 --> 00:41:33.320
of people wanting to start
doing endurance exercise

00:41:33.320 --> 00:41:35.172
and start swimming and
cycling and running.

00:41:35.172 --> 00:41:37.130
And then you have a launch
of people coming off

00:41:37.130 --> 00:41:39.000
of the back of the
Harvard Fatigue Lab.

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:41.930
So the fatigue lab
actually shut down in 1947.

00:41:41.930 --> 00:41:44.720
So you have these people
interested in physical fitness,

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:46.160
but nowhere to go.

00:41:46.160 --> 00:41:48.440
Well, all those people left
the Harvard fatigue. lab

00:41:48.440 --> 00:41:50.760
and started their own
labs at other places.

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:52.760
So you've launched
the careers of people

00:41:52.760 --> 00:41:56.150
like Dave Costill
and John Holloszy

00:41:56.150 --> 00:41:59.090
and some of these very famous
exercise physiologists.

00:41:59.090 --> 00:42:00.680
And they start
building laboratories.

00:42:00.680 --> 00:42:02.510
And we start, for
the first time ever,

00:42:02.510 --> 00:42:04.940
studying the
science of exercise.

00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:07.550
So years go by, and
these people happen.

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:10.550
The 1960s the 1970s is what
we call the runner's boom.

00:42:10.550 --> 00:42:12.440
So people start--
in fact, if you

00:42:12.440 --> 00:42:14.930
look at the numbers of people
who are doing marathons,

00:42:14.930 --> 00:42:18.350
it explodes through
these two-decade spans

00:42:18.350 --> 00:42:21.500
because it's like, we could
do these endurance feats.

00:42:21.500 --> 00:42:24.473
Notice both those feats were
endurance, running short term

00:42:24.473 --> 00:42:25.640
as well as going over there.

00:42:25.640 --> 00:42:29.420
No one has thought anything
about strength training

00:42:29.420 --> 00:42:30.220
and here's why.

00:42:30.220 --> 00:42:33.910
In the late 1880s, there was a
very famous physician named--

00:42:33.910 --> 00:42:35.740
George Winship, I
think was his name,

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:37.720
who was a big proponent
of strength training.

00:42:37.720 --> 00:42:41.440
Well, he died in the age of 50
something of a heart attack.

00:42:41.440 --> 00:42:44.195
And that terrified people of
strength training for 70 years

00:42:44.195 --> 00:42:45.820
because they're like,
whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:42:45.820 --> 00:42:48.520
That stuff will kill you
because he was a doctor,

00:42:48.520 --> 00:42:49.870
he was trying-- he was
running around the country,

00:42:49.870 --> 00:42:52.240
doing these exhibitions and
reporting it, and then he died.

00:42:52.240 --> 00:42:54.040
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
sort of like Atkins--

00:42:54.040 --> 00:42:54.790
ANDY GALPIN: 100%.

00:42:54.790 --> 00:42:55.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --dying.

00:42:55.480 --> 00:42:57.605
Although some people say
he died of a heart attack.

00:42:57.605 --> 00:42:59.650
Other people said he
fell through the ice

00:42:59.650 --> 00:43:00.400
into cold water.

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:04.180
That's debated, but the
fact that a heavy proponent

00:43:04.180 --> 00:43:07.462
of a given nutrition
plan dies suddenly.

00:43:07.462 --> 00:43:08.170
ANDY GALPIN: Yep.

00:43:08.170 --> 00:43:09.795
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Not
good for business.

00:43:09.795 --> 00:43:14.170
ANDY GALPIN: So now the
little storm is brewing.

00:43:14.170 --> 00:43:16.330
1940s-- and I'm going
back a little bit,

00:43:16.330 --> 00:43:17.680
but bear with me for a second--

00:43:17.680 --> 00:43:19.930
there's a guy named
Peter Karpovich,

00:43:19.930 --> 00:43:23.530
and he's a scientist
out of Springfield,

00:43:23.530 --> 00:43:25.220
the decorated
physical education,

00:43:25.220 --> 00:43:27.970
PE, that's a legendary
place, Springfield College.

00:43:27.970 --> 00:43:30.920
And he is anti strength training
for a lot of the same reasons.

00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:33.160
In his entire career, he
talked about, don't do this.

00:43:33.160 --> 00:43:35.702
He's the one that launched these
ideas that strength training

00:43:35.702 --> 00:43:38.080
will make you lose
flexibility, it

00:43:38.080 --> 00:43:40.480
will be bad for kids,
all these things

00:43:40.480 --> 00:43:43.420
that we know now are
clearly not true.

00:43:43.420 --> 00:43:44.920
He's a proponent
of these things.

00:43:44.920 --> 00:43:47.710
And there is a
show that happened

00:43:47.710 --> 00:43:50.690
at Springfield College,
and a guy named Bob York--

00:43:50.690 --> 00:43:54.190
and if you-- York Barbell,
that's still around today--

00:43:54.190 --> 00:43:56.770
is going around the country and
putting on these exhibitions.

00:43:56.770 --> 00:43:58.300
They come to
Springfield, and it's

00:43:58.300 --> 00:44:02.412
sort of like a
new-age social media

00:44:02.412 --> 00:44:04.120
thing, where it's
like, the students know

00:44:04.120 --> 00:44:06.820
what's about to happen
because Karpovich shows up

00:44:06.820 --> 00:44:08.200
to this event.

00:44:08.200 --> 00:44:10.570
And everyone knows he
hates strength training.

00:44:10.570 --> 00:44:12.640
And everyone is like
waiting for it to end, just

00:44:12.640 --> 00:44:13.890
to see what he's going to say.

00:44:13.890 --> 00:44:15.370
So this whole
exhibition goes on,

00:44:15.370 --> 00:44:16.630
and these people are doing--

00:44:16.630 --> 00:44:18.160
you've got to remember back
at the time, bodybuilding,

00:44:18.160 --> 00:44:19.750
weightlifting, power
lifting, strength, strongman,

00:44:19.750 --> 00:44:21.100
it's all the same thing.

00:44:21.100 --> 00:44:22.990
There's no differentiation yet.

00:44:22.990 --> 00:44:24.880
And it finishes, and
Karpovich stands up,

00:44:24.880 --> 00:44:26.710
and the crowd goes silent.

00:44:26.710 --> 00:44:28.108
And he just asks one question.

00:44:28.108 --> 00:44:29.650
And he just points
to one of the guys

00:44:29.650 --> 00:44:31.947
and says, scratch your back.

00:44:31.947 --> 00:44:34.030
And now he's just assuming
and waiting for the guy

00:44:34.030 --> 00:44:35.770
to be like, ah and
not be able to put

00:44:35.770 --> 00:44:37.207
his hand behind his head.

00:44:37.207 --> 00:44:39.040
And I think he pointed
to John Grimek, who's

00:44:39.040 --> 00:44:40.630
a famous bodybuilder.

00:44:40.630 --> 00:44:43.082
And he reached back and
scratched his back, no problem.

00:44:43.082 --> 00:44:45.040
And then they proceeded
to grab two dumbbells--

00:44:45.040 --> 00:44:47.140
I think they were 50-pounds
dumbbells-- and do a backflip,

00:44:47.140 --> 00:44:49.025
standing backflip with
both in each hand.

00:44:49.025 --> 00:44:50.650
They started doing
the splits on stage,

00:44:50.650 --> 00:44:54.110
and they start performing all
kinds of physical function

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:54.610
tests.

00:44:54.610 --> 00:44:56.980
And Karpovich is stunned.

00:44:56.980 --> 00:44:58.970
He's like, holy-- he
has nothing to say.

00:44:58.970 --> 00:45:00.970
He leaves there, and his
whole life has changed.

00:45:00.970 --> 00:45:04.570
All these things he was claiming
were shown, in his face,

00:45:04.570 --> 00:45:05.710
to be false.

00:45:05.710 --> 00:45:07.690
He does a 180 on his career.

00:45:07.690 --> 00:45:10.300
He starts running study after
study on strength training

00:45:10.300 --> 00:45:12.430
and starts finding
immediately there

00:45:12.430 --> 00:45:14.680
are no detriments
to strength training

00:45:14.680 --> 00:45:16.180
in terms of global health.

00:45:16.180 --> 00:45:18.430
Of course, you can do it
wrong and things like that.

00:45:18.430 --> 00:45:21.110
And in fact, here comes a
whole bunch of benefits.

00:45:21.110 --> 00:45:22.990
So through the 1950s,
while this thing

00:45:22.990 --> 00:45:24.790
is going on with
the endurance folks,

00:45:24.790 --> 00:45:28.450
no one's still strength
training because there's

00:45:28.450 --> 00:45:29.410
no record to see.

00:45:29.410 --> 00:45:31.870
There's no American
College of Sports Medicine.

00:45:31.870 --> 00:45:32.800
There's no societies.

00:45:32.800 --> 00:45:33.610
There's no science.

00:45:33.610 --> 00:45:34.660
We're not sure it's safe.

00:45:34.660 --> 00:45:37.270
And meanwhile, Karpovich is
just hammering study after study

00:45:37.270 --> 00:45:39.520
after study showing you it's
safe, it's safe, is safe,

00:45:39.520 --> 00:45:40.780
but it hasn't picked up yet.

00:45:40.780 --> 00:45:43.880
And then everything
changed in 1977.

00:45:43.880 --> 00:45:45.500
Thank you, Arnold
Schwarzenegger.

00:45:45.500 --> 00:45:47.360
He came out with the trifold.

00:45:47.360 --> 00:45:49.430
He hits you with pumping
iron, which I know

00:45:49.430 --> 00:45:50.628
you know that movie, right?

00:45:50.628 --> 00:45:51.170
Pumping Iron.

00:45:51.170 --> 00:45:51.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
an interesting movie.

00:45:51.800 --> 00:45:54.012
Even for those not
interested in bodybuilding,

00:45:54.012 --> 00:45:55.970
it's a very interesting
movie because it really

00:45:55.970 --> 00:45:59.570
gives a window into not
just him, but the way

00:45:59.570 --> 00:46:03.000
in which weight training
started to show up

00:46:03.000 --> 00:46:04.885
as a regular practice.

00:46:04.885 --> 00:46:07.260
When I was growing up, the
only people who weight trained

00:46:07.260 --> 00:46:10.320
were people preparing for
football, bodybuilders,

00:46:10.320 --> 00:46:12.720
who basically didn't exist
in the town where I grew up.

00:46:12.720 --> 00:46:16.860
And the only people who did
yoga were yogis doing Bikram,

00:46:16.860 --> 00:46:20.340
but now you drive through
any major American city

00:46:20.340 --> 00:46:22.410
or European city and
there is yoga studios,

00:46:22.410 --> 00:46:24.840
there's gyms with free weights.

00:46:24.840 --> 00:46:25.897
ANDY GALPIN: Yup.

00:46:25.897 --> 00:46:27.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Arnold Schwarzenegger

00:46:27.480 --> 00:46:31.602
is largely responsible, I think,
for initiating that shift.

00:46:31.602 --> 00:46:32.310
ANDY GALPIN: Yup.

00:46:32.310 --> 00:46:33.268
Because think about it.

00:46:33.268 --> 00:46:37.440
He hit us with Pumping Iron,
Conan, and then The Terminator,

00:46:37.440 --> 00:46:40.660
almost in back-to-back--
very close, within years.

00:46:40.660 --> 00:46:43.320
So you've got this whole
cascade of the '70s of people

00:46:43.320 --> 00:46:45.440
running, cycling, and swimming.

00:46:45.440 --> 00:46:47.940
Now, science is starting to
come out that it's not dangerous

00:46:47.940 --> 00:46:49.357
and maybe, actually,
some benefit.

00:46:49.357 --> 00:46:50.070
And then boom.

00:46:50.070 --> 00:46:53.680
Not only is it not
bad for you, it

00:46:53.680 --> 00:46:58.130
can make you into a
real world superhero.

00:46:58.130 --> 00:47:00.020
I mean, think about the
psychology of a child

00:47:00.020 --> 00:47:02.900
growing up, watching
somebody like Conan.

00:47:02.900 --> 00:47:06.140
Think about what Batman looked
like in the 1950s and '60s.

00:47:06.140 --> 00:47:07.830
And then boom.

00:47:07.830 --> 00:47:09.807
I can look like that?

00:47:09.807 --> 00:47:11.640
Now, not everyone wants
to look like Arnold,

00:47:11.640 --> 00:47:13.920
but you see the power
that can land in people.

00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:16.140
No one had ever seen
or thought you can make

00:47:16.140 --> 00:47:17.520
your body transform like that.

00:47:17.520 --> 00:47:20.340
You could maybe be born
like that, but no chance.

00:47:20.340 --> 00:47:21.985
That's within the
grasp of all of you.

00:47:21.985 --> 00:47:23.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: When
I was a kid growing up,

00:47:23.860 --> 00:47:25.680
one of my favorite books was The
Guinness Book of World Records.

00:47:25.680 --> 00:47:26.460
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

00:47:26.460 --> 00:47:27.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
still have images

00:47:27.960 --> 00:47:31.370
in my mind of the
coldest animal.

00:47:31.370 --> 00:47:32.370
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, sure.

00:47:32.370 --> 00:47:34.470
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The
longest lifespan, et cetera.

00:47:34.470 --> 00:47:37.860
And there was a picture in
there of Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:47:37.860 --> 00:47:39.660
And you know what
his record was?

00:47:39.660 --> 00:47:41.590
It said, perfectly
developed man.

00:47:41.590 --> 00:47:42.840
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:44.040
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Which
is, as you point out,

00:47:44.040 --> 00:47:46.170
that isn't the physique
that most people aspire to.

00:47:46.170 --> 00:47:47.795
ANDY GALPIN: It
doesn't matter, though.

00:47:47.795 --> 00:47:49.930
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But it
did inspire this shift.

00:47:49.930 --> 00:47:51.638
The other thing about
resistance training

00:47:51.638 --> 00:47:57.060
that I think has a certain
allure for some people, men

00:47:57.060 --> 00:48:00.810
and women, is that it's one
of the few forms of exercise

00:48:00.810 --> 00:48:03.450
that because of the enhanced
blood flow to the muscle that

00:48:03.450 --> 00:48:05.580
occurs during the training,
the so-called "pump,"

00:48:05.580 --> 00:48:08.670
it gives you a transient
but somewhat real

00:48:08.670 --> 00:48:11.382
window into what
your results will be.

00:48:11.382 --> 00:48:12.090
ANDY GALPIN: 100%

00:48:12.090 --> 00:48:14.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: When you run
and you're gasping for air,

00:48:14.670 --> 00:48:16.350
you aren't
experiencing what it's

00:48:16.350 --> 00:48:18.150
like to be faster
than you are that day.

00:48:18.150 --> 00:48:18.580
ANDY GALPIN: That's correct.

00:48:18.580 --> 00:48:19.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
when you weight train,

00:48:19.410 --> 00:48:22.560
you get an aesthetic picture
into how your functionality

00:48:22.560 --> 00:48:23.730
and aesthetic will change.

00:48:23.730 --> 00:48:25.105
It disappears a
few hours later--

00:48:25.105 --> 00:48:25.855
ANDY GALPIN: Sure.

00:48:25.855 --> 00:48:28.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --as the
so-called "pump" subsides,

00:48:28.110 --> 00:48:31.552
but it's a very interesting
form of exercise in that way.

00:48:31.552 --> 00:48:33.510
It's almost as if you go
in to learn a language

00:48:33.510 --> 00:48:35.813
and during the process of
learning, for brief moments,

00:48:35.813 --> 00:48:37.980
you're actually fluent, and
then it gets taken away.

00:48:37.980 --> 00:48:38.280
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

00:48:38.280 --> 00:48:40.170
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So it puts
the dopamine carrot out

00:48:40.170 --> 00:48:40.900
in front of you.

00:48:40.900 --> 00:48:40.980
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

00:48:40.980 --> 00:48:42.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: This
is just me hypothesizing

00:48:42.510 --> 00:48:44.527
as to why weight training
might have taken off

00:48:44.527 --> 00:48:45.360
the way that it did.

00:48:45.360 --> 00:48:47.318
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, I
mean, it's like if you got

00:48:47.318 --> 00:48:49.470
paid every hour on the
hour when you were working.

00:48:49.470 --> 00:48:50.730
And then at the end
of the day, They

00:48:50.730 --> 00:48:52.080
take the money back,
but you still--

00:48:52.080 --> 00:48:54.622
as the time clock is going on
in your day, you're looking up,

00:48:54.622 --> 00:48:57.240
and you're watching your bank
account grow in real life.

00:48:57.240 --> 00:49:00.850
You can see why it's so
addicting to those folks.

00:49:00.850 --> 00:49:03.090
So to finish the
story here, going back

00:49:03.090 --> 00:49:04.440
to your actual question answer.

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:06.990
This is happening in the
late '70s, early '80s.

00:49:06.990 --> 00:49:10.440
And so now Joe Wieder,
all these gyms,

00:49:10.440 --> 00:49:13.780
they're exploding because
people want to look like that

00:49:13.780 --> 00:49:15.420
or they realize
they have the chance

00:49:15.420 --> 00:49:16.980
to change how they
physically look.

00:49:16.980 --> 00:49:19.020
That had never been
a reality before.

00:49:19.020 --> 00:49:20.190
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Mostly men
at that point, I'm guessing.

00:49:20.190 --> 00:49:21.780
ANDY GALPIN: Almost
exclusively, yeah,

00:49:21.780 --> 00:49:25.920
for a large number of reasons,
cultural acceptance, et cetera.

00:49:25.920 --> 00:49:28.800
Even with endurance stuff, you
could get fitter and run faster

00:49:28.800 --> 00:49:30.420
and that's better,
but it wasn't going

00:49:30.420 --> 00:49:33.660
to change how you looked
unless you were losing fat.

00:49:33.660 --> 00:49:35.280
Now you can change
how you look, which

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:36.510
is so incredibly addicting.

00:49:36.510 --> 00:49:38.497
In fact, there's a
very famous quote.

00:49:38.497 --> 00:49:40.080
I think it was
actually Joe Wieder who

00:49:40.080 --> 00:49:42.420
said, "Show me one man
who wants to be strong,

00:49:42.420 --> 00:49:45.310
and I'll show you 10 who
want to look strong."

00:49:45.310 --> 00:49:47.530
It's like, that's
very, very powerful.

00:49:47.530 --> 00:49:49.108
There's this a whole--

00:49:49.108 --> 00:49:50.900
there's tons of this
history I can go into,

00:49:50.900 --> 00:49:52.358
which is sort of
explaining to you.

00:49:52.358 --> 00:49:54.550
But now you know you're
in the mid- '80s,

00:49:54.550 --> 00:49:56.855
and you have what I
call my generation.

00:49:56.855 --> 00:49:59.230
So you have my generation,
who fall in love with strength

00:49:59.230 --> 00:50:02.710
training in the 1980s and
'90s, but there's really

00:50:02.710 --> 00:50:04.480
no scientific field for it.

00:50:04.480 --> 00:50:05.890
It's not really come about yet.

00:50:05.890 --> 00:50:08.500
The science of endurance
and exercise physiology

00:50:08.500 --> 00:50:10.690
is now humming along
at a massive rate

00:50:10.690 --> 00:50:12.850
because these people came
up in the '70s and '80s,

00:50:12.850 --> 00:50:15.730
and they're five, 10, 15
years in their career.

00:50:15.730 --> 00:50:16.510
They're producing.

00:50:16.510 --> 00:50:18.267
They're generating
graduate students.

00:50:18.267 --> 00:50:19.600
They're starting their own labs.

00:50:19.600 --> 00:50:21.850
And they exercise physiology,
still to this day,

00:50:21.850 --> 00:50:25.600
is 80% endurance, steady state
stuff almost exclusively.

00:50:25.600 --> 00:50:29.782
Well, now my generation,
you love sports.

00:50:29.782 --> 00:50:30.490
You love lifting.

00:50:30.490 --> 00:50:31.573
You love all these things.

00:50:31.573 --> 00:50:34.635
And now what we see happen
is the Chicago Bulls,

00:50:34.635 --> 00:50:36.760
Michael Jordan starts
picking up strength training.

00:50:36.760 --> 00:50:37.360
Ooh.

00:50:37.360 --> 00:50:37.960
That's on TV.

00:50:37.960 --> 00:50:41.020
He's on SportsCenter in the
mid 1990s, lifting weights.

00:50:41.020 --> 00:50:43.480
And we go back, actually,
to the late 1970s.

00:50:43.480 --> 00:50:45.430
And I'm not sure if
you're a football fan,

00:50:45.430 --> 00:50:47.830
but any football fan will
recognize the Nebraska

00:50:47.830 --> 00:50:50.350
Cornhuskers in
the 1970s and '80s

00:50:50.350 --> 00:50:52.030
changed how football's played.

00:50:52.030 --> 00:50:54.730
Well, the reason is because
they started strength training.

00:50:54.730 --> 00:50:57.220
And they started doing it with
a guy named Boyd Epley, who

00:50:57.220 --> 00:50:59.050
was the founder of the NFCA.

00:50:59.050 --> 00:51:01.450
So the National Strength
Conditioning Association

00:51:01.450 --> 00:51:03.980
is formed in the
late 1970s as well.

00:51:03.980 --> 00:51:06.880
So just like ASCM was developed
the year after those two

00:51:06.880 --> 00:51:10.690
events happened, 1978, the year
after Arnold comes out, boom,

00:51:10.690 --> 00:51:11.800
NFCA is formed.

00:51:11.800 --> 00:51:15.430
And now you have a scientific
organization dedicated

00:51:15.430 --> 00:51:16.660
to strength and conditioning.

00:51:16.660 --> 00:51:19.300
You've got NFL strength
conditioning coaches that

00:51:19.300 --> 00:51:20.620
are starting to come on board.

00:51:20.620 --> 00:51:23.037
You've got scientists that are
starting to come into labs.

00:51:23.037 --> 00:51:25.660
And strength conditioning
becomes a scientific field.

00:51:25.660 --> 00:51:29.800
Well, everything swings now,
from an exercise perspective,

00:51:29.800 --> 00:51:31.060
into bodybuilding.

00:51:31.060 --> 00:51:33.560
And so almost all
of the things--

00:51:33.560 --> 00:51:36.310
in fact, we were sort of
talking before, I could run

00:51:36.310 --> 00:51:37.870
a whole bunch of tricks on you.

00:51:37.870 --> 00:51:39.880
And I could ask you a whole
bunch of questions about things

00:51:39.880 --> 00:51:41.422
that you think are
absolute standards

00:51:41.422 --> 00:51:43.410
or guarantees about training.

00:51:43.410 --> 00:51:44.410
I'm supposed to do this.

00:51:44.410 --> 00:51:45.830
I'm never supposed to do that.

00:51:45.830 --> 00:51:46.780
ANDREW HUBERMAN: For instance?

00:51:46.780 --> 00:51:48.988
ANDY GALPIN: For instance,
is it OK to train a muscle

00:51:48.988 --> 00:51:51.628
group on back-to-back days?

00:51:51.628 --> 00:51:53.170
Most people are at
home thinking, no,

00:51:53.170 --> 00:51:54.550
you're not supposed
to train a muscle--

00:51:54.550 --> 00:51:56.092
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It
needs to recover.

00:51:56.092 --> 00:51:58.060
ANDY GALPIN: And
that's total nonsense.

00:51:58.060 --> 00:52:02.620
Other things like body part
split training, training one

00:52:02.620 --> 00:52:07.775
muscle group per day,
other things like cardio,

00:52:07.775 --> 00:52:09.400
endurance training
influencing, will it

00:52:09.400 --> 00:52:10.840
ruin my gains for my lift?

00:52:10.840 --> 00:52:14.620
All of these things are on
at a base of assumptions

00:52:14.620 --> 00:52:16.780
that come from bodybuilding.

00:52:16.780 --> 00:52:19.780
Now, that's a fantastic world,
but because everything started

00:52:19.780 --> 00:52:21.405
in the late 1970s
as bodybuilding,

00:52:21.405 --> 00:52:23.530
in terms of-- basically
strength training was that.

00:52:23.530 --> 00:52:27.763
Weightlifting and powerlifting
were not at all around.

00:52:27.763 --> 00:52:28.930
They were, but nobody cared.

00:52:28.930 --> 00:52:31.090
Again, show me someone
who wants to be strong.

00:52:31.090 --> 00:52:33.220
I'll show you 10 who
want to look strong.

00:52:33.220 --> 00:52:34.960
The physique thing
just dominated,

00:52:34.960 --> 00:52:37.107
and we're not getting
out of that yet.

00:52:37.107 --> 00:52:38.440
We're not all the way out of it.

00:52:38.440 --> 00:52:40.357
We're starting to, though,
because here's why.

00:52:40.357 --> 00:52:43.300
People started to realize,
this bodybuilding thing

00:52:43.300 --> 00:52:44.150
is fantastic.

00:52:44.150 --> 00:52:45.520
I can change my physique.

00:52:45.520 --> 00:52:49.760
I'm getting better, but
damn, these workouts

00:52:49.760 --> 00:52:52.340
take an hour and
a half, two hours.

00:52:52.340 --> 00:52:54.860
And I'm going to spend that
whole time on one or two body

00:52:54.860 --> 00:52:58.130
parts, which means I'm going to
have to lift six days a week,

00:52:58.130 --> 00:53:00.527
and I'm going to have
to do that consistently.

00:53:00.527 --> 00:53:01.610
Now all of a sudden, boom.

00:53:01.610 --> 00:53:04.093
Two hours on my elbow flexors.

00:53:04.093 --> 00:53:05.510
Damn, my elbow's
starting to hurt.

00:53:05.510 --> 00:53:07.260
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
yet, my understanding

00:53:07.260 --> 00:53:09.640
is that it doesn't really
require two hours a day--

00:53:09.640 --> 00:53:10.640
ANDY GALPIN: Not at all.

00:53:10.640 --> 00:53:11.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --of
training in order

00:53:11.360 --> 00:53:13.640
to get benefits, even
just for hypertrophy.

00:53:13.640 --> 00:53:14.690
ANDY GALPIN: Totally.

00:53:14.690 --> 00:53:16.190
But a lot of the
times, you're going

00:53:16.190 --> 00:53:17.870
to have to get
some amount of time

00:53:17.870 --> 00:53:20.660
in because you're spending
so much isolation.

00:53:20.660 --> 00:53:23.570
So we've gone away from
training movement, running

00:53:23.570 --> 00:53:26.180
as a movement,
cycling as a movement,

00:53:26.180 --> 00:53:29.842
training my biceps as a
muscle or muscle group,

00:53:29.842 --> 00:53:31.550
training my hamstrings
or a muscle group.

00:53:31.550 --> 00:53:32.750
That's not a human movement.

00:53:32.750 --> 00:53:35.870
So we've done a 180 in terms
of selecting the exercises

00:53:35.870 --> 00:53:40.040
from movement-based
prescription to now muscle

00:53:40.040 --> 00:53:40.935
group-based training.

00:53:40.935 --> 00:53:42.560
So when you're
isolating muscle groups,

00:53:42.560 --> 00:53:44.330
that means a whole
chunk of your body

00:53:44.330 --> 00:53:46.980
is really not doing
much throughout the day.

00:53:46.980 --> 00:53:49.790
So what happens if you're
doing, say, legs on Monday

00:53:49.790 --> 00:53:52.600
and you miss Monday
because you're on a flight?

00:53:52.600 --> 00:53:58.138
Now your legs have to
wait a whole other week,

00:53:58.138 --> 00:53:59.680
So this starts to
become problematic.

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:00.888
People start getting beat up.

00:54:00.888 --> 00:54:04.300
People start realizing, I
actually don't feel that great.

00:54:04.300 --> 00:54:05.350
I'm not super fit.

00:54:05.350 --> 00:54:07.060
I'm sweating just
walking up the stairs.

00:54:07.060 --> 00:54:07.810
I'm out of breath.

00:54:07.810 --> 00:54:08.320
Why?

00:54:08.320 --> 00:54:09.880
Because all that
training, you've

00:54:09.880 --> 00:54:11.800
done nothing for your
cardiovascular fitness.

00:54:11.800 --> 00:54:15.040
You've done nothing to improve
heart rate, oxygenation,

00:54:15.040 --> 00:54:15.830
blood flow.

00:54:15.830 --> 00:54:19.990
And so that paradigm swung way
too hard into the exercising,

00:54:19.990 --> 00:54:22.990
especially lifting
weights, is single joint,

00:54:22.990 --> 00:54:29.200
often machine, often slow, often
high-volume isolation stuff.

00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:31.430
And that left a giant
opening of people going,

00:54:31.430 --> 00:54:32.330
well, wait a minute.

00:54:32.330 --> 00:54:33.970
What if you could
get in the gym,

00:54:33.970 --> 00:54:36.400
I can promise you the
same or better result

00:54:36.400 --> 00:54:37.757
in under 30 minutes?

00:54:37.757 --> 00:54:39.340
And, in fact, you'll
also feel better.

00:54:39.340 --> 00:54:40.780
You'll lose more weight.

00:54:40.780 --> 00:54:43.570
And that opened up
group exercise classes,

00:54:43.570 --> 00:54:46.810
kettlebell stuff, CrossFit
type of stuff, circuit training

00:54:46.810 --> 00:54:48.978
because you can
come in, you won't

00:54:48.978 --> 00:54:50.770
get so beat up because
the volume is lower,

00:54:50.770 --> 00:54:52.300
the time is much lower.

00:54:52.300 --> 00:54:55.450
You get multiple adaptations
at the same time.

00:54:55.450 --> 00:54:56.030
Great.

00:54:56.030 --> 00:54:58.550
The problem with that,
though, fast forward 10 years,

00:54:58.550 --> 00:55:01.570
is it started burying
people because you've now

00:55:01.570 --> 00:55:05.740
de-emphasized movement
quality, and you've

00:55:05.740 --> 00:55:08.200
overemphasized scores.

00:55:08.200 --> 00:55:09.560
So this is a classic example.

00:55:09.560 --> 00:55:12.790
If you go and you watch Pumping
Iron or any bodybuilder,

00:55:12.790 --> 00:55:14.710
you'll see if they're
doing a bicep curl,

00:55:14.710 --> 00:55:17.350
they don't even really pay
attention to the rep range.

00:55:17.350 --> 00:55:18.670
They don't really pay
attention to the load.

00:55:18.670 --> 00:55:19.810
They are looking
at their muscle.

00:55:19.810 --> 00:55:20.680
They're trying to
figure out, how

00:55:20.680 --> 00:55:22.060
do I get that thing to fire?

00:55:22.060 --> 00:55:22.810
They're squeezing.

00:55:22.810 --> 00:55:23.350
They're flexing.

00:55:23.350 --> 00:55:24.760
They're posing at
the end of every set.

00:55:24.760 --> 00:55:27.093
They're trying to figure out,
am I getting enough pumps?

00:55:27.093 --> 00:55:30.580
It is exclusively founded
on exercise quality.

00:55:30.580 --> 00:55:33.850
The rep brains, the
numbers, almost irrelevant.

00:55:33.850 --> 00:55:37.780
When you go to the other
model, exercise technique,

00:55:37.780 --> 00:55:38.688
it doesn't matter.

00:55:38.688 --> 00:55:40.230
Just get the most
amount of weight up

00:55:40.230 --> 00:55:41.970
or the amount of reps
or the fastest time,

00:55:41.970 --> 00:55:42.770
et cetera, et cetera.

00:55:42.770 --> 00:55:43.020
High-intensity.

00:55:43.020 --> 00:55:43.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN: This
would be CrossFit.

00:55:43.830 --> 00:55:45.090
I've walked past some CrossFit.

00:55:45.090 --> 00:55:46.290
I've done two CrossFit classes.

00:55:46.290 --> 00:55:47.020
ANDY GALPIN: I don't
want to get sued.

00:55:47.020 --> 00:55:47.820
So you said "CrossFit."

00:55:47.820 --> 00:55:48.320
I didn't.

00:55:48.320 --> 00:55:50.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh,
no I enjoyed them.

00:55:50.110 --> 00:55:52.350
I definitely felt like
I was working hard.

00:55:52.350 --> 00:55:52.710
ANDY GALPIN: Oh, you will.

00:55:52.710 --> 00:55:54.502
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
observed a lot of people

00:55:54.502 --> 00:55:57.780
in very close proximity
doing Olympic lifts

00:55:57.780 --> 00:55:59.127
and doing kipping--

00:55:59.127 --> 00:56:00.960
that's where you kick
your legs, folks, say,

00:56:00.960 --> 00:56:04.860
sort of like bucking and
kipping type pull-ups. No.

00:56:04.860 --> 00:56:06.360
I enjoyed it.

00:56:06.360 --> 00:56:08.920
It wasn't for me
for the long term,

00:56:08.920 --> 00:56:13.200
but it did seem that there was
a lot of ballistic movement

00:56:13.200 --> 00:56:15.520
in close proximity
to other people.

00:56:15.520 --> 00:56:17.610
So the hazard to me
seemed more about that

00:56:17.610 --> 00:56:18.978
than the actual movement.

00:56:18.978 --> 00:56:21.270
ANDY GALPIN: Well, again,
the point I'm setting up here

00:56:21.270 --> 00:56:24.360
is, that was actually a
really brilliant solution

00:56:24.360 --> 00:56:26.700
for a lot of the problems
the classic bodybuilding

00:56:26.700 --> 00:56:28.210
hypertrophy introduced.

00:56:28.210 --> 00:56:30.000
So it got away from
isolation movements

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:32.040
and got people doing
big movements, which

00:56:32.040 --> 00:56:34.440
are more effective,
generally better.

00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:36.780
It got people doing
things fast and explosive.

00:56:36.780 --> 00:56:37.860
That's more athletic.

00:56:37.860 --> 00:56:39.480
That is more important
for longevity.

00:56:39.480 --> 00:56:41.610
It solved a lot of the problems.

00:56:41.610 --> 00:56:43.290
Joint health wasn't
getting crashed.

00:56:43.290 --> 00:56:48.390
The issue they went with is they
just pushed the pace on score

00:56:48.390 --> 00:56:49.740
rather than quality.

00:56:49.740 --> 00:56:51.630
They pushed the pace
on how many people can

00:56:51.630 --> 00:56:52.797
be in here at the same time.

00:56:52.797 --> 00:56:56.160
So now you're doing higher-risk
movements, higher intensity,

00:56:56.160 --> 00:56:58.712
higher fatigue,
and with a total--

00:56:58.712 --> 00:57:00.420
not that they don't
care about technique,

00:57:00.420 --> 00:57:02.795
but it's not the thing that
they're most concerned about.

00:57:02.795 --> 00:57:05.140
It's getting the number
and the thing done.

00:57:05.140 --> 00:57:06.660
They solved the
time issue, though.

00:57:06.660 --> 00:57:09.270
You can get tremendous
results in three days

00:57:09.270 --> 00:57:12.630
a week, under 45 minutes
each session, et cetera.

00:57:12.630 --> 00:57:13.920
Burn people out, though.

00:57:13.920 --> 00:57:16.260
Way too much high
intensity, way too often.

00:57:16.260 --> 00:57:18.600
And the other problem,
safety concerns,

00:57:18.600 --> 00:57:21.358
all kinds of orthopedic
issues and other stuff.

00:57:21.358 --> 00:57:23.400
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Can I
interrupt you for a moment

00:57:23.400 --> 00:57:25.108
and just ask a question,
as we go through

00:57:25.108 --> 00:57:29.310
this arc of the history of why
endurance training predominated

00:57:29.310 --> 00:57:32.100
or strength training or
bodybuilding type training

00:57:32.100 --> 00:57:33.912
or CrossFit type
training because I think

00:57:33.912 --> 00:57:36.120
this is fascinating, and I
know we're about to arrive

00:57:36.120 --> 00:57:37.155
at where we are today--

00:57:37.155 --> 00:57:37.500
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

00:57:37.500 --> 00:57:40.000
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --and what the
future looks like for people

00:57:40.000 --> 00:57:42.810
and what they should
focus on and do.

00:57:42.810 --> 00:57:45.750
At what point, if
any, do you think

00:57:45.750 --> 00:57:50.220
resistance training started
to become adopted by women?

00:57:50.220 --> 00:57:53.640
There was no equivalent
of Arnold Schwarzenegger

00:57:53.640 --> 00:57:55.770
there was Linda Hamilton
in The Terminator.

00:57:55.770 --> 00:57:56.520
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

00:57:56.520 --> 00:57:59.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: There are
some impressive physiques,

00:57:59.430 --> 00:58:03.840
certainly, on female
actresses and athletes.

00:58:03.840 --> 00:58:06.627
The Williams sisters,
very impressive

00:58:06.627 --> 00:58:07.710
musculature and physiques.

00:58:07.710 --> 00:58:10.002
And, of course, their tennis
playing speaks for itself.

00:58:12.500 --> 00:58:14.780
Has that happened yet?

00:58:14.780 --> 00:58:18.500
What I mean is, do you think,
since you work with both men

00:58:18.500 --> 00:58:22.760
and women, do you think
that most women understand

00:58:22.760 --> 00:58:25.880
that weight training,
done properly

00:58:25.880 --> 00:58:28.610
is going to be extremely
beneficial for them, maybe even

00:58:28.610 --> 00:58:32.060
especially for them, in terms
of offsetting bone density

00:58:32.060 --> 00:58:34.250
loss and things of that sort?

00:58:34.250 --> 00:58:39.470
Or are we still waiting
for the popular stimulus

00:58:39.470 --> 00:58:42.770
for getting 80% of
young women thinking,

00:58:42.770 --> 00:58:44.300
I want to lift weights?

00:58:44.300 --> 00:58:46.300
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

00:58:46.300 --> 00:58:49.670
Hard for me to answer
because I'm not a woman.

00:58:49.670 --> 00:58:51.030
Now, I have a daughter.

00:58:51.030 --> 00:58:53.030
She's four, so we'll see.

00:58:53.030 --> 00:58:55.592
What I can say is, I've
probably worked with--

00:58:55.592 --> 00:58:57.800
I don't know how many
professional athletes in total.

00:58:57.800 --> 00:58:58.580
A lot.

00:58:58.580 --> 00:59:01.370
I've worked with them probably
14 professional sports.

00:59:01.370 --> 00:59:03.740
I've worked with
Cy Young winners,

00:59:03.740 --> 00:59:06.620
MVPs, all the credentials.

00:59:06.620 --> 00:59:10.738
I bet 35%, 40% of the athletes
I've worked with are female.

00:59:10.738 --> 00:59:12.530
So I've worked with
Olympic gold medalists.

00:59:12.530 --> 00:59:14.900
I've worked with bronze
medallists in multiple sports.

00:59:14.900 --> 00:59:17.390
I've worked with the most
decorated powerlifter

00:59:17.390 --> 00:59:20.360
of all time in a number
of these areas, fighters,

00:59:20.360 --> 00:59:23.030
world championship,
all of these things.

00:59:23.030 --> 00:59:27.840
For me, I feel like that
burst has already happened.

00:59:27.840 --> 00:59:30.743
My students, if you
look at my classroom,

00:59:30.743 --> 00:59:32.160
I don't know what
the numbers are,

00:59:32.160 --> 00:59:34.680
but there is no small number
of females in exercise

00:59:34.680 --> 00:59:36.495
science and excess physiology.

00:59:36.495 --> 00:59:39.120
If you look at our laboratories,
that's one thing you will see.

00:59:39.120 --> 00:59:42.090
There are very few female
exercise scientists.

00:59:42.090 --> 00:59:45.750
There are very few female
strength conditioning coaches,

00:59:45.750 --> 00:59:50.010
but that number is coming
down at an astronomical rate.

00:59:50.010 --> 00:59:54.060
You have people that are
being hired in every sport.

00:59:54.060 --> 00:59:55.080
You pick the NFL.

00:59:55.080 --> 00:59:57.030
You pick Major League Baseball.

00:59:57.030 --> 00:59:58.950
Every few months we're
hearing, first female

00:59:58.950 --> 01:00:02.610
hired for this, first
female hired for that.

01:00:02.610 --> 01:00:05.675
The Yankees, Rachel
Balkovec is fantastic.

01:00:05.675 --> 01:00:07.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
Rachel's been out to my lab.

01:00:07.800 --> 01:00:08.425
She's terrific.

01:00:08.425 --> 01:00:09.175
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:00:09.175 --> 01:00:09.690
Oh, yeah.

01:00:09.690 --> 01:00:10.447
She's fantastic.

01:00:10.447 --> 01:00:12.030
I mean, she's now
being hired as the--

01:00:12.030 --> 01:00:14.430
I think she's a hitting coach
now, actual sport coach.

01:00:14.430 --> 01:00:15.672
She's going to be a GM.

01:00:15.672 --> 01:00:16.380
This is her goal.

01:00:16.380 --> 01:00:17.820
She's a terminator.

01:00:17.820 --> 01:00:18.987
So that's already happening.

01:00:18.987 --> 01:00:21.112
And my students that are
coming through our program

01:00:21.112 --> 01:00:22.530
are getting placed
in these roles.

01:00:22.530 --> 01:00:25.320
They haven't gotten
through yet a lot

01:00:25.320 --> 01:00:27.780
in terms of being
an actual scientist,

01:00:27.780 --> 01:00:29.910
but they're getting there.

01:00:29.910 --> 01:00:32.250
Sports scientists in the NBA
are being hired, females,

01:00:32.250 --> 01:00:33.480
in terms of big data collection.

01:00:33.480 --> 01:00:34.897
And sports science
and tech, we'll

01:00:34.897 --> 01:00:39.240
cover in another discussion,
but I think it's happening.

01:00:39.240 --> 01:00:42.240
Whether or not the
cultural and social--

01:00:42.240 --> 01:00:44.280
I can't speak to that
end of the equation.

01:00:44.280 --> 01:00:47.370
What I can speak
to, though, is one

01:00:47.370 --> 01:00:49.860
of the things I think is
most fun coming forward

01:00:49.860 --> 01:00:52.620
scientifically is a
number of years ago,

01:00:52.620 --> 01:00:55.590
NIH came through
with their mandates.

01:00:55.590 --> 01:00:58.620
They're saying, it's no longer
acceptable to exclude women

01:00:58.620 --> 01:01:02.748
from scientific research because
we just did that for decades.

01:01:02.748 --> 01:01:04.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
what happened-- just

01:01:04.540 --> 01:01:06.730
to fill this in because
I think it's worth

01:01:06.730 --> 01:01:11.410
noting is that for many
years, studies even on rodents

01:01:11.410 --> 01:01:13.960
were mainly carried
out on male rodents

01:01:13.960 --> 01:01:16.420
because the assumption--
and the assumption

01:01:16.420 --> 01:01:18.580
turned out to be wrong--
but the assumption was

01:01:18.580 --> 01:01:21.060
that the physiology
of female rodents--

01:01:21.060 --> 01:01:22.810
because they don't
have a menstrual cycle.

01:01:22.810 --> 01:01:23.560
It's not 28 days.

01:01:23.560 --> 01:01:24.643
They have an estrus cycle.

01:01:24.643 --> 01:01:27.070
It's four days, a
different type of cycle--

01:01:27.070 --> 01:01:29.380
that would somehow
disrupt the data.

01:01:29.380 --> 01:01:31.300
It turns out that's
entirely wrong.

01:01:31.300 --> 01:01:33.670
Now it's actually required.

01:01:33.670 --> 01:01:36.250
When you sit on a grant study
panel, which are the people who

01:01:36.250 --> 01:01:38.200
evaluate grants, they ask.

01:01:38.200 --> 01:01:41.560
They literally say, did they
meet the criteria for sex

01:01:41.560 --> 01:01:42.700
as a biological variable?

01:01:42.700 --> 01:01:44.575
Here we're not talking
about sex as the verb.

01:01:44.575 --> 01:01:46.360
We're talking about
sex as biological sex.

01:01:46.360 --> 01:01:50.300
And if you don't say yes, that's
a strong hit against the grant.

01:01:50.300 --> 01:01:53.360
And if you say yes, then
it checks off that box.

01:01:53.360 --> 01:01:56.380
So it's now required that
both male and female rodents

01:01:56.380 --> 01:01:58.180
and humans be studied
in a given study

01:01:58.180 --> 01:02:00.790
unless the study is specifically
geared toward understanding

01:02:00.790 --> 01:02:03.505
that only exists in one
or the other population--

01:02:03.505 --> 01:02:03.850
ANDY GALPIN: Reproductive
study, things like that.

01:02:03.850 --> 01:02:05.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --such as
menopause, for instance,

01:02:05.860 --> 01:02:06.250
ANDY GALPIN: Yup, totally.

01:02:06.250 --> 01:02:07.708
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--menstrual cycle,

01:02:07.708 --> 01:02:09.700
andropause, for instance.

01:02:09.700 --> 01:02:11.740
But no, this is
extremely important.

01:02:11.740 --> 01:02:12.968
I'm excited to hear that.

01:02:12.968 --> 01:02:15.010
ANDY GALPIN: So where I
was going to go with that

01:02:15.010 --> 01:02:17.275
is actually, so that was
step one, which is cool.

01:02:17.275 --> 01:02:18.400
You've got to include them.

01:02:18.400 --> 01:02:21.670
Where we haven't gotten to yet,
but I've seen more and more

01:02:21.670 --> 01:02:23.380
grant applications
come through for this,

01:02:23.380 --> 01:02:24.880
it's just the funding
hasn't it yet,

01:02:24.880 --> 01:02:28.750
which is, it's one thing to let
women be in the same studies.

01:02:28.750 --> 01:02:29.500
That's great.

01:02:29.500 --> 01:02:31.000
It's another thing,
though, to start

01:02:31.000 --> 01:02:34.450
performing high-performance
research specifically

01:02:34.450 --> 01:02:35.710
for female questions.

01:02:35.710 --> 01:02:37.000
That has not happened yet.

01:02:37.000 --> 01:02:38.410
And that's just a funding issue.

01:02:38.410 --> 01:02:39.760
We haven't gotten money yet.

01:02:39.760 --> 01:02:41.110
People aren't supporting that.

01:02:41.110 --> 01:02:43.570
We don't get a lot of financial
support for sports science,

01:02:43.570 --> 01:02:46.360
but we can't track down
the money yet of me going,

01:02:46.360 --> 01:02:48.100
I want to do a study
in female athletes

01:02:48.100 --> 01:02:50.380
that answers female
athlete questions.

01:02:50.380 --> 01:02:51.520
These won't help men.

01:02:51.520 --> 01:02:53.770
These are questions
specific to the female.

01:02:53.770 --> 01:02:55.060
That's the next step.

01:02:55.060 --> 01:02:57.220
That's where we've got
to get to so we can say,

01:02:57.220 --> 01:03:00.190
maybe we should do
things differently

01:03:00.190 --> 01:03:02.650
around training or recovery
or we shouldn't or it

01:03:02.650 --> 01:03:03.340
doesn't matter.

01:03:03.340 --> 01:03:06.010
There's a handful of--

01:03:06.010 --> 01:03:09.520
not lower-quality,
but some studies.

01:03:09.520 --> 01:03:10.720
I don't love them yet.

01:03:10.720 --> 01:03:12.370
There just needs to
be a ton of work.

01:03:12.370 --> 01:03:16.990
Birth control is a very
good example the information

01:03:16.990 --> 01:03:20.527
for women at female athletes
or even just hard exercisers--

01:03:20.527 --> 01:03:22.360
you don't have to be a
competitive athlete--

01:03:22.360 --> 01:03:24.130
around what is
birth control doing?

01:03:24.130 --> 01:03:24.918
What types?

01:03:24.918 --> 01:03:25.960
How should I manage that?

01:03:25.960 --> 01:03:29.350
What conversations should
I be having with my doctor?

01:03:29.350 --> 01:03:30.100
Almost nothing.

01:03:30.100 --> 01:03:32.680
Women have nothing to go on
for high-performance stuff.

01:03:32.680 --> 01:03:36.490
So what if I'm trying to compete
in an event or run a race?

01:03:36.490 --> 01:03:39.820
All those types of questions
should be answered.

01:03:39.820 --> 01:03:43.690
Normative value, normative
data, performance testing.

01:03:43.690 --> 01:03:45.260
It's just not there
on the female.

01:03:45.260 --> 01:03:46.010
So that's an area.

01:03:46.010 --> 01:03:48.812
I think-- if somebody really
wanted to make a change,

01:03:48.812 --> 01:03:50.020
the scientists want to do it.

01:03:50.020 --> 01:03:50.520
I know it.

01:03:50.520 --> 01:03:52.000
I've talked to so
many in our field

01:03:52.000 --> 01:03:54.250
that would really
love to explore it

01:03:54.250 --> 01:03:56.080
because it's getting there.

01:03:56.080 --> 01:03:58.240
Like I said, the coaching
side is getting there.

01:03:58.240 --> 01:03:58.990
They're seeing it.

01:03:58.990 --> 01:03:59.980
They're hiring these people.

01:03:59.980 --> 01:04:01.188
I'm seeing it in my students.

01:04:04.010 --> 01:04:05.800
My followings is not all men.

01:04:05.800 --> 01:04:07.480
It's a very large
percentage of females,

01:04:07.480 --> 01:04:09.970
and all I do is post
about exercise science.

01:04:09.970 --> 01:04:11.080
This is all I do.

01:04:11.080 --> 01:04:13.060
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
this podcast is very--

01:04:13.060 --> 01:04:16.240
we know very clearly the
audience is 50% women,

01:04:16.240 --> 01:04:16.978
50% men--

01:04:16.978 --> 01:04:18.020
ANDY GALPIN: That's nice.

01:04:18.020 --> 01:04:19.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--which is great.

01:04:19.130 --> 01:04:21.430
ANDY GALPIN: So just to jump
back in our history discussion

01:04:21.430 --> 01:04:23.380
and to finish that point
of where we're at now

01:04:23.380 --> 01:04:26.380
and where I think we're
going to go or should go.

01:04:26.380 --> 01:04:30.280
So we walked through the
bodybuilding running everything

01:04:30.280 --> 01:04:31.660
and people walking into a gym.

01:04:31.660 --> 01:04:34.240
Any time they lift
weights, they're

01:04:34.240 --> 01:04:36.400
making all of their choices
based on the assumption

01:04:36.400 --> 01:04:38.800
that maximizing muscle
size is the goal.

01:04:38.800 --> 01:04:40.490
And clearly, that's
not the case.

01:04:40.490 --> 01:04:43.690
There are other adaptations
you may be after.

01:04:43.690 --> 01:04:45.630
So we talked about
how that had problems,

01:04:45.630 --> 01:04:47.005
and then we talked
about how some

01:04:47.005 --> 01:04:50.480
of these other forms of
exercise filled those gaps

01:04:50.480 --> 01:04:53.435
and then what problems
those things introduced.

01:04:53.435 --> 01:04:55.060
Well, I think we're
actually at a point

01:04:55.060 --> 01:04:58.210
where that pendulum is slowly
shifting into the middle.

01:04:58.210 --> 01:04:59.917
What I mean by that
is, if you want

01:04:59.917 --> 01:05:02.500
to maximize muscle strength, we
look towards the power lifting

01:05:02.500 --> 01:05:03.000
community.

01:05:03.000 --> 01:05:04.990
If you want to
maximize muscle power,

01:05:04.990 --> 01:05:07.115
we're going to look to the
weightlifting community.

01:05:07.115 --> 01:05:09.700
If you want to look for muscular
endurance, well-roundedness,

01:05:09.700 --> 01:05:11.492
maybe we look into the
CrossFit communities

01:05:11.492 --> 01:05:14.010
and some of these obstacle
course races or functionality

01:05:14.010 --> 01:05:14.510
things.

01:05:14.510 --> 01:05:17.920
So what we can do now
is generate protocols

01:05:17.920 --> 01:05:20.680
that get us the exact
adaptations we want

01:05:20.680 --> 01:05:23.770
and not ones we don't want,
because we can look back

01:05:23.770 --> 01:05:26.350
at each of these different
styles of training

01:05:26.350 --> 01:05:29.080
and pick and choose
optimal protocols

01:05:29.080 --> 01:05:30.200
or combinations for them.

01:05:30.200 --> 01:05:32.500
So if somebody simply
wants to get healthy,

01:05:32.500 --> 01:05:35.410
like we talked about when we
listed the nine adaptations

01:05:35.410 --> 01:05:37.390
and I mentioned health
wasn't one of them,

01:05:37.390 --> 01:05:39.820
that's because what
determines your health

01:05:39.820 --> 01:05:42.220
versus what determines my
optimal health differs.

01:05:42.220 --> 01:05:45.460
So if I need more hypertrophy,
I can look towards bodybuilding

01:05:45.460 --> 01:05:47.980
concepts, but if I
have enough or maybe

01:05:47.980 --> 01:05:50.050
for personal reasons, I
decide I have too much

01:05:50.050 --> 01:05:53.060
or I don't want to add any
more, then I can say, hey,

01:05:53.060 --> 01:05:55.300
how can I get stronger
without getting bigger?

01:05:55.300 --> 01:05:57.490
And boom, I look towards
powerlifting concepts.

01:05:57.490 --> 01:05:58.940
How can I get more powerful?

01:05:58.940 --> 01:06:03.010
How can I get faster but I
don't, again, want to lose fat?

01:06:03.010 --> 01:06:03.730
OK, great.

01:06:03.730 --> 01:06:05.050
Or if I want physique changes.

01:06:05.050 --> 01:06:06.550
So we have all these
different areas

01:06:06.550 --> 01:06:09.760
we can pick and choose
from that have expertise

01:06:09.760 --> 01:06:12.790
in specific adaptations
and develop ourselves

01:06:12.790 --> 01:06:15.315
perfect protocols based
on that information.

