01:07:34.060 --> 01:07:36.460
Can you walk us through the
nine different adaptations

01:07:36.460 --> 01:07:39.670
that you mentioned
earlier and give us

01:07:39.670 --> 01:07:44.050
a way to assess our
level of ability

01:07:44.050 --> 01:07:46.572
or our level of adaptation
in each of those nine?

01:07:46.572 --> 01:07:47.530
ANDY GALPIN: All right.

01:07:47.530 --> 01:07:50.200
The very first one we want to
talk about is movement skill.

01:07:50.200 --> 01:07:53.170
Now, set aside sport specific.

01:07:53.170 --> 01:07:55.870
So I'm not going to give you
an assessment for optimal golf

01:07:55.870 --> 01:07:56.680
technique swing.

01:07:56.680 --> 01:07:58.610
This is really
about human movement

01:07:58.610 --> 01:08:00.893
so that you stay
injury free and you

01:08:00.893 --> 01:08:02.810
can continue to train
for as long as possible.

01:08:02.810 --> 01:08:04.352
So what are the
minimum requirements?

01:08:04.352 --> 01:08:08.170
Now, if you can have access
to a highly-qualified physical

01:08:08.170 --> 01:08:10.090
therapist or
movement specialist,

01:08:10.090 --> 01:08:11.530
that's the best route.

01:08:11.530 --> 01:08:12.340
Go to them.

01:08:12.340 --> 01:08:15.790
Have them identify all of
your movement patterns,

01:08:15.790 --> 01:08:19.240
overhead pressing, squatting,
running, all these things.

01:08:19.240 --> 01:08:20.380
That's your gold standard.

01:08:20.380 --> 01:08:22.270
If you want to do it
yourself, though, here

01:08:22.270 --> 01:08:25.520
is a very simple
four-step solution.

01:08:25.520 --> 01:08:28.510
So the way that I teach
this is I go joint by joint.

01:08:28.510 --> 01:08:31.750
And so I think of this as the
major ones, your shoulder,

01:08:31.750 --> 01:08:36.770
your elbow, your low back,
hip, knee, and ankle.

01:08:36.770 --> 01:08:39.100
Now, what you can do is do
a representative movement

01:08:39.100 --> 01:08:39.950
for you.

01:08:39.950 --> 01:08:41.890
So if you bench a
lot, use the bench.

01:08:41.890 --> 01:08:44.200
If you do pull-ups,
use the pull-up.

01:08:44.200 --> 01:08:45.460
If you squat, do that.

01:08:45.460 --> 01:08:47.830
I would recommend doing
an upper-body press,

01:08:47.830 --> 01:08:51.250
an upper-body pull, a lower-body
press, a lower-body pull.

01:08:51.250 --> 01:08:56.319
An example would be a push-up,
a pull-up, or a bent row,

01:08:56.319 --> 01:08:58.270
a squat, and then a deadlift.

01:08:58.270 --> 01:09:01.327
That would be a very, very
well-rounded approach.

01:09:01.327 --> 01:09:03.160
What you're going to
do is do that movement.

01:09:03.160 --> 01:09:06.120
And I would record
it for yourself.

01:09:06.120 --> 01:09:09.340
And record a frontal
view and a side view.

01:09:09.340 --> 01:09:13.800
Probably do three to 10
repetitions per angle,

01:09:13.800 --> 01:09:15.147
slow and controlled.

01:09:15.147 --> 01:09:16.439
You don't need any body weight.

01:09:16.439 --> 01:09:20.040
What you want to do is move, and
you want to look for key things

01:09:20.040 --> 01:09:21.689
at every joint.

01:09:21.689 --> 01:09:23.707
So again, imagine
I'm doing a squat.

01:09:23.707 --> 01:09:25.290
I'm going to do a
squat, and I'm going

01:09:25.290 --> 01:09:26.580
to focus on just my ankle.

01:09:26.580 --> 01:09:28.830
And I'm going look for these
four things of the ankle.

01:09:28.830 --> 01:09:31.200
And then I'm going to go
back and watch my knee

01:09:31.200 --> 01:09:33.359
and look for these same
four things of the knee

01:09:33.359 --> 01:09:35.220
through the hip, et cetera.

01:09:35.220 --> 01:09:36.700
So what are these four things?

01:09:36.700 --> 01:09:38.819
Number one is you want
to look for symmetry.

01:09:38.819 --> 01:09:41.220
So symmetry is
front to back, left

01:09:41.220 --> 01:09:45.420
to right, and your right
limb and your left limb.

01:09:45.420 --> 01:09:48.300
And so what we want to
look for are if they aren't

01:09:48.300 --> 01:09:49.840
moving perfectly, that's fine.

01:09:49.840 --> 01:09:51.758
But you want to see
is one moving further

01:09:51.758 --> 01:09:52.800
ahead than the other one?

01:09:52.800 --> 01:09:55.680
Is one turning to the
side and one's not?

01:09:55.680 --> 01:09:59.080
Is one fidgeting and
twitching around differently?

01:09:59.080 --> 01:10:00.750
So you want to
look just to check,

01:10:00.750 --> 01:10:02.458
to see and make sure
that they're stable.

01:10:02.458 --> 01:10:03.300
That's one.

01:10:03.300 --> 01:10:05.860
Number two, you want
to look for stability.

01:10:05.860 --> 01:10:07.590
So a key indicators
here are things

01:10:07.590 --> 01:10:11.970
if you can't get through
Squat, a controlled squat,

01:10:11.970 --> 01:10:14.212
where your knees
don't start shaking.

01:10:14.212 --> 01:10:15.670
That would be an
instability issue.

01:10:15.670 --> 01:10:17.920
So can you do the movement slow?

01:10:17.920 --> 01:10:20.440
Can you pause at the bottom,
maybe three seconds, maybe five

01:10:20.440 --> 01:10:21.180
seconds or 10?

01:10:21.180 --> 01:10:24.330
You should have complete
control of that movement

01:10:24.330 --> 01:10:26.700
and all of these joints.

01:10:26.700 --> 01:10:29.100
Are your hips sliding to
one side when you stand up?

01:10:29.100 --> 01:10:32.310
Is one elbow closer to your
body when you're benching

01:10:32.310 --> 01:10:33.810
and the other one's
more flared out?

01:10:33.810 --> 01:10:35.590
These are the things
I'm talking about.

01:10:35.590 --> 01:10:39.060
I'm not worried about what
angle they should be at or not.

01:10:39.060 --> 01:10:42.250
You're simply looking for
asymmetries or instabilities.

01:10:42.250 --> 01:10:43.800
So again, as you're
pushing up, does

01:10:43.800 --> 01:10:45.960
one elbow start flipping
and twitching and going

01:10:45.960 --> 01:10:46.930
all over the place?

01:10:46.930 --> 01:10:49.260
The third one is what
I call awareness.

01:10:49.260 --> 01:10:52.800
So there are a lot of
movement technique issues

01:10:52.800 --> 01:10:54.480
that, simply, people don't know.

01:10:54.480 --> 01:10:55.860
And so you'll watch them squat.

01:10:55.860 --> 01:10:57.402
I do this in my
classes all the time.

01:10:57.402 --> 01:10:59.205
I'll have 100 kids
out there, squatting.

01:10:59.205 --> 01:11:01.080
And you'll see some
horrible squat technique.

01:11:01.080 --> 01:11:02.710
And then when you
just tell them, hey,

01:11:02.710 --> 01:11:05.270
did you realize your heels are
supposed to be on the ground

01:11:05.270 --> 01:11:06.312
all times when you squat?

01:11:06.312 --> 01:11:07.200
They're like, oh, OK.

01:11:07.200 --> 01:11:08.160
And they can correct it.

01:11:08.160 --> 01:11:09.577
It's not actually
a movement flaw.

01:11:09.577 --> 01:11:11.010
It was just simply an awareness.

01:11:11.010 --> 01:11:12.630
I didn't know, and
then I actually

01:11:12.630 --> 01:11:15.090
didn't realize that was
happening that position.

01:11:15.090 --> 01:11:16.770
So we want all of
our joints to be

01:11:16.770 --> 01:11:18.760
going through a general
full range of motion,

01:11:18.760 --> 01:11:19.770
which is number four.

01:11:19.770 --> 01:11:22.740
So the ankle-- during
a squat, your knees

01:11:22.740 --> 01:11:25.380
should be able to go as far
over your toe as possible while

01:11:25.380 --> 01:11:27.030
maintaining good
position, your feet

01:11:27.030 --> 01:11:30.090
flat on the floor, your
three points of contact,

01:11:30.090 --> 01:11:33.053
your whole flat foot, and you're
not compromising another joint.

01:11:33.053 --> 01:11:34.470
So that's all
you're going to look

01:11:34.470 --> 01:11:39.270
for are those four things,
symmetry, stability, awareness,

01:11:39.270 --> 01:11:42.780
and range of motion through each
joint, through each movement.

01:11:42.780 --> 01:11:46.460
It sounds difficult
and time-consuming.

01:11:46.460 --> 01:11:47.210
It's really not.

01:11:47.210 --> 01:11:49.490
You can generally clear
these things in one

01:11:49.490 --> 01:11:51.247
or two repetitions in
a couple of seconds.

01:11:51.247 --> 01:11:53.330
And what you're really
going to look for-- there's

01:11:53.330 --> 01:11:57.058
lots of scoring schemes you can
test, that physical therapists

01:11:57.058 --> 01:11:57.725
will sort of do.

01:11:57.725 --> 01:12:01.610
I just look for
absolutely terrible,

01:12:01.610 --> 01:12:05.513
can't do it at all, minor
flaw, or pretty close to good.

01:12:05.513 --> 01:12:06.930
That's really all
I'm looking for.

01:12:06.930 --> 01:12:08.690
So my scoring system
is zero, one, three.

01:12:08.690 --> 01:12:09.560
Zero is like, you're
not going to do

01:12:09.560 --> 01:12:12.350
this exercise because you're
at a very high acute risk.

01:12:12.350 --> 01:12:15.770
You might get hurt
on rep one tomorrow.

01:12:15.770 --> 01:12:17.770
Number one, a score of
one is like, there's

01:12:17.770 --> 01:12:18.783
a minor flaw here.

01:12:18.783 --> 01:12:20.200
We can probably
do it, but we need

01:12:20.200 --> 01:12:23.222
to be cautious of
load and volume.

01:12:23.222 --> 01:12:24.930
And the other one is,
maybe it's perfect,

01:12:24.930 --> 01:12:29.130
maybe it's not, but go ahead
and it on a reasonable protocol.

01:12:29.130 --> 01:12:30.280
You'll be fine.

01:12:30.280 --> 01:12:32.040
So that's generally
what you would

01:12:32.040 --> 01:12:35.340
need to do as a cost-free method
of identifying good movement

01:12:35.340 --> 01:12:38.603
technique within any of the
things that you would do.

01:12:38.603 --> 01:12:40.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
What about speed?

01:12:40.020 --> 01:12:41.100
ANDY GALPIN: I actually
don't think this

01:12:41.100 --> 01:12:42.408
is one most people should test.

01:12:42.408 --> 01:12:43.950
If you're a
high-performance athlete,

01:12:43.950 --> 01:12:47.820
we can run a 40-yard dash or
we can do some different things

01:12:47.820 --> 01:12:50.527
with a velocity
transducer on a barbell,

01:12:50.527 --> 01:12:52.110
if you're a weightlifter
or something.

01:12:52.110 --> 01:12:54.600
For most people,
pure speed is really

01:12:54.600 --> 01:12:57.870
maximum velocity
or acceleration are

01:12:57.870 --> 01:13:00.270
the two ways we break it down.

01:13:00.270 --> 01:13:02.220
It's generally not
that necessary to test.

01:13:02.220 --> 01:13:04.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What
about number three, power,

01:13:04.620 --> 01:13:07.752
which I believe before you
told me was speed times force.

01:13:07.752 --> 01:13:09.960
ANDY GALPIN: So the reason
why I don't worry too much

01:13:09.960 --> 01:13:11.960
about speed is because
you can infer a lot of it

01:13:11.960 --> 01:13:13.050
from a power test.

01:13:13.050 --> 01:13:16.740
And a power test is easier to
do as well as easier to train

01:13:16.740 --> 01:13:18.000
for for most people.

01:13:18.000 --> 01:13:21.540
So the cost-free version
here is a simple broad jump.

01:13:21.540 --> 01:13:25.230
So this is stand
with normal position,

01:13:25.230 --> 01:13:27.630
jump out as far in front
of you as you possibly can,

01:13:27.630 --> 01:13:29.760
and measure the distance
between where you started

01:13:29.760 --> 01:13:31.830
and the back of your
heel, where it lands.

01:13:31.830 --> 01:13:36.132
A super basic number to look
for there is your height.

01:13:36.132 --> 01:13:38.340
So you should be able to
broad jump how tall you are.

01:13:38.340 --> 01:13:42.030
If you're 5' 5," you should
hit 5' 5," 6' 5," et cetera.

01:13:42.030 --> 01:13:43.530
It's not perfect.

01:13:43.530 --> 01:13:47.040
That's going to ratchet down
a little bit, about 15%,

01:13:47.040 --> 01:13:48.000
for females.

01:13:48.000 --> 01:13:49.590
They just simply
don't have the power,

01:13:49.590 --> 01:13:50.673
in general, that men have.

01:13:50.673 --> 01:13:53.173
And so you're going to want to
bring that down a little bit,

01:13:53.173 --> 01:13:54.960
but it's a very crude number.

01:13:54.960 --> 01:13:58.500
If you were to look at a
high-performance NFL player,

01:13:58.500 --> 01:13:59.910
if they're six
feet tall, they're

01:13:59.910 --> 01:14:02.700
going to be jumping
nine to 10 to 11 feet.

01:14:02.700 --> 01:14:05.100
If you can jump
your body height--

01:14:05.100 --> 01:14:06.690
we're not looking
for optimization

01:14:06.690 --> 01:14:07.710
in this particular test.

01:14:07.710 --> 01:14:12.060
You are looking for red flags if
you can jump your body height,

01:14:12.060 --> 01:14:13.268
you're going to be just fine.

01:14:13.268 --> 01:14:15.352
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's
incredibly straightforward

01:14:15.352 --> 01:14:16.740
and yet, I have one question.

01:14:16.740 --> 01:14:17.130
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:14:17.130 --> 01:14:19.255
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm assuming
that I can squat down

01:14:19.255 --> 01:14:20.850
as low as I need
to before I jump,

01:14:20.850 --> 01:14:25.360
I can swing my arms from
back to front as harder,

01:14:25.360 --> 01:14:28.660
with as much momentum
as I can muster.

01:14:28.660 --> 01:14:30.160
And when I land,
you said I'm going

01:14:30.160 --> 01:14:32.680
to take the measure from
where the back of my heels.

01:14:32.680 --> 01:14:34.270
ANDY GALPIN: You want to measure
the distance you actually

01:14:34.270 --> 01:14:34.770
covered.

01:14:34.770 --> 01:14:37.360
So to clarify, there's
no running approach here.

01:14:37.360 --> 01:14:39.520
There's no steps into it.

01:14:39.520 --> 01:14:41.140
You're going to
stand at a still.

01:14:41.140 --> 01:14:43.568
Yeah, you can swing, bounce
as much as you'd like to do.

01:14:43.568 --> 01:14:44.860
You're going to projectile off.

01:14:44.860 --> 01:14:45.940
So you're going to
measure the distance

01:14:45.940 --> 01:14:47.860
from the tip of your
toe-- so basically, stand

01:14:47.860 --> 01:14:50.740
behind the line and
then the furthest

01:14:50.740 --> 01:14:52.790
point back where you land.

01:14:52.790 --> 01:14:54.910
So basically, the
worst possible score,

01:14:54.910 --> 01:14:57.130
not the best possible
because your feet

01:14:57.130 --> 01:14:58.510
won't land symmetrically.

01:14:58.510 --> 01:15:00.708
One's probably going to
be a little bit farther.

01:15:00.708 --> 01:15:03.250
Now, technically, if you fall
backwards and your hand touches

01:15:03.250 --> 01:15:05.980
the ground, we mark that
number, but in this case,

01:15:05.980 --> 01:15:09.880
just use the furthest point
back over your back heel,

01:15:09.880 --> 01:15:10.870
and go from there.

01:15:10.870 --> 01:15:12.470
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'll be
trying it tomorrow morning.

01:15:12.470 --> 01:15:14.038
ANDY GALPIN: Now,
if you have access

01:15:14.038 --> 01:15:16.330
to a little bit more technology
or you just really want

01:15:16.330 --> 01:15:19.840
to know a better number,
a classic vertical jump

01:15:19.840 --> 01:15:21.200
is a good starting place.

01:15:21.200 --> 01:15:23.710
So you can actually do this
in a simple, cost-free way.

01:15:23.710 --> 01:15:25.970
You can just measure
two of your hands.

01:15:25.970 --> 01:15:28.450
Put them together so that
both of your middle fingers

01:15:28.450 --> 01:15:29.050
are touching.

01:15:29.050 --> 01:15:31.612
Overlap them, and put them
directly over your head.

01:15:31.612 --> 01:15:33.820
And then you want to reach
up as high as you can get.

01:15:33.820 --> 01:15:35.693
And you mark that on the wall.

01:15:35.693 --> 01:15:37.610
My brother and I used
to do this all the time.

01:15:37.610 --> 01:15:41.537
We would take a highlighter,
the yellow ones, and color

01:15:41.537 --> 01:15:43.120
as much as we could
on our fingertips,

01:15:43.120 --> 01:15:45.130
touch the wall so that
the highlighter would

01:15:45.130 --> 01:15:46.060
stain the wall--

01:15:46.060 --> 01:15:48.580
if you actually go back to
my house from my childhood,

01:15:48.580 --> 01:15:50.692
you'll see these markers
all over our house.

01:15:50.692 --> 01:15:52.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm sure
your parents were thrilled.

01:15:52.900 --> 01:15:54.250
ANDY GALPIN: My dad didn't care.

01:15:54.250 --> 01:15:55.180
Single dad, he didn't care.

01:15:55.180 --> 01:15:56.350
He's just like,
whatever, you guys.

01:15:56.350 --> 01:15:57.220
Do whatever you want.

01:15:57.220 --> 01:15:58.210
So you want to measure that.

01:15:58.210 --> 01:15:59.585
And then, of
course, you're going

01:15:59.585 --> 01:16:02.560
to jump with those two hands
and touch as high as you can up.

01:16:02.560 --> 01:16:05.143
And you're going to measure the
distance between your standing

01:16:05.143 --> 01:16:07.940
reach and the actual height
that you jumped there.

01:16:07.940 --> 01:16:10.190
Now, the reason you're doing
it two-handed by the way,

01:16:10.190 --> 01:16:11.710
is because if you
do one-handed, you

01:16:11.710 --> 01:16:14.990
can actually reach pretty high
by offsetting your shoulders.

01:16:14.990 --> 01:16:16.720
And now you're getting
into differences

01:16:16.720 --> 01:16:19.570
of who has more shoulder
mobility, who has the ability

01:16:19.570 --> 01:16:21.730
to get up there.

01:16:21.730 --> 01:16:24.070
A two-handed standard
approach is there.

01:16:24.070 --> 01:16:26.320
Same thing, no
running approach here.

01:16:26.320 --> 01:16:27.010
You can dip.

01:16:27.010 --> 01:16:27.610
You can drive.

01:16:27.610 --> 01:16:28.630
You can do all those things.

01:16:28.630 --> 01:16:30.088
You can swing your
arms, but you're

01:16:30.088 --> 01:16:33.100
going to be a two-handed touch,
is a general way to do that.

01:16:33.100 --> 01:16:35.740
You want to look for
a number of something

01:16:35.740 --> 01:16:38.360
like 24 inches or higher.

01:16:38.360 --> 01:16:40.780
If you're past the
age of 50, that number

01:16:40.780 --> 01:16:43.000
can come down a little
bit, to closer to 20.

01:16:43.000 --> 01:16:45.340
And again, for females, it's
going to be ratcheted down

01:16:45.340 --> 01:16:47.980
about 15% everywhere you go.

01:16:47.980 --> 01:16:53.200
If you're a middle-aged female,
and you're jumping 20 inches,

01:16:53.200 --> 01:16:54.505
you're in a pretty good spot.

01:16:54.505 --> 01:16:56.380
You're going to be
looking really nice there.

01:16:56.380 --> 01:16:58.690
Now, if you can do
that on a force plate,

01:16:58.690 --> 01:16:59.900
that's even better.

01:16:59.900 --> 01:17:02.770
So these are,
basically, scales that

01:17:02.770 --> 01:17:06.010
will go out to multiple digits,
sometimes five to nine digits

01:17:06.010 --> 01:17:06.910
past zero.

01:17:06.910 --> 01:17:08.090
And you're going to
stand on these things,

01:17:08.090 --> 01:17:09.130
you're going to do
the exact same test.

01:17:09.130 --> 01:17:10.810
And these are very interesting
because they'll tell you

01:17:10.810 --> 01:17:12.643
not only how high
you jump, but they'll

01:17:12.643 --> 01:17:14.560
tell you how much force
you put in the ground.

01:17:14.560 --> 01:17:16.870
They can also tell you
how long it took you--

01:17:16.870 --> 01:17:19.000
and this is called your
rate of force development--

01:17:19.000 --> 01:17:22.000
as well as impulse and speed and
a bunch of other stuff, which

01:17:22.000 --> 01:17:25.150
are important to help you
understand where on the power

01:17:25.150 --> 01:17:27.520
spectrum you need to be.

01:17:27.520 --> 01:17:31.510
So you would do that in addition
to using some sort of velocity

01:17:31.510 --> 01:17:32.960
transducer on a barbell.

01:17:32.960 --> 01:17:35.530
So a very classic
thing to do would be,

01:17:35.530 --> 01:17:37.120
let's say, you're
going to do a squat.

01:17:37.120 --> 01:17:39.928
And you're going to put
this device on the barbell,

01:17:39.928 --> 01:17:41.470
and that's going to
measure the speed

01:17:41.470 --> 01:17:42.760
at which the barbell moves.

01:17:42.760 --> 01:17:45.820
And you're going to do that at
40% of your one repetition max,

01:17:45.820 --> 01:17:49.060
50%, 60%, 70%, 80%,
90%, up to 100%.

01:17:49.060 --> 01:17:50.560
And that allows you
to create what's

01:17:50.560 --> 01:17:52.670
called a force-velocity curve.

01:17:52.670 --> 01:17:54.550
And you can start to
see at what point,

01:17:54.550 --> 01:17:56.230
when you start
loading things heavy,

01:17:56.230 --> 01:17:58.660
do you start slowing
down too much.

01:17:58.660 --> 01:18:01.390
And that will tell you what
part of the force-velocity curve

01:18:01.390 --> 01:18:04.210
that you want to train in
to optimize your power.

01:18:04.210 --> 01:18:05.918
Why that's important,
a lot of people

01:18:05.918 --> 01:18:07.960
will do things like, when
I'm training for power,

01:18:07.960 --> 01:18:09.190
how heavy should I lift?

01:18:09.190 --> 01:18:11.050
Well, the general
answer people say

01:18:11.050 --> 01:18:13.300
is, 30% of your one-rep
max, but that's actually not

01:18:13.300 --> 01:18:14.170
true at all.

01:18:14.170 --> 01:18:16.330
What's most optimal
for power development--

01:18:16.330 --> 01:18:19.270
which we'll discuss
more much later--

01:18:19.270 --> 01:18:21.070
is depending on
where you're flawed

01:18:21.070 --> 01:18:22.340
in the force-velocity curve.

01:18:22.340 --> 01:18:25.012
So if you have access
to technology like that,

01:18:25.012 --> 01:18:27.220
that can give you a lot more
insight and information.

01:18:27.220 --> 01:18:31.000
If not, do the broad jump
test or the highlighter

01:18:31.000 --> 01:18:33.760
on your fingertips and jump
it up and touch the wall test.

01:18:33.760 --> 01:18:35.500
ANDREW HUBERMAN: At
Andy Galpin's house.

01:18:35.500 --> 01:18:37.417
ANDY GALPIN: Hey,
just come along.

01:18:37.417 --> 01:18:38.750
The walls are already messed up.

01:18:38.750 --> 01:18:41.300
Just go ahead and come up to
Washington, and we'll do it.

01:18:41.300 --> 01:18:43.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Fantastic.

01:18:43.230 --> 01:18:44.298
What about strength?

01:18:44.298 --> 01:18:45.090
ANDY GALPIN: Right.

01:18:45.090 --> 01:18:46.423
So strength is really important.

01:18:46.423 --> 01:18:48.360
You need to measure
this in multiple areas.

01:18:48.360 --> 01:18:50.500
And we'll start off
with grip strength.

01:18:50.500 --> 01:18:52.210
So you can do this in two ways.

01:18:52.210 --> 01:18:53.970
You can buy a hand
grip dynamometer.

01:18:53.970 --> 01:18:58.320
Now, these are anywhere
between $20 to $100, anywhere.

01:18:58.320 --> 01:19:00.180
These actually used
to be, when I was

01:19:00.180 --> 01:19:01.590
in school, hundreds of dollars.

01:19:01.590 --> 01:19:04.770
And now you can literally buy
them on any website for $25.

01:19:04.770 --> 01:19:06.570
So my honest recommendation
is technically,

01:19:06.570 --> 01:19:07.770
that's not cost-free.

01:19:07.770 --> 01:19:10.020
I know your whole thing about
the cost-free protocols,

01:19:10.020 --> 01:19:13.170
but $25, I'm calling
that basically cost-free.

01:19:13.170 --> 01:19:14.670
You can bring that
in and test that.

01:19:14.670 --> 01:19:16.620
And that's just a little device
where you're going to squeeze,

01:19:16.620 --> 01:19:17.190
and you're going to do it.

01:19:17.190 --> 01:19:19.530
And I would do your right
hand and your left hand.

01:19:19.530 --> 01:19:21.510
You want to look for
asymmetries there,

01:19:21.510 --> 01:19:23.490
but you want to look
for something like--

01:19:23.490 --> 01:19:27.490
typically, they're going to
give you a value in kilograms.

01:19:27.490 --> 01:19:30.810
And you want to look for
something like a minimum score

01:19:30.810 --> 01:19:32.430
here of 40 kilograms.

01:19:32.430 --> 01:19:36.485
Ideally, you're up past 60 would
be a really good spot to be in.

01:19:36.485 --> 01:19:38.610
You want to make sure that
there's no less than 10%

01:19:38.610 --> 01:19:40.410
variation between your
left and right hand.

01:19:40.410 --> 01:19:42.450
Your non-dominant hand
actually shouldn't be

01:19:42.450 --> 01:19:44.140
that much weaker in this test.

01:19:44.140 --> 01:19:45.973
So what you'll actually
see, a lot of times,

01:19:45.973 --> 01:19:48.810
is the non-dominant can
be oftentimes stronger

01:19:48.810 --> 01:19:51.660
because the dominant hand is
more for movement precision,

01:19:51.660 --> 01:19:52.990
writing, things like that.

01:19:52.990 --> 01:19:54.700
So you want them to be close.

01:19:54.700 --> 01:19:57.600
If you are a male and you are
under 40 kilograms on a hand

01:19:57.600 --> 01:20:01.690
grip dynamometer, we're
going to need to train that.

01:20:01.690 --> 01:20:03.670
If you're a female, it's
not that much lower,

01:20:03.670 --> 01:20:07.150
but about 35 kilograms
is the cutoff point.

01:20:07.150 --> 01:20:10.883
If you're above 55, we can
add it to your training,

01:20:10.883 --> 01:20:12.550
but I'm not worried
about leaving it out

01:20:12.550 --> 01:20:13.160
of your training.

01:20:13.160 --> 01:20:14.702
If you're a female,
if it's above 50,

01:20:14.702 --> 01:20:17.120
that's my cut off of
where we want to go.

01:20:17.120 --> 01:20:18.820
So that's a fairly cheap one.

01:20:18.820 --> 01:20:20.320
Another one that
you can actually do

01:20:20.320 --> 01:20:21.550
is just a dead hang.

01:20:21.550 --> 01:20:23.680
So you can hold on
to any bar, ideally,

01:20:23.680 --> 01:20:26.048
one that is thin
enough to where you can

01:20:26.048 --> 01:20:27.340
wrap your whole hand around it.

01:20:27.340 --> 01:20:30.750
So you don't want to be
using a giant fat grip.

01:20:30.750 --> 01:20:32.500
You're going to have
a false reading here.

01:20:32.500 --> 01:20:34.660
So something like
going to the gym

01:20:34.660 --> 01:20:38.582
and jumping on any pull-up
bar or pull-up rack.

01:20:38.582 --> 01:20:40.790
And you want to hang, and
this is a simple time test.

01:20:40.790 --> 01:20:43.510
So in general, we
should be able to hang

01:20:43.510 --> 01:20:47.530
for a minimum of 30 seconds
is what we're looking for.

01:20:47.530 --> 01:20:52.450
30 to 50 seconds is my
good, but we could probably

01:20:52.450 --> 01:20:53.410
get better here.

01:20:53.410 --> 01:20:55.210
If you're cruising
above 60 seconds,

01:20:55.210 --> 01:20:57.665
I'm generally pretty happy.

01:20:57.665 --> 01:20:59.290
This is actually a
good example of when

01:20:59.290 --> 01:21:02.010
females tend to be better.

01:21:02.010 --> 01:21:03.760
Grip strength on women
tends to be strong,

01:21:03.760 --> 01:21:05.840
and they can hang for
quite a long time.

01:21:05.840 --> 01:21:09.640
So those standards don't really
change that much for women.

01:21:09.640 --> 01:21:12.310
Now, if you are
exceptionally large,

01:21:12.310 --> 01:21:14.050
this thing doesn't
scale perfectly.

01:21:14.050 --> 01:21:17.200
If you're 240 pounds
and even if you're lean,

01:21:17.200 --> 01:21:21.240
it's just hard to hang
and hold 240 pounds.

01:21:21.240 --> 01:21:23.670
Conversely, if
you're 145 pounds,

01:21:23.670 --> 01:21:25.290
even if you're
unhealthy, you're going

01:21:25.290 --> 01:21:26.410
to be able to hang
for a long time.

01:21:26.410 --> 01:21:28.077
It's just not that
much weight to carry.

01:21:28.077 --> 01:21:30.482
So just rough numbers
to start off with.

01:21:30.482 --> 01:21:32.190
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
that's grip strength.

01:21:32.190 --> 01:21:34.200
What about strength
elsewhere in the body?

01:21:34.200 --> 01:21:36.540
ANDY GALPIN: The primary
ones, you can do an upper body

01:21:36.540 --> 01:21:39.150
strength test if you would like,
although it's not technically

01:21:39.150 --> 01:21:40.980
something we do very often.

01:21:40.980 --> 01:21:43.270
I'm happy to do it one at
max bench press or something

01:21:43.270 --> 01:21:43.770
like that.

01:21:43.770 --> 01:21:44.838
That's great.

01:21:44.838 --> 01:21:46.380
What I'm generally
more interested in

01:21:46.380 --> 01:21:47.910
is a leg extension test.

01:21:47.910 --> 01:21:51.270
And the reason I like this
is, a back squat is better.

01:21:51.270 --> 01:21:54.490
A barbell back squat
is-- look, that's my jam.

01:21:54.490 --> 01:21:55.560
That's my life.

01:21:55.560 --> 01:21:57.570
It's just very
technically demanding,

01:21:57.570 --> 01:21:59.130
and it's challenging.

01:21:59.130 --> 01:22:00.060
You need spotters.

01:22:00.060 --> 01:22:02.130
You need comfort.

01:22:02.130 --> 01:22:03.430
A lot goes into this.

01:22:03.430 --> 01:22:05.820
So for the average person,
a leg extension test

01:22:05.820 --> 01:22:07.033
is fairly standardized.

01:22:07.033 --> 01:22:08.700
You don't have to
worry about technique,

01:22:08.700 --> 01:22:11.050
and people can just
get into it and go.

01:22:11.050 --> 01:22:15.143
And so what you want to look for
there is a couple of standards

01:22:15.143 --> 01:22:15.810
you want to hit.

01:22:15.810 --> 01:22:19.050
Again, a very simple
answer here is body weight.

01:22:19.050 --> 01:22:22.077
Can you do a leg extension
with your body weight?

01:22:22.077 --> 01:22:23.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN: One repetition?

01:22:23.410 --> 01:22:24.490
ANDY GALPIN: One repetition.

01:22:24.490 --> 01:22:25.810
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I can
answer that right now.

01:22:25.810 --> 01:22:26.240
ANDY GALPIN: Can you?

01:22:26.240 --> 01:22:26.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No.

01:22:26.800 --> 01:22:27.340
ANDY GALPIN: You can't?

01:22:27.340 --> 01:22:28.173
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No.

01:22:28.173 --> 01:22:30.190
I can hack squat a
reasonable amount of weight,

01:22:30.190 --> 01:22:32.590
but I was on the leg
extension this morning,

01:22:32.590 --> 01:22:34.810
and it was a Nautilus machine.

01:22:34.810 --> 01:22:37.870
And I certainly could not
leg extension my body weight.

01:22:37.870 --> 01:22:39.160
ANDY GALPIN: Let me clarify.

01:22:39.160 --> 01:22:40.577
Were you doing a single leg?

01:22:40.577 --> 01:22:41.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No.

01:22:41.410 --> 01:22:42.620
ANDY GALPIN: So
bilateral, you can't leg

01:22:42.620 --> 01:22:43.330
extension your body weight?

01:22:43.330 --> 01:22:44.163
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No.

01:22:44.163 --> 01:22:46.593
But I certainly can hamstring
curl my body weight.

01:22:46.593 --> 01:22:47.260
ANDY GALPIN: OK.

01:22:47.260 --> 01:22:50.560
So we maybe have some
deficiencies in our quads

01:22:50.560 --> 01:22:52.295
that we need to go
after, but that's

01:22:52.295 --> 01:22:53.920
a pretty good number
you want to be at.

01:22:53.920 --> 01:22:57.760
If you go up in age past
age 40, every decade that

01:22:57.760 --> 01:23:00.040
can come down about
10%, and you'll still

01:23:00.040 --> 01:23:01.160
be in a pretty good slot.

01:23:01.160 --> 01:23:03.730
So if you're 50
years old and you're

01:23:03.730 --> 01:23:08.230
170 pounds, if you can do 160,
you're in a pretty good spot.

01:23:08.230 --> 01:23:10.810
And then you could just,
again, take it down about 10%

01:23:10.810 --> 01:23:14.230
every decade after
40, but prior to 40,

01:23:14.230 --> 01:23:16.270
there's really no
change in strength,

01:23:16.270 --> 01:23:19.120
but certainly, somebody
in their 40s to 50s

01:23:19.120 --> 01:23:21.820
should be able to leg
extension their body weight.

01:23:21.820 --> 01:23:23.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Noted.

01:23:23.680 --> 01:23:25.630
I look forward to
our discussion a bit

01:23:25.630 --> 01:23:27.700
later, talking about
how to build strength.

01:23:27.700 --> 01:23:28.450
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:23:28.450 --> 01:23:29.980
Any of these
strength tests, they

01:23:29.980 --> 01:23:32.890
don't have to be done to a
technical true one-rep max.

01:23:32.890 --> 01:23:36.350
You can use what are called
repetition conversion equation.

01:23:36.350 --> 01:23:38.770
So put on a load
that you think is

01:23:38.770 --> 01:23:41.140
kind of close to your
maximum and just do it

01:23:41.140 --> 01:23:42.490
for as many reps as you can.

01:23:42.490 --> 01:23:44.830
As long as it's under
five reps total,

01:23:44.830 --> 01:23:47.110
you can then actually
go online and enter that

01:23:47.110 --> 01:23:50.060
into any number of calculators
anywhere, and it will tell you,

01:23:50.060 --> 01:23:53.140
OK, you did three
repetitions at 200 pounds.

01:23:53.140 --> 01:23:56.772
Your one-rep max is
probably 215, whatever.

01:23:56.772 --> 01:23:57.980
So there's estimate equation.

01:23:57.980 --> 01:23:59.680
So if you don't want
to spend the time

01:23:59.680 --> 01:24:01.600
or you're not truly
comfortable absolutely

01:24:01.600 --> 01:24:04.060
going to your true
one-rep max, just

01:24:04.060 --> 01:24:07.060
get to a number that's fairly
close and do as many as you can

01:24:07.060 --> 01:24:10.330
and then go on line, again,
one-rep max estimator equations

01:24:10.330 --> 01:24:11.980
are everywhere.

01:24:11.980 --> 01:24:14.740
If you get past five
repetitions or so,

01:24:14.740 --> 01:24:18.110
the accuracy of those
equations starts going down.

01:24:18.110 --> 01:24:21.187
So don't put on something and
go, oh, I did 12 reps of it

01:24:21.187 --> 01:24:23.020
and then try to figure
out your one-rep max.

01:24:23.020 --> 01:24:23.687
It'll get close.

01:24:23.687 --> 01:24:25.895
You start moving past that,
you're just getting worse

01:24:25.895 --> 01:24:27.110
and worse and worse accuracy.

01:24:27.110 --> 01:24:29.652
So I want to make sure whether
you're doing the leg extension

01:24:29.652 --> 01:24:32.230
test or a front squat
test, you don't technically

01:24:32.230 --> 01:24:35.110
have to do it in
absolute one-rep max.

01:24:35.110 --> 01:24:36.980
If neither of those
are an option,

01:24:36.980 --> 01:24:40.960
another one I like a lot
here is simply a front squat

01:24:40.960 --> 01:24:42.730
or a goblet squat hold.

01:24:42.730 --> 01:24:45.220
So you're going to hold a
weight in front of your chest.

01:24:45.220 --> 01:24:46.390
A kettlebell is great here.

01:24:46.390 --> 01:24:47.620
A dumbbell is fine here.

01:24:47.620 --> 01:24:50.957
And you want to hold about
half of your body weight,

01:24:50.957 --> 01:24:52.540
go all the way to
the bottom position,

01:24:52.540 --> 01:24:55.710
and try to hold that
for about 45 seconds.

01:24:55.710 --> 01:25:00.052
So it's a pretty good indicator
of number one, your position.

01:25:00.052 --> 01:25:02.010
It's hard to be in a bad
position for that long

01:25:02.010 --> 01:25:04.660
at that load as well as
core strength and low back

01:25:04.660 --> 01:25:05.160
stability.

01:25:05.160 --> 01:25:07.530
So it's a very
different indicator

01:25:07.530 --> 01:25:09.180
than, say, the leg
extension test,

01:25:09.180 --> 01:25:11.130
but it's a really nice one.

01:25:11.130 --> 01:25:13.170
It doesn't require
many moving parts.

01:25:13.170 --> 01:25:15.000
It's more difficult
than the leg extension,

01:25:15.000 --> 01:25:16.692
but it's quite a
bit more functional.

01:25:16.692 --> 01:25:18.150
And it's going to
give you insights

01:25:18.150 --> 01:25:20.790
into a lot more areas
than just the quadriceps.

01:25:20.790 --> 01:25:23.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So 45 seconds
down at the bottom of the squat

01:25:23.450 --> 01:25:25.200
and then returning to
a standing position.

01:25:25.200 --> 01:25:26.220
ANDY GALPIN: Yep.

01:25:26.220 --> 01:25:28.983
And if you can't do the
return, actually, I'm

01:25:28.983 --> 01:25:31.650
not that worried, but as long as
you can hold that good position

01:25:31.650 --> 01:25:34.440
without a technical breakdown
in that 45 seconds, that's

01:25:34.440 --> 01:25:36.270
a really good spot.

01:25:36.270 --> 01:25:38.610
As an intro, I want
a third of your body

01:25:38.610 --> 01:25:40.020
weight for 30 seconds.

01:25:40.020 --> 01:25:41.103
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Terrific.

01:25:41.103 --> 01:25:45.230
I plan to attempt all of those
strength tests very soon.

01:25:45.230 --> 01:25:46.670
What about hypertrophy?

01:25:46.670 --> 01:25:47.660
ANDY GALPIN: Sure.

01:25:47.660 --> 01:25:48.780
Actually, before
we get into that,

01:25:48.780 --> 01:25:50.060
I want to jump back
really quickly.

01:25:50.060 --> 01:25:51.890
It's important to add
a couple of caveats

01:25:51.890 --> 01:25:53.182
to the strength training stuff.

01:25:53.182 --> 01:25:54.800
So there's two
that I want to do.

01:25:54.800 --> 01:25:59.810
Number one, these are assuming
you are technically proficient.

01:25:59.810 --> 01:26:03.230
So I don't want you to do
any exercise to exhaustion

01:26:03.230 --> 01:26:05.407
or to maximum
strength if you're not

01:26:05.407 --> 01:26:06.740
comfortable with your technique.

01:26:06.740 --> 01:26:08.382
So adjust these accordingly.

01:26:08.382 --> 01:26:10.340
If you're not comfortable
with the front squat,

01:26:10.340 --> 01:26:11.150
do the leg extension.

01:26:11.150 --> 01:26:13.567
If you're uncomfortable with
that, do something different.

01:26:13.567 --> 01:26:16.960
So we never want to
utilize maximum testing

01:26:16.960 --> 01:26:18.850
if it's going to come
with a consequence

01:26:18.850 --> 01:26:20.270
of serious acute injury.

01:26:20.270 --> 01:26:22.930
So that's the most
important flag.

01:26:22.930 --> 01:26:26.050
The second one is,
your warm-up protocol

01:26:26.050 --> 01:26:29.950
will have a huge effect
on your actual results.

01:26:29.950 --> 01:26:31.518
And so whenever
you do these tests,

01:26:31.518 --> 01:26:34.060
especially if you're going to
do a test and then a test again

01:26:34.060 --> 01:26:37.000
down the line, you want to make
sure that warm-up protocol is

01:26:37.000 --> 01:26:38.020
standardized.

01:26:38.020 --> 01:26:39.280
Now, again, the NFCA--

01:26:39.280 --> 01:26:43.450
and I can give you resources--
has specific guides for exactly

01:26:43.450 --> 01:26:44.950
what to do for your
warm-up protocol

01:26:44.950 --> 01:26:46.480
prior to one-rep max testing.

01:26:46.480 --> 01:26:48.648
So we can go there, and
you can look that stuff up.

01:26:48.648 --> 01:26:50.440
We can add that to show
notes or something.

01:26:50.440 --> 01:26:51.357
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:26:51.357 --> 01:26:52.930
And I think when we
get into a deeper

01:26:52.930 --> 01:26:54.790
discussion about
strength and hypertrophy

01:26:54.790 --> 01:26:56.440
and resistance
training in general,

01:26:56.440 --> 01:26:58.690
maybe we could touch into
the best warm-up protocol.

01:26:58.690 --> 01:27:01.030
I know I have mine,
and I'm certain it's

01:27:01.030 --> 01:27:03.430
going to be suboptimal
based on everything--

01:27:03.430 --> 01:27:04.030
ANDY GALPIN: Maybe that's
causing the problems.

01:27:04.030 --> 01:27:05.080
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --based
on every conversation

01:27:05.080 --> 01:27:06.730
we've ever had, where I
learned all the things

01:27:06.730 --> 01:27:07.480
I'm doing incorrectly.

01:27:07.480 --> 01:27:09.700
But I do make changes on the
basis of what you tell me.

01:27:09.700 --> 01:27:11.408
ANDY GALPIN: It's not
incorrectly so much

01:27:11.408 --> 01:27:12.725
as it is suboptimal.

01:27:12.725 --> 01:27:14.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's a very kind way

01:27:14.350 --> 01:27:15.558
of telling me it's incorrect.

01:27:15.558 --> 01:27:16.810
Thank you.

01:27:16.810 --> 01:27:18.303
What about hypertrophy?

01:27:18.303 --> 01:27:19.720
ANDY GALPIN: So
the thing you want

01:27:19.720 --> 01:27:24.860
to pay attention to here is,
you have the aesthetic portion

01:27:24.860 --> 01:27:25.490
of hypertrophy.

01:27:25.490 --> 01:27:26.910
That's entirely up to you.

01:27:26.910 --> 01:27:28.040
There is no rationale.

01:27:28.040 --> 01:27:30.080
You can decide what you feel
like looks good or doesn't

01:27:30.080 --> 01:27:30.580
look good.

01:27:30.580 --> 01:27:31.710
That's irrelevant.

01:27:31.710 --> 01:27:34.730
There is a sufficient
amount you need to have,

01:27:34.730 --> 01:27:37.160
where below that, it's
detrimental to your health,

01:27:37.160 --> 01:27:38.670
regardless of your outcomes.

01:27:38.670 --> 01:27:41.510
And so the best way to do
this is a couple of ways.

01:27:41.510 --> 01:27:44.760
Any sort of body composition
tests can do this.

01:27:44.760 --> 01:27:48.500
So whether this is a scan
through like a DEXA scan,

01:27:48.500 --> 01:27:51.680
which is a gold
standard or other ways

01:27:51.680 --> 01:27:54.662
of biological, bioelectrical
impedance or otherwise.

01:27:54.662 --> 01:27:56.120
So there's a ton
of different tests

01:27:56.120 --> 01:27:57.960
you can get that
are pretty close.

01:27:57.960 --> 01:27:59.930
So what you want
to pay attention

01:27:59.930 --> 01:28:05.200
to when you get a DEXA scan
is a number called FFFMI.

01:28:05.200 --> 01:28:07.340
And so that stands for
"fat-free mass index."

01:28:07.340 --> 01:28:10.220
So you can look at again, any
number of online calculators.

01:28:10.220 --> 01:28:11.620
These are all standard,
so it doesn't actually

01:28:11.620 --> 01:28:12.560
matter where you pull them up.

01:28:12.560 --> 01:28:14.352
You don't have to worry
about looking it up

01:28:14.352 --> 01:28:16.730
and whether or not it's
right or not or something.

01:28:16.730 --> 01:28:18.910
And so that's going
to actually tell you

01:28:18.910 --> 01:28:20.560
if you have sufficient
muscle mass.

01:28:20.560 --> 01:28:23.770
And so a number you want to look
for, in general, is something

01:28:23.770 --> 01:28:25.600
like if you're a
man, your FFMI should

01:28:25.600 --> 01:28:28.610
be something like 20 or higher.

01:28:28.610 --> 01:28:31.390
If you're a woman, you want
to look for something like 18.

01:28:31.390 --> 01:28:32.710
So those are the targets.

01:28:32.710 --> 01:28:36.220
If you get past
24, 25 for a man,

01:28:36.220 --> 01:28:38.410
that's a lot of muscle
mass, assuming you're

01:28:38.410 --> 01:28:39.250
reasonably lean.

01:28:39.250 --> 01:28:44.680
Now, if your FFMI is like 24,
25 but your body fat is 40%,

01:28:44.680 --> 01:28:48.400
you're actually just a
very, very large individual.

01:28:48.400 --> 01:28:49.640
You're not in a great spot.

01:28:49.640 --> 01:28:51.340
So when we say these
sort of numbers,

01:28:51.340 --> 01:28:53.007
it's the assumption
that you're probably

01:28:53.007 --> 01:28:57.590
sub 30% body fat for a man
and sub 35% for a woman.

01:28:57.590 --> 01:28:59.230
So those are the numbers.

01:28:59.230 --> 01:29:00.610
There are online calculators.

01:29:00.610 --> 01:29:03.310
All you really need to know
is your total body weight,

01:29:03.310 --> 01:29:05.777
your body fat percentage,
and then your height.

01:29:05.777 --> 01:29:07.360
And you can enter
those three numbers,

01:29:07.360 --> 01:29:09.280
and then they'll tell
you your FFMI score,

01:29:09.280 --> 01:29:11.080
and it'll correct for
an adjusted value.

01:29:11.080 --> 01:29:12.580
And then most of
those will actually

01:29:12.580 --> 01:29:14.413
tell you the grading
rubric and then they'll

01:29:14.413 --> 01:29:16.000
say, good, average,
bad, et cetera,

01:29:16.000 --> 01:29:18.130
but those are the
numbers we look at.

01:29:18.130 --> 01:29:23.950
If you are, as a man, sub
17, as a woman, sub 15,

01:29:23.950 --> 01:29:28.060
now we're in an area of pretty
severe physiological detriment

01:29:28.060 --> 01:29:29.590
for insufficient muscle.

01:29:29.590 --> 01:29:31.243
And in some of our
later discussions,

01:29:31.243 --> 01:29:32.660
we'll talk about
why that matters.

01:29:32.660 --> 01:29:35.290
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So that's
not sub 17% body fat.

01:29:35.290 --> 01:29:37.173
That says
specifically, the FFMI.

01:29:37.173 --> 01:29:38.590
ANDY GALPIN: That's
correct, yeah.

01:29:38.590 --> 01:29:40.660
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What
about muscular endurance?

01:29:40.660 --> 01:29:43.550
Is this where you're going to
tell me I need to do wall sits?

01:29:43.550 --> 01:29:45.050
ANDY GALPIN: So
this is really nice.

01:29:45.050 --> 01:29:47.350
You can do any
number of tests here.

01:29:47.350 --> 01:29:52.710
A standard plank it is a good
test of muscle endurance.

01:29:52.710 --> 01:29:55.930
So can you hold a front
plank for 60 seconds?

01:29:55.930 --> 01:29:59.130
Can you hold a side
plank for 45 seconds?

01:29:59.130 --> 01:30:00.150
Pretty easy.

01:30:00.150 --> 01:30:02.103
If you're able to do a push-up.

01:30:02.103 --> 01:30:04.020
So if you can't, that
sort of tells you alone.

01:30:04.020 --> 01:30:05.103
It's actually interesting.

01:30:05.103 --> 01:30:06.480
If you can't do
a single push-up,

01:30:06.480 --> 01:30:08.250
that's not a muscular
endurance issue.

01:30:08.250 --> 01:30:09.750
That's actually now
a strength issue

01:30:09.750 --> 01:30:12.250
because that's a
one-rep max problem.

01:30:12.250 --> 01:30:13.770
So we want to be able to--

01:30:13.770 --> 01:30:17.160
again, for a general male, we
should have no problem doing

01:30:17.160 --> 01:30:19.227
25 plus consecutive push-ups.

01:30:19.227 --> 01:30:21.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I apologize
for interrupting you,

01:30:21.310 --> 01:30:23.290
but as long as we're
talking about push-ups,

01:30:23.290 --> 01:30:25.100
could you just mention form?

01:30:25.100 --> 01:30:28.070
Are we talking chest
touching the ground?

01:30:28.070 --> 01:30:29.390
Elbows breaking right angles?

01:30:29.390 --> 01:30:32.845
What is a proper push-up,
according to your laboratory?

01:30:32.845 --> 01:30:34.970
ANDY GALPIN: Unless you
have a very specific reason

01:30:34.970 --> 01:30:37.730
to limit range of motion,
I want all my testing

01:30:37.730 --> 01:30:40.620
done through a full
joint range of motion.

01:30:40.620 --> 01:30:42.420
This is different
for the person.

01:30:42.420 --> 01:30:45.200
So it's individualized to them,
but in general, for a push-up,

01:30:45.200 --> 01:30:47.360
this would be a full
complete lockout

01:30:47.360 --> 01:30:50.210
of the elbows on the top
and a full chest touch

01:30:50.210 --> 01:30:52.610
or close to it at the ground.

01:30:52.610 --> 01:30:53.683
You can do it different.

01:30:53.683 --> 01:30:55.100
It doesn't really
matter, but just

01:30:55.100 --> 01:30:57.267
keep it standard from your
pretest to your post-test

01:30:57.267 --> 01:30:59.090
if you're trying
to mark progress,

01:30:59.090 --> 01:31:01.280
but for us, unless we have
a very specific reason,

01:31:01.280 --> 01:31:04.000
we're going full range of
motion for all of these tests.

01:31:04.000 --> 01:31:04.833
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

01:31:04.833 --> 01:31:06.360
So 25 push-ups for a male.

01:31:06.360 --> 01:31:08.720
ANDY GALPIN: 25 push-ups
for a male is the standard.

01:31:08.720 --> 01:31:10.820
And even something
like 10 is a number

01:31:10.820 --> 01:31:14.360
we're looking for, again,
as minimum categories

01:31:14.360 --> 01:31:16.310
for an upper body
muscular endurance.

01:31:16.310 --> 01:31:18.560
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And not to
get too down in the weeds,

01:31:18.560 --> 01:31:20.960
but I have observed
other people-- of course,

01:31:20.960 --> 01:31:21.680
never myself.

01:31:21.680 --> 01:31:23.570
No, I'm kidding-- but
observed other people

01:31:23.570 --> 01:31:26.960
pausing maybe at repetition
15, catching their breath

01:31:26.960 --> 01:31:28.740
and then continuing.

01:31:28.740 --> 01:31:30.365
ANDY GALPIN: That
would be a fail test.

01:31:30.365 --> 01:31:31.370
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
continue like a piston.

01:31:31.370 --> 01:31:32.090
ANDY GALPIN: That
would be a fail test.

01:31:32.090 --> 01:31:33.140
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So no pauses.

01:31:33.140 --> 01:31:33.650
ANDY GALPIN: Correct.

01:31:33.650 --> 01:31:35.275
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Just
up down, up down,

01:31:35.275 --> 01:31:38.205
and trying to hit at
least 10, but ideally 25.

01:31:38.205 --> 01:31:40.580
ANDY GALPIN: I learned this
lesson in one of our studies,

01:31:40.580 --> 01:31:44.250
probably nine years ago,
where we didn't clarify that.

01:31:44.250 --> 01:31:46.637
And so we actually
had an individual--

01:31:46.637 --> 01:31:47.720
he wasn't being nefarious.

01:31:47.720 --> 01:31:51.140
He just figured out, if I do
a couple, take a quick break

01:31:51.140 --> 01:31:54.530
and do a couple, he quadrupled
his post-test results

01:31:54.530 --> 01:31:57.260
from his pre-test result
because he figured out

01:31:57.260 --> 01:31:59.220
that little hack.

01:31:59.220 --> 01:32:00.750
So you want to standardize it.

01:32:00.750 --> 01:32:02.685
It's not that I'm
against or have

01:32:02.685 --> 01:32:03.810
some sort of strong belief.

01:32:03.810 --> 01:32:06.360
It's just trying to keep
protocol standardized, which

01:32:06.360 --> 01:32:08.430
means any break, failed test.

01:32:08.430 --> 01:32:12.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So 10 to 25
push-ups, minimum, for males.

01:32:12.210 --> 01:32:13.310
What about for females?

01:32:13.310 --> 01:32:14.518
ANDY GALPIN: So I'll clarify.

01:32:14.518 --> 01:32:17.010
If it's sub 10 for
a man, that's again,

01:32:17.010 --> 01:32:19.860
and you're like, very
severe red flag problem.

01:32:19.860 --> 01:32:22.522
We really, really like
to see a number above 25.

01:32:22.522 --> 01:32:23.730
That's where we're anchoring.

01:32:23.730 --> 01:32:29.312
Anything between 10 and 25 is
like, yeah, but not severe.

01:32:29.312 --> 01:32:31.270
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It means
they have work to do.

01:32:31.270 --> 01:32:32.160
ANDY GALPIN: We have work to do.

01:32:32.160 --> 01:32:33.870
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And for females?

01:32:33.870 --> 01:32:34.670
ANDY GALPIN: For
a female, you're

01:32:34.670 --> 01:32:35.712
going to scale that back.

01:32:35.712 --> 01:32:38.550
So a female, the answer
could be as little as zero.

01:32:38.550 --> 01:32:41.160
So you're going to see that,
can you do a full position?

01:32:41.160 --> 01:32:42.300
If they're in that
position, we're

01:32:42.300 --> 01:32:44.425
generally not going to do
a muscular endurance test

01:32:44.425 --> 01:32:45.570
from the knees.

01:32:45.570 --> 01:32:47.687
We already know the
answer is you're zero.

01:32:47.687 --> 01:32:49.770
We'll actually default to
another test, which I'll

01:32:49.770 --> 01:32:51.640
talk about in a second here.

01:32:51.640 --> 01:32:54.510
So for those folks, that's going
to scale down a little bit.

01:32:54.510 --> 01:32:57.480
So basically, you're looking
at 15 is that marker, like 25

01:32:57.480 --> 01:33:00.260
was for the male, where
I want to see above 15.

01:33:00.260 --> 01:33:01.260
And if I do, we're good.

01:33:01.260 --> 01:33:06.150
Anything between five to
15 is the number of, OK.

01:33:06.150 --> 01:33:10.500
If you're sub five, we
generally have some problems.

01:33:10.500 --> 01:33:13.560
And if that is different,
between one and zero,

01:33:13.560 --> 01:33:16.557
then zero is a problem.

01:33:16.557 --> 01:33:17.890
So we should be able to do that.

01:33:17.890 --> 01:33:21.750
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So if a female
cannot do 10 full push-ups.

01:33:21.750 --> 01:33:22.590
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:33:22.590 --> 01:33:25.597
10 full push-ups hard for a
female, depending on size.

01:33:25.597 --> 01:33:26.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

01:33:26.430 --> 01:33:29.140
Let's say a female can't do
five full push-ups. You said,

01:33:29.140 --> 01:33:31.980
rather than go to a
knees-down version,

01:33:31.980 --> 01:33:35.400
what would you do to assess
their muscular endurance?

01:33:35.400 --> 01:33:38.760
And would you then
also encourage

01:33:38.760 --> 01:33:40.080
them to work on their strength?

01:33:40.080 --> 01:33:42.030
ANDY GALPIN: Well, absolutely.

01:33:42.030 --> 01:33:43.770
So again, if they
can't do-- they

01:33:43.770 --> 01:33:47.115
can do anything less than three,
you're going to be strength.

01:33:47.115 --> 01:33:49.740
In fact, if you want to look at
muscular endurance in general--

01:33:49.740 --> 01:33:51.325
so this is a bit
of an off topic,

01:33:51.325 --> 01:33:52.700
but I promise I'll
keep it short,

01:33:52.700 --> 01:33:54.980
and I'll come right back.

01:33:54.980 --> 01:33:58.790
When I was a doctoral
student, I had two lab mates.

01:33:58.790 --> 01:34:02.075
One of them was a runner,
a female, 120 pounds,

01:34:02.075 --> 01:34:03.200
something like that, small.

01:34:03.200 --> 01:34:05.117
The other one was a male,
and he was basically

01:34:05.117 --> 01:34:07.557
like a straight bro,
he lifts weights,

01:34:07.557 --> 01:34:09.140
doesn't do any other
sort of training.

01:34:09.140 --> 01:34:12.740
Does a very classic not training
program, but kind of training.

01:34:12.740 --> 01:34:15.353
And they were bantering
back and forth for a while.

01:34:15.353 --> 01:34:17.270
And basically she was
saying, you're so unfit.

01:34:17.270 --> 01:34:18.020
You can't run at all.

01:34:18.020 --> 01:34:18.980
And he's saying, you're so weak.

01:34:18.980 --> 01:34:20.010
You can't do a pull-up.

01:34:20.010 --> 01:34:21.660
And so they challenged each
other to a competition.

01:34:21.660 --> 01:34:23.420
They said, at the
end of the year,

01:34:23.420 --> 01:34:27.200
the girl is going
to do 26 pull-ups,

01:34:27.200 --> 01:34:30.290
and the guy had to run
a marathon, so 26 miles.

01:34:30.290 --> 01:34:31.470
So that was the thing.

01:34:31.470 --> 01:34:35.433
And then there was some sort of
consequence for whoever failed.

01:34:35.433 --> 01:34:37.600
So the guy quickly tried
to figure out, how the hell

01:34:37.600 --> 01:34:39.350
am I going to run 26
miles when I have not

01:34:39.350 --> 01:34:41.490
run a mile in many,
many, many years?

01:34:41.490 --> 01:34:43.720
So he just started running,
three miles, four miles,

01:34:43.720 --> 01:34:45.070
whatever.

01:34:45.070 --> 01:34:47.740
Well, of course, both of
them ran immediately to me.

01:34:47.740 --> 01:34:49.240
And then she was
like, how the hell?

01:34:49.240 --> 01:34:50.410
I can't do a pull-up.

01:34:50.410 --> 01:34:51.560
And I was like, great.

01:34:51.560 --> 01:34:54.312
And I gave her a very specific
maximal strength protocol.

01:34:54.312 --> 01:34:55.270
And she was like, whoa.

01:34:55.270 --> 01:34:57.910
I want to go to the
assisted pull-up machine

01:34:57.910 --> 01:35:00.490
and work on doing sets of
25 because I've got to get

01:35:00.490 --> 01:35:01.713
my muscular endurance up.

01:35:01.713 --> 01:35:03.880
And I tried to explain to
her, your muscle endurance

01:35:03.880 --> 01:35:06.070
is irrelevant if
you can't do one.

01:35:06.070 --> 01:35:07.450
It's never going to matter.

01:35:07.450 --> 01:35:10.510
She did the muscular endurance
protocol, the entire thing.

01:35:10.510 --> 01:35:11.388
Didn't listen to me.

01:35:11.388 --> 01:35:12.430
The end of the year came.

01:35:12.430 --> 01:35:15.580
She still produced
exactly zero pull-ups.

01:35:15.580 --> 01:35:19.690
So the point is, if you
look at muscular endurance,

01:35:19.690 --> 01:35:21.868
where is it strength
and where is it actually

01:35:21.868 --> 01:35:22.660
muscular endurance?

01:35:22.660 --> 01:35:26.880
The general number that you're
looking for is under 80%.

01:35:26.880 --> 01:35:29.130
That's going to tell you,
is this a muscular endurance

01:35:29.130 --> 01:35:31.047
problem or is it an
absolute strength problem?

01:35:31.047 --> 01:35:33.338
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Under 80%
of a one-repetition maximum?

01:35:33.338 --> 01:35:34.120
ANDY GALPIN: Yup.

01:35:34.120 --> 01:35:36.330
So what I mean by that is this.

01:35:36.330 --> 01:35:39.250
In fact, this actually
is in your question.

01:35:39.250 --> 01:35:41.020
The other way to assess
muscular endurance

01:35:41.020 --> 01:35:45.100
is take the exact strength
tests you did from the talk

01:35:45.100 --> 01:35:46.960
five minutes ago,
which one did you do?

01:35:46.960 --> 01:35:51.160
Load that to 75%, and then do
that for as many repetitions

01:35:51.160 --> 01:35:51.760
as you can.

01:35:51.760 --> 01:35:54.400
And that is a
tremendous barometer

01:35:54.400 --> 01:35:55.330
of muscular endurance.

01:35:55.330 --> 01:35:57.760
So if you were able to do 200
pounds in your leg extension

01:35:57.760 --> 01:36:00.625
test, put 75% on that and do
that as many reps as you can.

01:36:00.625 --> 01:36:03.160
You want to look for more
than eight repetitions.

01:36:03.160 --> 01:36:05.290
If you are below
eight repetitions,

01:36:05.290 --> 01:36:09.060
then we have a muscular
endurance problem.

01:36:09.060 --> 01:36:11.980
If it is higher than that,
if you've got 15 or 20,

01:36:11.980 --> 01:36:14.350
then we know we have
probably some problems

01:36:14.350 --> 01:36:17.240
in your peak strength
or the test itself.

01:36:17.240 --> 01:36:19.450
So that is a good--
eight to 12 number

01:36:19.450 --> 01:36:23.416
is where you want to be
looking at for there.

01:36:23.416 --> 01:36:26.222
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What
about anaerobic capacity?

01:36:26.222 --> 01:36:27.930
ANDY GALPIN: This
one's more challenging.

01:36:27.930 --> 01:36:30.180
You either have to
go to a laboratory

01:36:30.180 --> 01:36:32.220
and do something
like a Wingate Test.

01:36:32.220 --> 01:36:36.360
So this is a
30-second maximal test

01:36:36.360 --> 01:36:40.050
where you're going to see how
much work can you possibly do

01:36:40.050 --> 01:36:41.280
in that 30 seconds.

01:36:41.280 --> 01:36:43.500
If you don't have
access to laboratory,

01:36:43.500 --> 01:36:47.130
you can do this on
any protocol you want.

01:36:47.130 --> 01:36:48.270
This could be sprinting.

01:36:48.270 --> 01:36:50.490
This can be on an air bike.

01:36:50.490 --> 01:36:52.590
This could be on
a rower, anything

01:36:52.590 --> 01:36:55.140
like that, anything where
you can exert maximal effort

01:36:55.140 --> 01:36:57.360
and you don't have to worry
about technical problems.

01:36:57.360 --> 01:37:01.382
So I generally don't like to do
things like a kettlebell swing

01:37:01.382 --> 01:37:02.340
or something like that.

01:37:02.340 --> 01:37:03.540
There's just too
many other variables.

01:37:03.540 --> 01:37:05.790
You need to be able to go
as hard as you possibly can,

01:37:05.790 --> 01:37:09.660
knowing you're going to get to
a place of tremendous fatigue.

01:37:09.660 --> 01:37:12.908
Now, in the lab, we often use
what's called a Bosco Protocol.

01:37:12.908 --> 01:37:14.700
And you're going to
stand on a force plate,

01:37:14.700 --> 01:37:16.575
and you're going to do
as many vertical jumps

01:37:16.575 --> 01:37:19.800
as fast as you can, as high
as you can, for 60 seconds.

01:37:19.800 --> 01:37:23.550
And you are absolutely
destroyed by second 45.

01:37:23.550 --> 01:37:25.950
So we'll either use that
Wingate Protocol or that Bosco

01:37:25.950 --> 01:37:26.760
Protocol.

01:37:26.760 --> 01:37:31.110
If you want, though, again,
take any of those other places,

01:37:31.110 --> 01:37:33.552
30 seconds or so,
up to 45 seconds,

01:37:33.552 --> 01:37:35.760
up to a minute if you want,
it doesn't really matter.

01:37:35.760 --> 01:37:38.400
And you just mark down
the distance you cover.

01:37:38.400 --> 01:37:38.900
That's all.

01:37:38.900 --> 01:37:40.858
We don't really have
standards for these things

01:37:40.858 --> 01:37:42.360
because it's going
to be different.

01:37:42.360 --> 01:37:45.780
How far you can travel in
30 seconds on a treadmill

01:37:45.780 --> 01:37:49.920
is just going to be so different
than sprinting in the field

01:37:49.920 --> 01:37:52.080
or on the assault
bike or whatever.

01:37:52.080 --> 01:37:54.720
So what you really want
to worry about there is,

01:37:54.720 --> 01:37:56.580
can you complete it?

01:37:56.580 --> 01:37:59.710
And then how awful do
you feel afterwards?

01:37:59.710 --> 01:38:02.760
So what you really want
to think about here

01:38:02.760 --> 01:38:05.900
is not those
protocols, but this.

01:38:05.900 --> 01:38:07.940
You want to think
about can you get

01:38:07.940 --> 01:38:11.310
close to your predicted
maximum heart rate?

01:38:11.310 --> 01:38:16.050
So the number we throw
out is 220 minus your age.

01:38:16.050 --> 01:38:18.828
So if you're 50 years
old, 220 minus 50,

01:38:18.828 --> 01:38:20.870
you should be able to get
to a maximum heart rate

01:38:20.870 --> 01:38:22.830
of around 170 beats per minute.

01:38:22.830 --> 01:38:25.882
Now, that number is
extremely generic.

01:38:25.882 --> 01:38:27.590
If you don't get there,
that doesn't have

01:38:27.590 --> 01:38:28.965
any indication of your fitness.

01:38:28.965 --> 01:38:31.340
If you get higher, that doesn't
mean you're any more fit.

01:38:31.340 --> 01:38:32.790
It's just a rough number.

01:38:32.790 --> 01:38:34.610
So here's what I want you to do.

01:38:34.610 --> 01:38:38.150
In this case, your heart rate
recovery is the better metric.

01:38:38.150 --> 01:38:41.160
So I want you to get up
to a maximum heart rate

01:38:41.160 --> 01:38:43.410
and then test your
heart rate recovery.

01:38:43.410 --> 01:38:45.090
And what you should
be looking for there

01:38:45.090 --> 01:38:48.890
is about a half a beat
recovery per second.

01:38:48.890 --> 01:38:50.680
So you're going to
get up to a place

01:38:50.680 --> 01:38:54.610
where you reach absolute
terrible exhaustion,

01:38:54.610 --> 01:38:59.620
maximum fatigue, test your
heart rate, and then count.

01:38:59.620 --> 01:39:00.490
Have a timer going.

01:39:00.490 --> 01:39:03.370
Within 60 seconds, you
should have, again,

01:39:03.370 --> 01:39:04.708
that half a beat per second.

01:39:04.708 --> 01:39:06.250
You should have a
heart rate recovery

01:39:06.250 --> 01:39:07.450
of 30 beats per minute.

01:39:07.450 --> 01:39:11.197
Within the next minute,
so two-minute recovery,

01:39:11.197 --> 01:39:13.030
it should be, again,
half that, so 60 beats.

01:39:13.030 --> 01:39:14.560
Those are rough
numbers to go by.

01:39:14.560 --> 01:39:17.470
And your three-minute recovery
is, again, half of that again.

01:39:17.470 --> 01:39:19.840
So that is the closest way.

01:39:19.840 --> 01:39:21.940
If your heart rate recovery
is worse than that,

01:39:21.940 --> 01:39:24.610
then we know we have a problem
in your anaerobic capacity

01:39:24.610 --> 01:39:27.820
or your cardiovascular capacity.

01:39:27.820 --> 01:39:29.420
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love it.

01:39:29.420 --> 01:39:31.607
What about number eight,
maximal heart rate?

01:39:31.607 --> 01:39:33.190
Because what you
just described sounds

01:39:33.190 --> 01:39:34.400
a lot like maximal heart rate.

01:39:34.400 --> 01:39:35.942
ANDY GALPIN: So this
is your VO2 max.

01:39:35.942 --> 01:39:38.890
So the gold standard here is to
actually go into a laboratory

01:39:38.890 --> 01:39:40.240
and get this thing done.

01:39:40.240 --> 01:39:43.360
So we can actually run a VO2 max
test, where you put a mask on,

01:39:43.360 --> 01:39:45.520
collect all your gases,
and run you to there.

01:39:45.520 --> 01:39:49.000
And there is a very specific
protocol for completion

01:39:49.000 --> 01:39:50.470
of a true maximum test.

01:39:50.470 --> 01:39:52.750
And any scientist
will know that.

01:39:52.750 --> 01:39:54.400
If you don't have
access to that,

01:39:54.400 --> 01:39:55.780
you can do a couple of tests.

01:39:55.780 --> 01:39:59.740
One of them is called a
12-Minute Cooper's Test.

01:39:59.740 --> 01:40:00.850
So this is simply time.

01:40:00.850 --> 01:40:02.830
You're going to run for 12
minutes as far as you can,

01:40:02.830 --> 01:40:04.997
and you're going to record
the distance you covered.

01:40:04.997 --> 01:40:09.190
Again, you can go online to
any number of calculators,

01:40:09.190 --> 01:40:10.660
enter that distance in.

01:40:10.660 --> 01:40:14.023
And that will tell you your
proximal or estimated VO2 max.

01:40:14.023 --> 01:40:15.940
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
that's a 12-minute sprint.

01:40:15.940 --> 01:40:18.100
ANDY GALPIN: 12-minute
sprint, maximum distance

01:40:18.100 --> 01:40:19.840
you can cover in 12 minutes.

01:40:19.840 --> 01:40:21.370
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Keeping a
steady pace the whole time

01:40:21.370 --> 01:40:21.620
or going--

01:40:21.620 --> 01:40:23.210
ANDY GALPIN: Do
whatever you want.

01:40:23.210 --> 01:40:26.620
The goal is to get maximum
distance covered in 12 minutes.

01:40:26.620 --> 01:40:30.610
So that's anywhere between
a mile to two plus miles,

01:40:30.610 --> 01:40:32.590
depending on how fit
you are, but you just

01:40:32.590 --> 01:40:35.820
do that Cooper 12-Minute Test.

01:40:35.820 --> 01:40:36.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Got it.

01:40:36.820 --> 01:40:37.990
ANDY GALPIN: I told you.

01:40:37.990 --> 01:40:39.730
So if you remember,
aerobic capacity

01:40:39.730 --> 01:40:41.530
is eight to 12
minutes where you're

01:40:41.530 --> 01:40:44.260
going to see a real true
test of that VO2 max.

01:40:44.260 --> 01:40:48.820
You simply can't get that
in under a few minutes.

01:40:48.820 --> 01:40:55.220
So if you want, you can do a
little gentler version of that.

01:40:55.220 --> 01:40:57.790
So there are a number
of submaximal tests.

01:40:57.790 --> 01:41:00.610
In fact, there is a one-mile
walk test you can do.

01:41:00.610 --> 01:41:03.110
So again, all you're going
to do is-- in this case,

01:41:03.110 --> 01:41:07.060
you have to have of either a
stopwatch or, ideally, a heart

01:41:07.060 --> 01:41:07.720
rate monitor.

01:41:07.720 --> 01:41:10.510
And all you have to do is--
this is a walk for one mile

01:41:10.510 --> 01:41:11.480
submaximal test.

01:41:11.480 --> 01:41:13.900
So you're going to walk
a mile, record the time,

01:41:13.900 --> 01:41:17.020
record your heart rate at
the end, enter those in.

01:41:17.020 --> 01:41:19.760
And those will give you, again,
estimates of your VO2 max.

01:41:19.760 --> 01:41:24.160
So that's the like, oh my gosh,
I can't run for 12 minutes

01:41:24.160 --> 01:41:27.280
as hard as I possibly can
or I don't want to do it.

01:41:27.280 --> 01:41:30.160
We have a lot of these
in our executive program.

01:41:30.160 --> 01:41:31.670
It's like, my knee
hurts too bad.

01:41:31.670 --> 01:41:33.420
I've got back pain
when I run or whatever.

01:41:33.420 --> 01:41:34.390
Can I-- OK.

01:41:34.390 --> 01:41:34.930
And you do the walk test.

01:41:34.930 --> 01:41:36.930
And it's pretty accurate
if you do it correctly.

01:41:36.930 --> 01:41:38.980
So technically, all
you have to actually do

01:41:38.980 --> 01:41:42.018
is measure your heart rate on
you neck and count 60 seconds,

01:41:42.018 --> 01:41:43.060
but it's just easier to--

01:41:43.060 --> 01:41:45.190
with everyone's
watches and stuff now,

01:41:45.190 --> 01:41:48.580
just wear the heart rate
monitor, plug in those numbers.

01:41:48.580 --> 01:41:52.820
And again, those are all
standard calculations.

01:41:52.820 --> 01:41:54.858
So anywhere you find
those, you don't

01:41:54.858 --> 01:41:56.150
have to worry about the source.

01:41:56.150 --> 01:41:57.340
So you just enter your
stuff in, and they're

01:41:57.340 --> 01:41:59.380
going to be running
off the same equation.

01:41:59.380 --> 01:42:01.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I like the
idea of the 12-minute run.

01:42:01.700 --> 01:42:03.670
I'm going to give it a
shot, see what happens.

01:42:03.670 --> 01:42:06.640
ANDY GALPIN: For years, we did
a one-mile version of this,

01:42:06.640 --> 01:42:09.240
and there's just a lot more
science on the 12-Minute Cooper

01:42:09.240 --> 01:42:09.740
Test.

01:42:09.740 --> 01:42:10.540
So we did that.

01:42:10.540 --> 01:42:16.020
It's pretty good, and it is
not even remotely close to fun.

01:42:16.020 --> 01:42:18.340
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It sounds
like fun for other reasons.

01:42:18.340 --> 01:42:19.340
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah, yeah.

01:42:19.340 --> 01:42:20.532
Well, it is it.

01:42:20.532 --> 01:42:22.865
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Fun in the
sense that it reveals a lot.

01:42:22.865 --> 01:42:23.150
ANDY GALPIN: Yup.

01:42:23.150 --> 01:42:24.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
powerful, potent.

01:42:24.710 --> 01:42:25.502
ANDY GALPIN: Super.

01:42:25.502 --> 01:42:28.682
There's no hiding.

01:42:28.682 --> 01:42:30.390
You can hide with the
leg extension test.

01:42:30.390 --> 01:42:31.650
It doesn't hurt that bad.

01:42:31.650 --> 01:42:35.640
But you cannot feel anything
but the 12-minute "run as far

01:42:35.640 --> 01:42:36.390
as you can" test.

01:42:36.390 --> 01:42:38.348
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So these
are really, actually,

01:42:38.348 --> 01:42:40.423
psychiatric diagnostic tests--

01:42:40.423 --> 01:42:41.340
ANDY GALPIN: They are.

01:42:41.340 --> 01:42:41.835
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --of a sort.

01:42:41.835 --> 01:42:42.825
ANDY GALPIN: For sure.

01:42:42.825 --> 01:42:46.440
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Number nine,
long-duration, steady-state

01:42:46.440 --> 01:42:47.610
exercise.

01:42:47.610 --> 01:42:49.320
I think of this as a.k.a.

01:42:49.320 --> 01:42:52.012
endurance, but as you
mentioned before, there

01:42:52.012 --> 01:42:53.220
are other forms of endurance.

01:42:53.220 --> 01:42:55.542
So long-duration,
steady-state exercise.

01:42:55.542 --> 01:42:56.250
ANDY GALPIN: Yep.

01:42:56.250 --> 01:42:59.380
So you really want
to think about this

01:42:59.380 --> 01:43:02.290
as not a standard number.

01:43:02.290 --> 01:43:05.650
You should maintain
consistent work output

01:43:05.650 --> 01:43:08.680
for over 20 plus minutes.

01:43:08.680 --> 01:43:10.750
And this one I want you
to just pick something

01:43:10.750 --> 01:43:12.230
that it was in your lifestyle.

01:43:12.230 --> 01:43:15.310
So is there a loop around
your house that you can do?

01:43:15.310 --> 01:43:18.640
Is there some protocol that
you like to use before?

01:43:18.640 --> 01:43:20.740
And you're simply going
to test your ability.

01:43:20.740 --> 01:43:22.900
Can you maintain work
without stopping?

01:43:22.900 --> 01:43:24.190
That's all it needs to be.

01:43:24.190 --> 01:43:27.310
Now ideally, I personally
like to throw a little twist

01:43:27.310 --> 01:43:30.008
in here, which is, can you do
this with nasal breathing only.

01:43:30.008 --> 01:43:31.300
That's when I feel really good.

01:43:31.300 --> 01:43:32.675
If you can go 30
straight minutes

01:43:32.675 --> 01:43:34.300
without needing
to take a break--

01:43:34.300 --> 01:43:37.750
walking doesn't really cut it
unless you're very, very unfit,

01:43:37.750 --> 01:43:40.450
in which case, if walking
30 minutes without a break

01:43:40.450 --> 01:43:42.800
is a challenge, OK.

01:43:42.800 --> 01:43:43.300
There.

01:43:43.300 --> 01:43:47.320
But if you can, I want you
moving at a non-walking pace.

01:43:47.320 --> 01:43:50.080
I don't care what zone this
is, two, three, four, five.

01:43:50.080 --> 01:43:51.070
I don't care.

01:43:51.070 --> 01:43:54.520
Show me you can maintain
minimum of 20 minutes of work

01:43:54.520 --> 01:43:57.850
with no breaks, no intervals,
no downtime and, again,

01:43:57.850 --> 01:44:00.280
ideally breathing
through your nose only.

01:44:00.280 --> 01:44:03.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love this
list, but it worries me a bit.

01:44:03.610 --> 01:44:06.550
Not because any one of
these tests is necessarily

01:44:06.550 --> 01:44:09.580
that overwhelming,
but because I'm

01:44:09.580 --> 01:44:13.068
unclear about how to
arrange performance

01:44:13.068 --> 01:44:14.110
of these different tests.

01:44:14.110 --> 01:44:14.530
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:44:14.530 --> 01:44:16.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: For
instance, do I separate them

01:44:16.820 --> 01:44:20.350
so I'm doing one test, like
long-duration output on one day

01:44:20.350 --> 01:44:22.060
and I'm doing strength
on another day?

01:44:22.060 --> 01:44:24.607
Those seem pretty obvious
to me, but are there

01:44:24.607 --> 01:44:26.440
ones that one can combine
on different days?

01:44:26.440 --> 01:44:29.350
How much time should one give
oneself in between these tests?

01:44:29.350 --> 01:44:32.320
And how often should
one do an assessment?

01:44:32.320 --> 01:44:34.588
Just as we don't want
to necessarily evaluate

01:44:34.588 --> 01:44:36.880
body weight changes by getting
on the scale three times

01:44:36.880 --> 01:44:40.690
a day, maybe once a day
at the same time each day

01:44:40.690 --> 01:44:42.430
is more practical.

01:44:42.430 --> 01:44:45.402
How often should we be
assessing our fitness for each

01:44:45.402 --> 01:44:46.360
and every one of these?

01:44:46.360 --> 01:44:48.693
ANDY GALPIN: Well, the way
that I would say this is, you

01:44:48.693 --> 01:44:50.770
want to pick the one
that is the worst

01:44:50.770 --> 01:44:52.460
and do that more frequently.

01:44:52.460 --> 01:44:56.740
So if, for example, you do
the upper body strength test

01:44:56.740 --> 01:44:59.470
and you are fantastic, if
you can bench press double

01:44:59.470 --> 01:45:03.900
your body weight, I don't need
to test your bench very often,

01:45:03.900 --> 01:45:04.980
for the average person.

01:45:04.980 --> 01:45:07.500
If you're not a powerlifter,
maybe once a year or something.

01:45:07.500 --> 01:45:08.400
Maybe not even that.

01:45:08.400 --> 01:45:09.820
We just don't need to get there.

01:45:09.820 --> 01:45:15.360
However, if we then test your
VO2 max and in your 12 minutes,

01:45:15.360 --> 01:45:17.550
you cover a total
of a half a mile,

01:45:17.550 --> 01:45:20.130
then we might want to
test that every month.

01:45:20.130 --> 01:45:23.242
And so we're going to let our
priorities emphasize which one

01:45:23.242 --> 01:45:24.450
we're going to do more often.

01:45:24.450 --> 01:45:26.760
I would recommend doing this
full battery once a year.

01:45:26.760 --> 01:45:29.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Full battery,
meaning the entire list

01:45:29.010 --> 01:45:29.760
on one day.

01:45:29.760 --> 01:45:32.018
ANDY GALPIN: No, not on
one day, but within a week.

01:45:32.018 --> 01:45:33.060
So you could take a week.

01:45:33.060 --> 01:45:36.010
Now, you could do these,
technically, all in two days.

01:45:36.010 --> 01:45:38.160
Three-day split here
is probably best.

01:45:38.160 --> 01:45:40.410
So if you were to just say,
hey, this is testing week.

01:45:40.410 --> 01:45:43.290
I actually love this for
beginning of the year

01:45:43.290 --> 01:45:45.960
or whenever it is that
you change your training,

01:45:45.960 --> 01:45:48.425
but I think once a year,
just like once a year you

01:45:48.425 --> 01:45:49.800
should probably
go to a physician

01:45:49.800 --> 01:45:55.200
and get full bloodwork, a
full heart scan and everything

01:45:55.200 --> 01:45:56.653
like that.

01:45:56.653 --> 01:45:58.320
And then if maybe you
had a heart issue,

01:45:58.320 --> 01:46:00.510
they would come back in and test
you more frequently, whatever

01:46:00.510 --> 01:46:01.140
the case is.

01:46:01.140 --> 01:46:02.682
You should probably
run through this.

01:46:02.682 --> 01:46:04.320
And you're going to
be thinking, yeah,

01:46:04.320 --> 01:46:06.750
but I don't want to give up on
my exercise routine that week.

01:46:06.750 --> 01:46:09.125
Well, I promise you, you're
not going to finish this week

01:46:09.125 --> 01:46:11.640
and think, I didn't do
very much work this week.

01:46:11.640 --> 01:46:12.880
It's going to feel great.

01:46:12.880 --> 01:46:15.120
And then you're going to
have a very nice barometer

01:46:15.120 --> 01:46:17.820
of exactly where you need
to change and prioritize

01:46:17.820 --> 01:46:19.950
your training for the next
quarter or half a year

01:46:19.950 --> 01:46:21.270
or wherever you want to go.

01:46:21.270 --> 01:46:23.640
If you want to actually
do this every six months,

01:46:23.640 --> 01:46:25.770
we end up, actually, doing
this, quite honestly,

01:46:25.770 --> 01:46:29.543
more like every six
months as a general test.

01:46:29.543 --> 01:46:30.960
That's a really
good way to do it,

01:46:30.960 --> 01:46:33.015
but minimum, if you're
arguing with me,

01:46:33.015 --> 01:46:34.890
give me once a year that
you want to do this.

01:46:34.890 --> 01:46:37.380
So which order to do them in?

01:46:37.380 --> 01:46:40.050
The non-fatiguing tests,
you can do whenever.

01:46:40.050 --> 01:46:43.530
So this is the body composition
scan, the FFMI, the body

01:46:43.530 --> 01:46:44.310
fat composition.

01:46:44.310 --> 01:46:46.200
All this stuff can
be done whenever.

01:46:46.200 --> 01:46:48.150
I generally like
to do that, though,

01:46:48.150 --> 01:46:50.050
as your very first activity.

01:46:50.050 --> 01:46:54.007
The reason is we know that
acute exercise can heavily

01:46:54.007 --> 01:46:55.590
influence things
like body composition

01:46:55.590 --> 01:46:58.150
measurements because of
inflammation, water storage, et

01:46:58.150 --> 01:46:58.650
cetera.

01:46:58.650 --> 01:47:02.170
So it's easiest to just get
that off of a 48 hour rest.

01:47:02.170 --> 01:47:04.860
You want to make sure you don't
do any hard exercise the day

01:47:04.860 --> 01:47:07.650
before a body composition
test and probably 48 hours

01:47:07.650 --> 01:47:08.470
before that.

01:47:08.470 --> 01:47:10.800
So just start yourself
off with that.

01:47:10.800 --> 01:47:13.050
Your movement tests
can be the same thing.

01:47:13.050 --> 01:47:16.080
You don't want to try to do a
assessment of how well you're

01:47:16.080 --> 01:47:19.540
squatting if you're incredibly
sore from your brutal squatting

01:47:19.540 --> 01:47:20.040
test.

01:47:20.040 --> 01:47:22.290
So tend to do those things
when you're the most fresh.

01:47:22.290 --> 01:47:25.950
Then what you want to do is
any skill or maximum strength

01:47:25.950 --> 01:47:28.950
or power goes at the very
beginning of the day.

01:47:28.950 --> 01:47:31.510
And any fatiguing thing
happens at the end.

01:47:31.510 --> 01:47:33.600
And so you could easily do this.

01:47:33.600 --> 01:47:38.560
All right, I'm going to do
my power test, my broad jump,

01:47:38.560 --> 01:47:39.060
great.

01:47:39.060 --> 01:47:41.102
You're not going to be
fatigued at all from that.

01:47:41.102 --> 01:47:43.590
And on the same day, since
I'm already pretty warmed up,

01:47:43.590 --> 01:47:47.580
now I'm going to roll right
into my leg strength test.

01:47:47.580 --> 01:47:49.110
And since I'm really
warmed up, I'm

01:47:49.110 --> 01:47:52.120
going to do my leg muscular
endurance test right there.

01:47:52.120 --> 01:47:53.880
So this is a very
common strategy we use.

01:47:53.880 --> 01:47:56.905
We do our one-rep max leg
extension, five minutes,

01:47:56.905 --> 01:47:59.280
seven minutes, whatever we
need to do, come back, load it

01:47:59.280 --> 01:48:02.160
to 75%, do as many
reps as you can.

01:48:02.160 --> 01:48:02.760
Boom.

01:48:02.760 --> 01:48:04.860
You could roll right into,
then, your upper body

01:48:04.860 --> 01:48:06.870
test or your grip strength
test or anything else

01:48:06.870 --> 01:48:08.160
that you want to do there.

01:48:08.160 --> 01:48:11.210
Is there a little
bit of influence?

01:48:11.210 --> 01:48:15.320
Yeah, but really, for most
people, it's not that bad.

01:48:15.320 --> 01:48:16.430
What influence I mean.

01:48:16.430 --> 01:48:19.100
If you do a leg strength
test, coming back and doing

01:48:19.100 --> 01:48:21.320
an upper body strength
test afterwards,

01:48:21.320 --> 01:48:22.550
it's not that big a deal.

01:48:22.550 --> 01:48:25.370
Give yourself 15, give
yourself 20 minutes.

01:48:25.370 --> 01:48:26.720
Give it plenty of time.

01:48:26.720 --> 01:48:28.470
So you can knock out
your strength testing

01:48:28.470 --> 01:48:31.145
and muscular endurance
testing all in one day.

01:48:31.145 --> 01:48:35.120
You could do your performance,
your skill diagnostic,

01:48:35.120 --> 01:48:37.235
your power jump
test, your strength,

01:48:37.235 --> 01:48:39.860
and your muscular endurance, and
all that stuff is knocked out.

01:48:39.860 --> 01:48:42.140
You're going to have to
come back on a separate day

01:48:42.140 --> 01:48:44.090
and do your anaerobic tests.

01:48:44.090 --> 01:48:47.300
This is 30-seconds maximal
endurance, things like that.

01:48:47.300 --> 01:48:49.760
You could, though, if
you wanted, do that

01:48:49.760 --> 01:48:52.173
after your long duration tests.

01:48:52.173 --> 01:48:53.840
You're long duration
test again, is just

01:48:53.840 --> 01:48:56.648
going to function
as a big, warm up.

01:48:56.648 --> 01:48:58.190
Or you could flip
those things or you

01:48:58.190 --> 01:48:59.600
can do them on separate days.

01:48:59.600 --> 01:49:03.577
You're going to have to do your
VO2 max test on its own day,

01:49:03.577 --> 01:49:05.660
for the most part, unless
you wanted to do, again,

01:49:05.660 --> 01:49:08.300
your movement or
your body composition

01:49:08.300 --> 01:49:09.530
before those things.

01:49:09.530 --> 01:49:12.200
So you really have the
ability to mix and match.

01:49:12.200 --> 01:49:15.920
Ideally, this most realistically
probably takes three days.

01:49:15.920 --> 01:49:18.440
If you want to separate
them into four or five,

01:49:18.440 --> 01:49:20.270
the more separation
you do, the better data

01:49:20.270 --> 01:49:21.710
you're going to get.

01:49:21.710 --> 01:49:24.740
It's just a question of how
pedantic are you really trying

01:49:24.740 --> 01:49:25.250
to get here?

01:49:25.250 --> 01:49:29.690
And are you willing to lose
5% to then save a whole day?

01:49:29.690 --> 01:49:32.180
Then you can do these
things in multiple stacks.

01:49:32.180 --> 01:49:33.860
So that's how I
would break it up.

01:49:33.860 --> 01:49:35.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So what I'm hearing

01:49:35.360 --> 01:49:37.092
is, better to do it
than to not do it.

01:49:37.092 --> 01:49:38.300
ANDY GALPIN: Most definitely.

01:49:38.300 --> 01:49:40.300
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And be rational.

01:49:40.300 --> 01:49:42.800
Don't try and do
your strength output

01:49:42.800 --> 01:49:44.795
late in the day when
you're fatigued.

01:49:44.795 --> 01:49:47.510
If you're going to combine
some of the steady state

01:49:47.510 --> 01:49:50.570
endurance and maximal
heart rate, fine.

01:49:50.570 --> 01:49:53.417
Understand there might be a
slight deficit there, but test

01:49:53.417 --> 01:49:54.500
it the same way each time.

01:49:54.500 --> 01:49:56.390
And what you're really
looking for is improvement.

01:49:56.390 --> 01:49:56.750
ANDY GALPIN: Yep.

01:49:56.750 --> 01:49:58.683
And you can also do
the heart rate recovery

01:49:58.683 --> 01:49:59.850
under any of the modalities.

01:49:59.850 --> 01:50:02.390
So you could do the heart rate
recovery after your VO2 max

01:50:02.390 --> 01:50:03.330
as well.

01:50:03.330 --> 01:50:05.180
So you finish that
thing, and then

01:50:05.180 --> 01:50:08.368
just, again, do the same
test for up to three minutes.

01:50:08.368 --> 01:50:10.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN: These
are fantastic tools.

01:50:10.160 --> 01:50:12.860
I'm almost tempted to say that
I'm willing to post my numbers,

01:50:12.860 --> 01:50:15.950
but that actually violates the
core principle that I think

01:50:15.950 --> 01:50:18.770
we're getting at here,
which is that it's highly

01:50:18.770 --> 01:50:21.680
unlikely that anybody is
going to be phenomenal

01:50:21.680 --> 01:50:22.400
across the board.

01:50:22.400 --> 01:50:24.710
I mean, certainly there will
be individuals that are,

01:50:24.710 --> 01:50:26.870
but based on everything
we talked about earlier,

01:50:26.870 --> 01:50:30.830
specificity of training and
how extensively somebody has

01:50:30.830 --> 01:50:35.280
been training a certain way
will, without question, lop

01:50:35.280 --> 01:50:37.350
side them, if you
will, toward being

01:50:37.350 --> 01:50:40.500
better in some of these
assessments and less good

01:50:40.500 --> 01:50:41.160
in others.

01:50:41.160 --> 01:50:43.710
And that's just simply the way
that these adaptations work.

01:50:43.710 --> 01:50:45.850
ANDY GALPIN: Yep.

01:50:45.850 --> 01:50:49.660
You don't need to be optimal
in all of these areas to be,

01:50:49.660 --> 01:50:52.150
quote, unquote, "optimal
health" from this perspective.

01:50:52.150 --> 01:50:53.567
You just want to
make sure, again,

01:50:53.567 --> 01:50:56.620
there's no severe
performance anchors.

01:50:56.620 --> 01:50:57.865
This is what we call them.

01:50:57.865 --> 01:50:59.740
We don't want any of
these severe constraints

01:50:59.740 --> 01:51:03.020
because you're going to
get limited by that thing.

01:51:03.020 --> 01:51:05.230
And so what you want
to do is move that up

01:51:05.230 --> 01:51:09.130
to just sufficient or concerning
and get it away from that.

01:51:09.130 --> 01:51:11.350
If you do that, that thing's
not going to catch you.

01:51:11.350 --> 01:51:13.935
You're going to be able to
continue to pursue optimization

01:51:13.935 --> 01:51:15.310
in any of the one
things that you

01:51:15.310 --> 01:51:17.470
have a specific
passion for, which

01:51:17.470 --> 01:51:19.000
is generally what moves people.

01:51:19.000 --> 01:51:21.940
You train so that
you feel better.

01:51:21.940 --> 01:51:25.360
You train because you know there
are all these benefits to it.

01:51:25.360 --> 01:51:29.200
And jeez, this audience probably
could list hundreds of them.

01:51:29.200 --> 01:51:31.255
But you also train
because you generally like

01:51:31.255 --> 01:51:32.380
to get better at something.

01:51:32.380 --> 01:51:34.245
A lot of us have something.

01:51:34.245 --> 01:51:36.370
And so you want to make
sure that you're not going,

01:51:36.370 --> 01:51:38.055
hey, I know you're
good at endurance,

01:51:38.055 --> 01:51:39.680
but you really
shouldn't train anymore.

01:51:39.680 --> 01:51:41.305
We don't want that
message, not at all.

01:51:41.305 --> 01:51:43.630
I want you to love
your training.

01:51:43.630 --> 01:51:46.300
We just want to make sure
that you're not loving that

01:51:46.300 --> 01:51:48.970
so much that you're not taking
some blinders off and missing

01:51:48.970 --> 01:51:52.240
another area, which
would actually--

01:51:52.240 --> 01:51:55.180
again, you pull that performance
anchor, this whole ship sails

01:51:55.180 --> 01:51:59.520
faster, with less effort
and less friction.

01:51:59.520 --> 01:52:01.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What I love
about this is also that,

01:52:01.830 --> 01:52:03.930
as you've described
it, it's not just

01:52:03.930 --> 01:52:06.540
for athletes or people that
are super into fitness.

01:52:06.540 --> 01:52:09.420
It's also for people that just
want to be healthy and want

01:52:09.420 --> 01:52:10.420
aesthetic changes.

01:52:10.420 --> 01:52:12.503
And that's why they're
exercising, which, I think,

01:52:12.503 --> 01:52:15.010
accounts for a fairly large
percentage of people out there.

01:52:15.010 --> 01:52:19.440
So I think what you described
is incredibly well-structured,

01:52:19.440 --> 01:52:22.540
incredibly clear, and
incredibly actionable.

01:52:22.540 --> 01:52:26.490
So I want to thank
you for that I'm

01:52:26.490 --> 01:52:30.390
serious about my willingness
to do this and at least share

01:52:30.390 --> 01:52:31.530
those numbers with you.

01:52:31.530 --> 01:52:35.760
And I think for most people that
