01:52:43.300 --> 01:52:44.800
ANDY GALPIN: So
before we close out,

01:52:44.800 --> 01:52:48.743
I want to go back and finish
off the metrics for VO2 max

01:52:48.743 --> 01:52:51.160
because I don't actually think
I gave you numbers on that.

01:52:51.160 --> 01:52:53.970
So in general, for
men, a minimum number

01:52:53.970 --> 01:52:56.100
we want to look at
here is 35 milliliters

01:52:56.100 --> 01:52:57.100
per kilogram per minute.

01:52:57.100 --> 01:52:59.340
And for women, that
would be about 30.

01:52:59.340 --> 01:53:02.850
So we can actually push a
lot higher on those things.

01:53:02.850 --> 01:53:05.530
In reality, I want
to see men above 50.

01:53:05.530 --> 01:53:08.030
ANDREW HUBERMAN: If I could
just interrupt you for a second.

01:53:08.030 --> 01:53:13.600
When you say 40 milliliters per
kilogram, milliliters of what,

01:53:13.600 --> 01:53:14.260
specifically?

01:53:14.260 --> 01:53:14.470
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:53:14.470 --> 01:53:16.480
So what, actually,
those metrics mean is

01:53:16.480 --> 01:53:18.680
the first one,
milliliters, is oxygen.

01:53:18.680 --> 01:53:22.280
So it's amount of oxygen.
Kilograms is body weight.

01:53:22.280 --> 01:53:25.180
So it's, how much oxygen can
you bring in per kilogram

01:53:25.180 --> 01:53:27.230
of body weight per minute?

01:53:27.230 --> 01:53:31.870
So does a volume of oxygen per
your size in a time duration.

01:53:31.870 --> 01:53:34.450
In fact, the way
that you calculate it

01:53:34.450 --> 01:53:37.442
is you multiply your
cardiac output by what's

01:53:37.442 --> 01:53:38.650
called your a-vO2 difference.

01:53:38.650 --> 01:53:41.380
Your cardiac output is your
heart rate times your stroke

01:53:41.380 --> 01:53:44.753
volume, so how much blood
you're pumping out per pump

01:53:44.753 --> 01:53:45.670
is your stroke volume.

01:53:45.670 --> 01:53:48.760
How many times you're
pumping or you're beating.

01:53:48.760 --> 01:53:50.770
You multiply that by
your a-vO2 difference.

01:53:50.770 --> 01:53:54.440
Your a-vO2 difference is
artery minus vein difference.

01:53:54.440 --> 01:53:56.320
So it's the amount of
oxygen in your arteries

01:53:56.320 --> 01:53:58.720
minus the amount of
oxygen in your vein, which

01:53:58.720 --> 01:54:00.448
is going to tell you
how much you took up

01:54:00.448 --> 01:54:01.990
in your capillaries,
in your muscles.

01:54:01.990 --> 01:54:04.240
So you take those two factors,
multiply them together,

01:54:04.240 --> 01:54:05.500
and there's your VO2 max.

01:54:05.500 --> 01:54:07.375
ANDREW HUBERMAN: As you
were describing that,

01:54:07.375 --> 01:54:09.160
I imagine you getting
to an fMRI machine

01:54:09.160 --> 01:54:12.070
and seeing that equation
lighting up in your brain

01:54:12.070 --> 01:54:14.470
because clearly, it's
committed to memory very well.

01:54:14.470 --> 01:54:16.270
Thank you for that
clear description.

01:54:16.270 --> 01:54:17.020
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:54:17.020 --> 01:54:19.240
So to finish those
numbers, I really, truly

01:54:19.240 --> 01:54:21.850
want to see a man above 50--

01:54:21.850 --> 01:54:24.680
and I'm not even really
stoked until I get above 55.

01:54:24.680 --> 01:54:25.960
In fact, it's sort of funny.

01:54:25.960 --> 01:54:28.900
Dave Costill, whose lab
I did my PhD in-- he

01:54:28.900 --> 01:54:30.790
was retired by the
time, but he's, again,

01:54:30.790 --> 01:54:34.000
one of these legendary figures
in exercise physiology,

01:54:34.000 --> 01:54:35.020
started in the '70s.

01:54:35.020 --> 01:54:37.300
He would always say,
"There's no human excuse

01:54:37.300 --> 01:54:40.630
to be below 60," which
I was always like, damn.

01:54:40.630 --> 01:54:42.920
That's really, actually,
pretty hard to get to.

01:54:42.920 --> 01:54:44.805
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Was
he at 60 or above?

01:54:44.805 --> 01:54:46.638
ANDY GALPIN: Oh, yeah.
he's still, actually,

01:54:46.638 --> 01:54:49.180
setting world records in
these last couple of years,

01:54:49.180 --> 01:54:52.360
all the Masters records for
swimming and cycling and stuff.

01:54:52.360 --> 01:54:55.180
So he was a super,
super fit guy.

01:54:55.180 --> 01:54:56.740
So he was always above 60.

01:54:56.740 --> 01:54:58.900
He's probably like
50 something now,

01:54:58.900 --> 01:55:00.290
even though he's 80 or whatever.

01:55:00.290 --> 01:55:01.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN: 80 years old.

01:55:01.540 --> 01:55:02.080
ANDY GALPIN: Yeah.

01:55:02.080 --> 01:55:03.640
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
With a VO2 max of 50.

01:55:03.640 --> 01:55:04.720
ANDY GALPIN: He's
probably really not 50.

01:55:04.720 --> 01:55:06.700
He's probably--
but he's probably

01:55:06.700 --> 01:55:08.770
going to-- remember
